INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3143,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11585036','Outpatient characteristics in a pediatric otolaryngology department during COVID-19 pandemic: A retrospective study.','Noda M, Koshu R, Sugimoto H, Yoshizaki T, Nomura A, Ito M.','2024','choking receipts jichi jichi conforms logopedics phoniatrics hypopnea polysomnographic tracheoesophagus logopedics phoniatrics tias pfapa tias pfapa aphtous adenitis tonsilitis tias hypopnea polysomnographic polysomnographic postpandemic snoring nurseries kindergartens daycare kindergarten teleworking palatine stunting nursery telework 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 masao ryota koshu hisashi tomokazu yoshizaki akihiro makoto 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 masao ryota koshu hisashi tomokazu yoshizaki akihiro makoto chizu enago enago jst jpmjpf2403 jichi masao hisashi chernew linetsky commonwealthfund kaboli huskamp mhlw toukei saikin byouin m19 1905kekka mhlw toukei saikin byouin 2005kekka kalder kostev behl mcgoogan bhaskaran opensafely petrilli m1966 dzioba getmedia 72332e75 442c bf45 5de6761a5986 inline chadha trecca gaffuri caversaccio suen caroll mccolley snoring windfuhr gunster alho koivu sorri cuthbertson swc e0223990 rickert rahbar kaditis boudewyns castronovo zucconi nosetti snoring lumeng chervin chodick 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 masao ryota koshu hisashi tomokazu yoshizaki akihiro makoto 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 1177_00368504241301809 masao ryota koshu hisashi tomokazu yoshizaki akihiro makoto','Abstract Objectives To investigate the characteristics of outpatients in the pediatric otolaryngology department of a tertiary Japanese hospital before and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Methods In this retrospective study conducted at a tertiary center in Japan, pediatric patients aged 0–15 years who visited the pediatric otolaryngology department between 2017 and 2022 were included. The number of outpatients in the department was compared between before and during the COVID-19 pandemic (January 2017–December 2022) by year, age, sex, and the disease for which the patient was examined. Additionally, the diseases that most substantially contributed to the change in outpatient visits were evaluated in detail regarding their severity. Results Among a total of 9219 outpatients, there were no significant differences between 2017–2019 and 2020–2022 (4650 vs. 4569). In contrast, the number of new outpatients decreased by 27.5% (from 1242 to 900) in each of the three years during the COVID-19 pandemic. In particular, the number of patients aged 1–6 years decreased as did that of male patients. Of eight disease categories, oral cavity- and pharyngeal- related diseases decreased the most significantly, by approximately 50%, and the decrease in the number of patients with obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is thought to have had an impact on the decrease in the number of patients. Furthermore, in cases in which polysomnography testing was performed for OSA, the proportion of patients with moderate and severe categories showed a significant decrease. Conclusions The number of new pediatric ORL patients, particularly those with severe OSA, reduced during the COVID 19 pandemic. These results may provide indications about the characteristics of OSA incidence and exacerbations. Keywords: Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), outpatients, obstructive sleep apnea, epidemiology, pediatric otolaryngology, retrospective study Introduction On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization declared COVID-19 a global pandemic, leading to dramatic changes in healthcare systems worldwide. Although telemedicine and artificial intelligence have enabled remote healthcare access, most care is still delivered in person, albeit with notable shifts in healthcare delivery and patient behavior. 1 In 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic began, patient visits decreased by 20%–60%, affecting the healthcare system and disease management. 2 – 5 This reduction led to concerns that patients, particularly those with serious or chronic conditions such as cancer, diabetes, hypertension, and rheumatism, were missing necessary medical consultations. 6 , 7 Thus, while many of these conditions have less impact on patients by sudden changes in condition after a period of time, 8 – 12 for pediatric diseases a delay in diagnosis and treatment could negatively impact relevant areas of development, such as the acquisition of language and social skills, growth due to malnutrition, and choking due to airway diseases. Therefore, it is necessary to identify and respond to changes in the patient population during and after COVID-19 pandemic. To date, there have been limited studies in pediatrics examining changes in patient characteristics related to COVID-19, with most research published shortly after the first year of the pandemic. In 2021, some studies utilized questionnaires directed at pediatric otolaryngologists; however, none have analyzed patient data collected over an extended period. 13 Furthermore, although some reports analyzed databases based on insurance disease names and receipts, their accuracy has not been examined. 13 – 18 Since pediatric otolaryngology includes several disease types, including infectious diseases, congenital malformations, and neoplastic lesions, it is necessary to accurately capture all of them and analyze changes due to pandemic. Conversely, the high risk of COVID-19 infection for healthcare providers also impacts the need to understand the impact on their characteristics and patient population in clinical practice. 16 In this study, the changes in pediatric otolaryngology outpatients before and after the COVID-19 pandemic were examined according to sex, age, and other individual characteristics, as well as disease classification. In addition, changes in factors such as severity and complications of the main diseases were examined before and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Methods Ethics statements This study was approved by the Jichi Medical University Institutional Review Board of Clinical Trials (Approval No 23-087). The study was conducted in accordance with the ethical principles of the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and its subsequent revisions, most recently updated in 2013. Informed consent was obtained via an opt-out form provided on the website. Those who rejected were excluded. To protect patient confidentiality, all patient details were de-identified, ensuring that no individual was identified in any manner. Study design and patient involvement In this retrospective study, we conducted an analysis of pediatric outpatients aged 0–15 years who visited the Department of Pediatric Otolaryngology of Jichi Medical University Hospital and Children''s Medical Center, a tertiary hospital in Japan, between January 2017 and December 2022. Patients were selected consecutively for this study, ensuring that every patient meeting the inclusion criteria during the study period was included. To investigate the differences in the characteristics of outpatients before and during the COVID-19 pandemic, we collected data on the number of outpatients by year, age, sex, and the disease for which they were examined before and during the pandemic (January 2017–December 2022). Based on the year 2020, when COVID spread across Japan, we examined how the data for 2020–2022 changed compared with that for 2017–2019. Furthermore, we evaluated the diseases that significantly contributed to the change in outpatient visits and analyzed their severity in detail. The reporting of this study conforms to the STROBE guidelines. 19 Data collection and outcome measures All data were independently collected by two pediatric otolaryngologists (M.N. and R.K.) by reviewing medical records and capturing data manually. Patient characteristics (age, sex, disease type, and number of visits) were collected. Each pediatric otolaryngologist (M.N. and R.K.) independently classified the pediatric otolaryngology conditions into eight categories (ear, nose, oral cavity and pharynx, head and neck surgery, laryngeal trachea-esophagus, logopedics and phoniatrics, dizziness and facial nerve, and swallowing and dysphagia). Based on the aggregated and organized data, we evaluated the trends regarding patient characteristics and disease types for each year. Regarding the diseases that showed the most significant changes, to analyze the severity of OSA-related patients, the apnea–hypopnea index (AHI) and complications related to apnea were collected. The AHI was scaled using polysomnographic data. The AHI data were classified as follows: normal, AHI < 1; mild. 1 ≤ AHI < 5; moderate, 5 ≤ AHI < 10; and severe, AHI ≥ 10. 20 , 21 Considering that causes other than OSA, such as facial deformities, cerebral palsy, and 21 trisomy, might have been involved in complicated cases, AHI severity was examined after excluding such complicated cases. Statistical analysis All statistical analyses were performed using SPSS 25.0 statistical software (IBM Corp.). For descriptive statistics, data from 2017 to 2019 were used as a baseline. Pearson''s chi-squared analyses were conducted to evaluate the population of the patients and the association between OSA severity levels during the pandemic and previous terms. A p-value <0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results Characteristics of the study population Our sample included 9219 patients who visited the pediatric otolaryngology department in a tertiary hospital before and during the COVID-19 pandemic ( Table 1 ). Among them, 4650 patients visited between 2017 and 2019 and 4569 between 2020 and 2022. Table 1. Characteristics of 9219 patients with outpatient in the department of pediatric otolaryngology grouped by age, sex, and number of visits per year. Total (n = 9219) 2017–2019 (n = 4650) 2020 (n = 1519) 2021 (n = 1546) 2022 (n = 1504) Age (year), mean (SD) 6.01 (4.15) 5.78 (4.01) 6.41 (4.23) 6.04 (4.11) 6.32 (4.46) No. of patient by age group No (%) age <1 758 (8.2) 127.3 (8.1) 113 (7.4) 124 (8.0) 139 (9.2) 1 ≤ age < 7 4704 (51.0) 821.3 (53.1) 752 (49.5) 786 (50.8) 702 (46.7) 7 ≤ age < 12 2919 (31.7) 480.7 (30.9) 496 (32.7) 496 (32.1) 485 (32.2) 12 ≤ age 838 (9.1) 120.7 (7.8) 158 (10.4) 140 (9.1) 178 (11.8) No. of patient by sex No (%) Male 5244 (56.9) 2708 (58.2) 861 (56.7) 874 (56.5) 801 (53.3) Female 3975 (43.1) 1942 (41.8) 658 (43.3) 672 (43.5) 703 (46.7) No. of patients per visit No (%) New patients 2142 (23.2) 1242 (26.7) 319 (21.0) 314 (20.3) 267 (17.8) Second and subsequent visit 7077 (76.8) 3408 (73.3) 1200 (79.0) 1232 (79.7) 1237 (82.2) Open in a new tab Table 2. Number of outpatients per visit according to age during each year of the COVID-19 pandemic compared with a previous term . Outpatients were categorized according to age, new patients and second or subsequent visits are summarized per year. Data were compared to 2017–2019 data used as a baseline. Average of 2017–2019 (Percentage of total patients) 2020 Percent change compared with 2017–2019 2021 Percentage change compared with 2017–2019 2022 Percentage change compared with 2017–2019 Age <1 Total 127.33 113 −11.2% 124 −2.6% 139 9.2% New patients 62.67 (49.2) 53 −15.4% 51 −18.6% 51 −18.6% Second and subsequent visits 64.67 (50.8) 60 −7.2% 73 12.9% 88 36.1% 1 ≤ age <7 Total 821.33 752 −8.4% 786 −4.3% 702 −14.5% New patients 212.33 (25.9) 137 −35.4% 137 −35.5% 94 −55.7% Second and subsequent visits 609 (74.1) 615 1.0% 649 6.6% 608 −0.2% 7 ≤ age <12 Total 480.67 496 3.2% 496 3.2% 485 0.9% New patients 108.67 (22.6) 92 −15.3% 90 −17.2% 93 −14.4% Second and subsequent visits 372 (77.4) 404 8.6% 406 9.1% 392 5.4% Age ≥12 Total 120.67 158 30.9% 140 16.0% 178 47.5% New patients 30.33 (25.1) 37 22.0% 36 18.7% 29 −4.4% Second and subsequent visits 90.33 (74.8) 121 34.0% 104 15.1% 149 65.0% Open in a new tab During the pandemic period (2020–2022), we observed a notable decrease in the number of new patients, particularly among those aged 1–6 years and male patients, these decreases were statistically significant, with χ²(1, N = 13769) = 14.17, p = 0.00017 for patients aged 1–6 years and χ²(1, N = 9220) = 6.90, p = 0.0086 for male patients (Figure 1). Overall, the number of new outpatients decreased by 27.5%, dropping from 1242 patients in the pre-pandemic period to 900 during the pandemic years. Among the age groups, the most significant reduction occurred in patients aged 1–6 years, with a decrease of 35.5% in 2020 and 2021, and a further decline to 55.7% in 2022 ( Table 1 , Figure 2A ). This reduction was especially prominent among children aged 3–5 years (Table 2, Figure 2B ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Number of outpatients each year of the COVID-19 pandemic compared with a previous term. A B: Number of outpatients by age per year. Groups were divided into four groups (age <1, 1 ≤ age <7, 7 ≤ age <13, 13 ≤ age) (A) and Number of new outpatients by age per year among 1 ≤ age <7 group (B). C: Number of new outpatients according to sex per year. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Outpatient trends in the department of pediatric otolaryngology (monthly data) . Vertical dotted lines indicate the timing of the first, second and third regional state of emergency on April 2020, January 2021, and August 2021, respectively. In addition, among new patients, there was a decreasing trend for boys ( Figure 2C ). Next, to examine the disease-specific characteristics of patients, new patients were categorized according to disease type ( Table 3 ). Among these, the oral cavity and pharynx were identified as areas of marked decline in incidence of occurrences. Further, among the eight disease categories, oral cavity- and pharynx-related diseases decreased the mostly by approximately 50%. Table 3. Number of new outpatients per disease classification during the COVID-19 pandemic compared with a previous term. Disease or symptoms were divided into eight different categories and compared to 2017–2019 data used as a baseline. Average of 2017–2019 (Percentage of total patients) Average of 2020–2022 (Percentage of total patients) Percentage change compared with 2017–2019 Ear 725 (52.0) 560 (55.6) −22.8% Oral cavity and pharynx 368 (26.4) 190 (18.8) −48.4% Nose 69 (4.9) 51 (5.1) −26.1% Head and neck surgery 57 (4.1) 59 (5.9) 3.5% Laryngeal tracheoesophagus 60 (4.3) 53 (5.3) −11.7% Logopedics and phoniatrics 55 (3.9) 44 (4.4) −20.0% Dizziness and facial nerve 47 (3.4) 41 (4.1) −12.8% Swallowing and dysphagia 14 (1.0) 10 (1.0) −28.6% Total 1395 1008 −27.7% Open in a new tab Secondary analysis related to the oral cavity and pharynx Table 4 shows the number of new outpatients per disease related to the oral cavity and pharynx during the COVID-19 pandemic compared with the previous term ( Table 4 , Figure 3 ). Table 4. Number of new outpatients per disease related to oral cavity and pharynx during the COVID-19 pandemic compared with the previous term (2017–2019). Average of 2017–2019 (Percentage of total patients) 2020 (Percentage of total patients) Percentage change compared to 2017–2019 2021 (Percentage of total patients) Percentage change compared with 2017–2019 2022 (Percentage of total patients) Percentage change compared with 2017–2019 OSA Total 102.3 (83.4) 58 (78.4) −43.3% 54 (81.8) −47.2% 33 (64.7) −67.8% OSA 90.7 (73.9) 42 (56.8) −53.7% 45 (68.2) −50.4% 28 (54.9) −69.1% OSA suspected case 11.7 (9.5) 16 (21.6) 37.1% 9 (13.6) −22.9% 5 (9.8) −57.2% Tonsillitis Chronic tonsillitis 3.7 (3.0) 9 (12.2) 145.2% 2 (3.0) −45.5% 2 (3.9) −45.5% TIAS Total 2 (1.7) 3 (4.1) 50.0% 6 (9.1) 5 (9.8) IgA nephropathy 1.7 (1.4) 1 (1.4) −40.1% 4 (6.1) 139.5% 5 (9.8) 199.4% PFAPA 0.3 (0.3) 2 (2.7) 2 (3.0) 0 (0.0) Total 122.7 74 −39.7% 66 −46.2% 51 −58.4% Open in a new tab The number of new outpatients attributable to diseases related to the oral cavity and pharynx during the COVID-19 pandemic is presented. OSA: Obstructive Sleep Apnea, TIAS: Tonsil induced autoimmune/inflammatory syndrome, PFAPA: Periodic fever with Aphtous Pharyngitis and Adenitis. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Trends in new outpatients related to oral cavity and pharynx, and OSA counted every month. Vertical dotted lines indicate the timing of the first, second, and third regional state of emergency on April 2020, January 2021, and August 2021, respectively. In each period, approximately 80% of patients had obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), followed by tonsilitis and tonsil-induced autoimmune/inflammatory syndrome (TIAS). The decreased number of patients with OSA, which accounts for most oral cavity and pharynx diseases, contributed to the overall decline in the number of new outpatients. The detailed relationship with OSA was analyzed by evaluating the apnea–hypopnea index (AHI) in all patients who underwent polysomnography (PSG), comparing AHI severity between the periods of 2017–2019 and 2020–2022 ( Table 5 , Figure 4A ). For all patients who underwent PSG for OSA, there was no significant difference in AHI severity between the two time periods (p = 0.058). Although changes in the distribution of AHI categories were observed, they did not reach statistical significance at the 5% level. Table 5. New outpatients who underwent PSG related to OSA each year of the COVID-19 pandemic compared with previous term (2017–2019) number of new patients who underwent PSG related to OSA. All data and data excluding complication cases. AHI was scaled based on polysomnographic data and classified according to previous reports as follows: normal: AHI < 1, mild: 1 ≤ AHI < 5, moderate: 5 ≤ AHI < 10, severe: 10 ≤ AHI. 2017–2019 (average/year) (% of total patients) 2020 (% of total patients) %Change Compared to baseline 2021 (% of total patients) %Change Compared to baseline 2022 (% of total patients) %Change Compared to baseline Al patients underwent PSG related to OSA Normal (AHI <1) 8.33 (11.1) 5 (13.2) 40% 6 (12.8) 28% 5 (20.8) 40% Mild (1≦AHI < 5) 28.67 (38.1) 17 (44.7) 41% 24 (51.1) 16% 12 (50.0) 58% Moderate (5≦AHI < 10) 15 (19.9) 4 (10.5) 73% 5 (10.6) 67% 2 (8.3) 87% Severe (10≦AHI) 23.33 (31.0) 12 (31.6) 48% 12 (25.5) 48% 5 (20.8) 79% OSA-related outpatients (excluded complication cases) Normal (AHI <1) 8.33 (11.4) 5 (14.3) 40% 6 (14.6) 28% 5 (21.7) 40% Mild (1≦AHI < 5) 28.67 (39.1) 17 (48.6) 41% 21 (51.2) 27% 12 (52.2) 58% Moderate (5≦AHI < 10) 14.67 (20.0) 4 (11.4) 73% 5 (12.2) 66% 1 (4.3) 93% Severe (10≦AHI) 21.67 (29.5) 9 (25.7) 58% 9 (22.0) 58% 5 (21.7) 77% Open in a new tab Figure 4. Open in a new tab Number of new outpatients who underwent PSG related to OSA during the COVID-19 pandemic compared with a previous term (2017–2019). (A) All data and (B) data excluding complications. AHI was scaled based on polysomnographic data and classified according to previous reports as follows: normal: AHI < 1, mild: 1 ≤ AHI < 5, moderate: 5 ≤ AHI < 10, severe: 10 ≤ AHI. After excluding patients with complications, a significant difference in AHI severity was observed between the two periods (p = 0.027, Table 5 , Figure 4B ). Thus, the exclusion of complications revealed a notable shift in the distribution of AHI severity, particularly in the moderate and severe categories, between the pre- and postpandemic periods. Discussion The present analysis of trends in pediatric otolaryngology outpatients before and during the COVID-19 pandemic using data from regional core hospitals in Japan revealed the following findings: First, the number of new pediatric otolaryngology outpatients decreased, particularly in the 1–6 years old range. Second, there was a decrease in the number of boys examined; the largest decrease in new patients according to the field was that for visits related to the oropharynx, particularly OSA. Finally, an analysis of patients with the main complaint of snoring based on the AHI, an indicator of OSA severity, showed a decrease in the proportion of patients with moderate or severe disease. These findings reveal trends in the impact of COVID-19 pandemic on pediatric otolaryngology, which implies that pediatric OSA is at least in some way associated with the COVID-19 pandemic. Previous questionnaire survey studies of pediatric otolaryngologists in Italy reported decreases in the number of outpatient visits and surgeries compared with the pre-COVID levels. In the present analysis of actual data from Japan, no significant reduction in the total number of patients was observed although the number of new patients declined. The reduction in new pediatric otolaryngology patients, regardless of the country, suggests that common factors, such as behavioral restrictions and reduced social contact, likely influenced this trend. 17 Additionally, a study from Switzerland also reported a decrease in the number of ENT-related surgeries, with a particularly notable reduction in procedures related to the neck and ears. 22 Although Japan is experiencing a rapid decline in birth rates, the effect of which should be considered, the results from other countries suggest that the COVID-19 pandemic had a significant influence. These findings indicate that the observed changes in pediatric otolaryngology might be attributable to the declining birth rate and the broader effects of the pandemic. The lower number of new patients was observed for children aged 1–6 years. The reasons for the present results may be due to patient-related factors, such as a reduced risk of infection, a lower number of consultations, and lifestyle changes. In line with these findings, single-center reports from other countries have shown a decrease in the number of otolaryngology emergency outpatients aged ≤18 years and ≥45 years one year after 2020. 23 In the Japanese education system, children attend nurseries and kindergartens until the age of six, when they attend primary school until they are 12 years old and move to junior high school. Childcare facilities provide opportunities for contact with young children and are considered a risk factor for otitis media 24 and viral and bacterial infections. Attendance at daycare or kindergarten is an important factor driving bacterial diversity. 25 We hypothesize that the risk of infection decreased because of the loss of personal contact, the reduction of medical visits to avoid COVID infection, and the lifestyle of parents of pediatric patients, such as teleworking, may have reduced the chances of going outside the home and being aware of minor abnormalities. Another hypothesis is that the decrease in clinic visits or the use of telemedicine might have hindered accurate oral examinations. Unlike general otolaryngology, a unique challenge in pediatric otolaryngology is that children often do not cooperate in clearly showing their oral or nasal cavities. Scott et al. noted that the inability to conduct in-person examinations during the COVID-19 pandemic made these observations more difficult, potentially leading to delays in diagnosis and treatment. 26 Our report also indicated a marked decrease in oropharyngeal diseases, especially those related to OSA. Pediatric otorhinolaryngology has a wide variety of diseases, including infectious, congenital, and neoplastic diseases. Previous reports have shown a significant decrease in infectious or inflammatory diseases such as otitis media and abscesses, whereas congenital anomalies and masses/malignancies remained particularly unchanged. 13 In that report, OSA/sleep-disordered breathing was classified as other, with respondents reporting a nearly equal increase, no change, and decrease at their institution. Interestingly, other reports from Germany observed a decrease in the number of patients with sore throat in primary care following the first lockdown. The present study also reported a significant decrease in all types of tonsil surgery, indicating that tonsil-related conditions were more significantly affected by the pandemic than other oral diseases. This trend appears to be consistent across various studies. 22 Pediatric OSA is particularly common at 2–8 years old, being caused by hypertrophy of the palatine tonsils, adenoid, or other immune tissues, which further narrow the airways, leading to inadequate oxygenation, stunting growth and resulting in daytime sleepiness and speech development disorders. 12 , 27 – 30 Between 5–8 years old airways get normally enlarged, even more so after infections and other events. We consider that the number of cases decreased due to reduced contact opportunities and infection risk lower preschool attendance, mask use, and hand washing. However, there was no clear decrease in repetitive or habitual tonsillitis, suggesting that the decrease in OSA may be due to causes other than reduced exposure. Furthermore, we observed a significant decrease in the number of patients with moderate or severe OSA, particularly when patients with severe complications were excluded. This result could be due to overall reluctance to see a doctor or decreased severity of the disease; however, the significant decrease in moderate and severe cases suggests decreasing severity of the disease. One of the reasons for the decrease is that the COVID pandemic has reduced contact opportunities not only because of the voluntary refrain from attending nursery school due to the COVID pandemic but also because parents are now seeing their children at home due to the widespread use of telework, enabling detection at an earlier stage. The results suggest that reducing contact opportunities may help reduce the severity of OSA, even after COVID-19 transmission has settled down. However, this study has some limitations. Specifically, the data were collected from a single medical institution, and the cohort was relatively small, which might have reduced the statistical power for detecting significant differences. Therefore, a larger sample size and more prospective studies are required to confirm the present results and generalize the findings to more diverse patient populations. Conclusion This study examined changes in the number of pediatric otolaryngology outpatients attributable to the spread of COVID-19, focusing on patient characteristics and major diseases. COVID-19 decreased the number of new pediatric otolaryngology patients, particularly among those aged 1–6 years and male patients, as well as those with conditions related to the oral cavity and pharynx. Additionally, the proportion of patients with moderate and severe OSA decreased during the pandemic, providing further insights into how the pandemic influenced the presentation and severity of conditions typically seen in pediatric otolaryngology. These findings offer valuable information for understanding the broader impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on patient care in this field. Supplemental Material sj-docx-2-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809 - Supplemental material for Outpatient characteristics in a pediatric otolaryngology department during COVID-19 pandemic: A retrospective study sj-docx-2-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809.docx (94.7KB, docx) Supplemental material, sj-docx-2-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809 for Outpatient characteristics in a pediatric otolaryngology department during COVID-19 pandemic: A retrospective study by Masao Noda, Ryota Koshu, Hisashi Sugimoto, Tomokazu Yoshizaki, Akihiro Nomura and Makoto Ito in Science Progress sj-docx-3-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809 - Supplemental material for Outpatient characteristics in a pediatric otolaryngology department during COVID-19 pandemic: A retrospective study sj-docx-3-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809.docx (33.7KB, docx) Supplemental material, sj-docx-3-sci-10.1177_00368504241301809 for Outpatient characteristics in a pediatric otolaryngology department during COVID-19 pandemic: A retrospective study by Masao Noda, Ryota Koshu, Hisashi Sugimoto, Tomokazu Yoshizaki, Akihiro Nomura and Makoto Ito in Science Progress Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank Mari Dias Shimada and Chizu Saito for clinical assistance with the study. The authors would like to thank Enago ( https://www.enago.jp ) for their assistance in the English language editing process. This work was supported by JST Grant Number JPMJPF2403. Footnotes Author contribution: Contributor M.N. was responsible for the organization. M.N., R.K., and M.I. were responsible for data analysis and developed the study design. All authors contributed to the preparation of the final manuscript. Consent to participate: Informed consent from the participants was obtained in the form of opt-out option on the website. Data availability statement: The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author (M.N.) upon reasonable request. The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Ethical considerations: This study was approved by the Jichi Medical University Institutional Review Board of Clinical Trials (Approval No 23-087). This study was conducted in accordance with the ethical principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and its subsequent revisions, most recently updated in 2013. Funding: The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Informed consent: Informed consent for patient information to be published in this article was not obtained because this study is a retrospective observational study, and an opt-out approach was applied with the details publicly disclosed on the hospital''s website. ORCID iDs: Masao Noda https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6281-2782 Hisashi Sugimoto https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8903-4423 Supplemental material: Supplemental material for this article is available online. References 1. Mehrotra A, Chernew M, Linetsky D, et al. The impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on outpatient visits: practices are adapting to the new normal. Commonwealth fund, 2021. Accessed December 14,