ersity of Washington experience. JAMA Surgery. 2020 doi: 10.1001/jamasurg.2020.1219. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Crosby DL, Sharma A. Insight on otolaryngology residency training during the COVID-19 pandemic. Otolaryngology. 2020 doi: 10.1177/0194599820922502. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Turri-Zanoni M, Battaglia P, Czaczkes C, Pelosi P, Castelnuovo P, Cabrini L. Elective tracheostomy during mechanical ventilation in patients affected by COVID-19: preliminary case series from Lombardy, Italy. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2020 doi: 10.1177/0194599820928963. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Zuckerman JD, Wise SK, Rogers GA, et al. The utility of cadaver dissection in endoscopic sinus surgery training corses. Am J Rhinol Allergy. 2009 doi: 10.2500/ajra.2009.23.3297. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Dharmarajan H, Andreson JL, Kim S, et al. Transition to a virtual multidisciplinary tumor board during the COVID-19 pandemic: university of Pittsburgh experience. New York: Wiley; 2020. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Shah JP. The impact of Covid-19 on head and neck surgery, education and training. Head Neck. 2020 doi: 10.1002/hed.26188. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',392,'2025-10-14 13:40:56','2025-10-14 13:40:56');
