INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3219,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10278777','Reimagining Nephrology Fellowship Education to Meet the Future Needs of Nephrology: A Report of the American Society of Nephrology Task Force on the Future of Nephrology.','Rosenberg ME, Anderson S, Farouk SS, Gibson KL, Hoover RS Jr, Humphreys BD, Orlowski JM, Udani SM, Waitzman JS, West M, Ibrahim T.','2023','reconsidering excite waitlist demonstrably aakh aakh aakh cmmi cmmi venture firms startups intensifies vacant scramble reconsidering aire cmo nonpublic definable aakh maximizes embodies injustices profitable aacom visas visas visas visas hardship aacom aakh aacom reimagining envisioning restructure aakh committing reimagining accomplishing realignment publicize foresee accredit 2020s fasn samira farouk fasn keisha fasn fasn fasn janis orlowski fasn suneel udani fasn waitzman furman cheryl fasn jerry vasilias finalizing appreciates molly kurtis farouk aurinia travere retrophin aurinia travere travere nonpaid ipna nonpaid treasurer chinook chinook evotec regmed chinook asci restaurant orlowski creighton trustees marquette trustees trustees montini trustees orlowski wolter udani aurinia calliditas dimerix goldfinch natera talaris travere calliditas caredx chinook dimerix goldfinch goldfinch mallinckrodt travere alexion calliditas goldfinch mallinckrodt natera travere nephcure aurinia natera nephcure waitzman tome waitzman samira farouk keisha janis orlowski suneel udani waitzman samira farouk keisha janis orlowski suneel udani waitzman vassalotti diamantidis renumerating kalantar semnephrol boulware quickfacts quickfacts pst045221 quaggin chiquita lasure webdocs asntxrfifinal2 federalregister kcc kcc trish markups kshirsagar privatized kidneynews p11_5 brosius cherney ay_2022_match tab1 burgner 2022_fellow_survey neph ay_2023_match jaar lipner brossman baudy thakar shankland obaidi rodby 148_nephrology_2022_tcc salsberg early_career_survey rope merell leisman sachdeva malieckal rangaswami nephrologymilestones2 pourafshar priamvada kotzen hfftp niyyar sfac160 koratala olaoye bhasin chhabra kazory m17 ay_2022_match kovesdy langham zoccali gfz174 aacom usher aacom usher sussman aakp aakp','Abstract The American Society of Nephrology (ASN) Task Force on the Future of Nephrology was established in April 2022 in response to requests from the American Board of Internal Medicine and the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education regarding training requirements in nephrology. Given recent changes in kidney care, ASN also charged the task force with reconsidering all aspects of the specialty''s future to ensure that nephrologists are prepared to provide high-quality care for people with kidney diseases. The task force engaged multiple stakeholders to develop 10 recommendations focused on strategies needed to promote: ( 1 ) just, equitable, and high-quality care for people living with kidney diseases; ( 2 ) the value of nephrology as a specialty to nephrologists, the future nephrology workforce, the health care system, the public, and government; and ( 3 ) innovation and personalization of nephrology education across the scope of medical training. This report reviews the process, rationale, and details (the “why” and the “what”) of these recommendations. In the future, ASN will summarize the “how” of implementing the final report and its 10 recommendations. Keywords: clinical nephrology, diversity, kidney biopsy, nephrology, education, fellowship Background The American Society of Nephrology (ASN) Task Force on the Future of Nephrology was established in April 2022 to reconsider all aspects of the future of nephrology and determine how to best prepare nephrology fellows for the challenges and opportunities the future will bring. ASN established the task force in response to requests from the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) and the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) about training requirements in nephrology. The task force was asked to assess the core competencies that will remain a part of kidney care, identify gaps that must be filled to support the future of the specialty, and envision a future that will inspire and excite the next generation of nephrologists, scientists, and other kidney health professionals—as well as make every effort possible to ensure high-quality care for the millions of people with kidney diseases. Factors Affecting the Future of Nephrology Much has changed in the specialty of nephrology during the 50 years since the Medicare End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) Program was established. The task force carefully considered these changes as it formulated its recommendations. During the past five decades, kidney care was synonymous with the lifesaving treatment of dialysis. While the kidney community understood the nuances of complex chronic kidney diseases, the broader health system, the public, government, and the media were mostly unaware of the need to consider kidney health. 1 , 2 Nephrologists and other health professionals who work in kidney care understand how kidney diseases stress our health care system. With the increased prevalence of risk factors and complications of kidney diseases ( e.g. , diabetes, obesity, and hypertension), a new paradigm is required to approach kidney care. Protecting kidney health and providing treatment choices—focusing on transplant first to people who suffer with kidney failure—must become priorities. With the increasing recognition of the importance of equity in health care, it is readily apparent that the prevalence of kidney diseases and our current approach to prevention and management is entrenched in disparity. 3 , 4 One third of the individuals who receive dialysis in the United States are Black/African American, although Black/African American people comprise only 13% of the US population. 5 , 6 On average, 100,000 people start dialysis each year, and 33% live in zip codes where more than 20% of the population lives below the poverty line. 5 , 6 Black/African Americans are less likely than White Americans to be identified as transplant candidates, referred for evaluation, or placed on the kidney transplant waitlist to receive kidney transplants. They are less likely to receive a living donor kidney transplant and also more likely to receive lower-quality kidneys, have organ offers declined for them, and have poorer transplant graft survival. Overall, “the current organ transplantation system is demonstrably inequitable.” 6 When the Medicare ESRD Program was established in 1972, in-center dialysis care became central to nephrology, and career opportunities focused on managing people with kidney failure in dialysis units. This dialysis-centric focus on treating kidney failure must change in order for the public health challenge to be understood, managed, and ultimately overcome. In July 2019, the US government articulated its vision for the future of kidney care in the Advancing American Kidney Health (AAKH) initiative, which shifted the emphasis from treating kidney failure to promoting kidney health. The three major goals of the AAKH initiative are ( 1 ) preventing kidney failure through better diagnosis, treatment, and incentives for preventive care; ( 2 ) increasing patient choice, including by encouraging higher value-based care (which are intended to provide “better care for individuals,” “better health for populations,” and “lower cost”), educating patients on treatment alternatives, and advancing the development of artificial kidneys; and ( 3 ) enhancing access to kidney transplants. 7 Supporting the second goal of the AAKH initiative, the US government recently implemented care delivery models through the Center for Medicare & Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) to incentivize nephrologists, other health professionals, and dialysis facilities from accountable care organizations to manage care for people with kidney diseases. These models attempt to provide “strong financial incentives…to delay the onset of dialysis and to incentivize kidney transplantation.” 8 The future of nephrology also involves changes in payment models, new incentive systems, and how health care is organized. Starting in 2021, the 21st Century Cures Act eliminated regulations that previously prohibited people with kidney failure from enrolling in Medicare Advantage after they initiated dialysis. Since the Cures Act, an estimated 45% of dialysis patients have enrolled in Medicare Advantage insurance plans. 9 In addition to the momentum from CMMI''s models and Medicare Advantage, the private sector has started to emphasize kidney care. 10 For-profit companies, venture capital firms, and startups are partnering with nephrologists and nephrology practices to manage their patients with advanced kidney diseases. Focusing on patients at risk for developing kidney failure, these value-based care initiatives reward nephrologists, health professionals, dialysis facilities, and other providers “who successfully slow kidney disease progression and increase home dialysis and transplantation rates. Companies that succeed will profit by capturing some of the resulting savings to payers.” 11 After decades of lagging behind other diseases in therapeutic development, the kidney community has recently become much more optimistic about the development of new therapies. With the growth of precision medicine, augmented intelligence, digital health, and data to support real-world evidence, there is broad interest in developing new therapies specifically for kidney patients. For example, several recent advancements are aimed at intervening earlier, slowing progression of kidney diseases, and improving outcomes. The kidney community is excited about the promise that new drugs and existing sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors show to prevent the progression from diabetic and nondiabetic kidney diseases to kidney failure. 12 In addition to SGLT2 inhibitors, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists and glucagon-like peptide 1 agonists were approved between 2020 and 2022 as therapies to treat earlier phases of kidney diseases. 12 Challenges in Nephrology Fellowship Education As the models for delivering kidney care evolve and the revolution in kidney research intensifies, the educational continuum for nephrologists will evolve and benefit from incorporating areas of subspecialization that have become more common in the past 10–20 years. Nephrology must expand, enhance, and nurture its workforce to address the needs of people with all stages of kidney diseases, while continuing to prioritize patient-centered care. 1 , 2 , 13 , 14 Unfortunately, the attractiveness of nephrology as a career reached its nadir during the past decade, manifesting as a decrease in applicants to nephrology fellowship training programs and a consequent increase in unfilled positions. 15 – 17 Despite the high rate of unfilled positions, the total number of nephrology fellows has not significantly decreased as fellowship programs fill vacant spots with applicants chosen in the post-Match scramble. The number of applicants to nephrology fellowship training programs and the number of matched candidates have started to increase slowly during the coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic. 15 – 17 In the most recent match for appointment year (AY) 2023, there was a 7% increase from previous year in candidates who matched into nephrology fellowship positions. 18 This modest growth provides some hope, but 58% (103 tracks) of the 178 nephrology training tracks offered in AY 2023 filled, with 73% of 493 positions offered being claimed on Match Day in this year''s cycle. The pass rate for the initial ABIM nephrology certifying examination decreased over the past decade, raising concerns about the quality of nephrology fellowship training programs and their fellows. 19 Speculation about potential reasons for the decrease in nephrology applicants includes lower reimbursement for nephrologists compared with other medical specialties, concerns about the work-life balance of nephrologists, complexity of the patient population, and lack of exciting new therapies and treatments. 20 , 21 The future of kidney care will include a variety of new tools and interventions that will require exceptional clinicians to provide specialized care for extremely complex patients and high-quality general nephrology care in a variety of settings, including rural and inner-city communities. The task force was charged with reconsidering all aspects of the specialty''s future to ensure that nephrologists are prepared to provide high-quality care for all people with kidney diseases. This report reviews the process, rationale, and details (the “why” and the “what”) of the task force''s recommendations to meet this charge. Process and Guiding Principles In January 2022, ABIM requested ASN''s perspective on whether current procedural requirements for certification in nephrology should be maintained or expanded. 22 For years, the ABIM Nephrology Board, nephrology fellowship training program directors, nephrology fellows, and other stakeholders have debated this topic. 23 – 26 In January 2022, ACGME initiated its 10-year review of program requirements among the 17 internal medicine subspecialties, including nephrology. 27 As a part of this process, ACGME requested ASN''s perspective on nephrology training program requirements. ASN sent ABIM and ACGME a joint letter in March 2022 requesting 8 months to establish a task force, engage the kidney community to consider all aspects of the future of nephrology, and determine how to best prepare nephrology fellows for the challenges and opportunities the future will bring. ABIM and ACGME approved this request, and ASN charged the task force with making recommendations on the following: • Articulating the core skills, knowledge, and experiences in kidney medicine that every nephrology fellow must learn during fellowship training. • Enhancing competency-based medical education in nephrology. • Considering individualization of training and subspecialization in nephrology. • Identifying gaps in current fellowship education. • Addressing issues related to interprofessional practice and health care justice. The task force members were chosen by the ASN Council, which is the society''s governing body elected by the society''s members (in a contested election with three candidates per position). The members, advisors, and staff of the task force are listed in the Acknowledgments. Through weekly meetings starting in April 2022, the task force received input through listening sessions from multiple stakeholders, including but not limited to people living with kidney diseases, nephrology fellows, leaders of nephrology fellowship training programs, nephrology division directors, private practice nephrologists, and chief medical officers of dialysis organizations (Figure 1 ). The structure of these listening sessions varied depending on the stakeholders who participated in the sessions. For almost all sessions, specific questions were developed and shared with the participants ahead of time and served as a starting point for the discussions. By design, these sessions were meant to be open-ended with minimal structure to facilitate the expression of ideas and opinions by the participants. Notes were taken for each session, and all sessions were video recorded and viewed by task force members if they could not attend in real time (most were at all sessions). Themes were identified based on the discussions and played an important role in the development of the recommendations. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Timeline and activities of the ASN Task Force on the Future of Nephrology. ABIM, American Board of Internal Medicine; ACC COCATS, American College of Cardiology''s Core Cardiology Training Symposium; ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; AIRE, Advancing Innovation in Residency Education; ASH, American Society of Hematology; ASN, American Society of Nephrology; CMO, chief medical officers; TPD, training program directors. The listening sessions were in addition to input provided by individual nephrologists, ASN committees (particularly the ASN Workforce and Training Committee), other kidney organizations, advisors to the task force from ABIM and ACGME, and other specialty medical societies (American College of Cardiology and American Society of Hematology). The task force used a data-driven approach to evaluate the current state of nephrology training and practice that included information from the following: • ABIM Procedural Requirements Survey of board-certified nephrology diplomates 28 and through sharing of the nonpublic components of the survey (F.S. McDonald, MD, ABIM personal communication, May 2022). • ASN 2022 Procedural Requirement Survey of nephrology fellowship training program directors (U.C. Brewster, MD, and S.M. Sozio, MD, personal communication, July 2022). • The 2022 iteration of ASN''s annual Nephrology Fellow Survey. 17 • ASN Data Resource Center''s reports regarding the current and future workforce, nephrology practice patterns, and fellows'' assessments of educational needs. 29 , 30 In formulating recommendations for the future of nephrology, the task force agreed on three guiding principles to serve as the anchor for the recommendations. The task force wanted to ensure each recommendation was grounded in these principles that are relevant to the future of nephrology and consequently to the future of nephrology fellowship education. Each recommendation needed to promote the following: 1. Just, equitable, and high-quality care for people living with kidney diseases. 2. The value of nephrology as a specialty to nephrologists, the future nephrology workforce, the health care system, the public, and government. 3. Innovation and personalization of nephrology education across the scope of medical training. Recommendations to Redefine the Future of Nephrology To redefine the future of nephrology, the task force makes the following recommendations (Figure 2 ): 1. Enhance competency-based nephrology education. 2. Establish individualized pathways to meet career goals. 3. Emphasize personalized care. 4. Reconsider expectations for training in procedures. 5. Close gaps in current nephrology training. 6. Promote the well-being of nephrology fellows. 7. Prioritize diversity, equity, inclusion, and health care justice. 8. Ensure equal opportunities for all nephrologists. 9. Foster interprofessional and interdisciplinary practice. 10. Inspire lifelong learning. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Ten recommendations for redefining the future of nephrology. The rationale (“why”) and specifics (“what”) of the recommendations are presented in Table 1 . They are organized in two parts. The first five recommendations are nephrology specific and directed at nephrology fellowship training programs accredited by ACGME as well as initial certification by ABIM. Recommendations six through 10 recognize how health care is evolving in the United States and beyond. Although also directed at ACGME-accredited nephrology fellowship training programs and ABIM''s initial certification, these recommendations are broader and will require partnerships with other medical specialties. Table 1. Recommendations of the American Society of Nephrology Task Force on the Future of Nephrology Why? What? Recommendation 1: Enhance competency-based nephrology education. Nephrology must enhance its approach to competency-based education by defining and standardizing three levels of competency across nephrology fellowship training programs. • Establishes knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes expected for all nephrology fellows • Enables all nephrology fellows to advance in their level of competency on the basis of their individualized interests and needs 32 , 33 • Is customized to meet different learning abilities and can produce more efficient, effective, and valuable outcomes • Nephrology must adopt a model of competency-based education, such as the American College of Cardiology''s Core Cardiology Training Symposium (COCATS). 31 Table 2 defines the three levels (I, II, and III) and description of these competencies • To standardize nephrology training, ASN must develop milestones for these levels of competency building on the foundation of current ACGME nephrology milestones 34 • The development of specific milestones will occur during the implementation phase of these recommendations and will engage appropriate expert stakeholders • Nephrology fellowship training program directors and other leaders must inform applicants whether levels II and III training is provided by their program Recommendation 2: Establish individualized pathways to meet career goals. Nephrology must establish individualized pathways to provide opportunities for fellows to explore advanced specialized care and other career goals in more depth. • Recognizes that the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes necessary for providing high-quality care to people with kidney diseases are expanding rapidly • Increases the likelihood that nephrology will become a more popular career destination for medical students and residents because the overall health care system values definable expertise 33 • Assists nephrologists dedicated to research and clinical trials in their development of fundamental insights into kidney physiology and expertise in designing trials that will lead to the development of kidney disease–modifying therapies • The second year of nephrology fellowship training must be leveraged to establish these individualized pathways (level II competency) • In addition to expected level I competency in these areas, priority areas for more advanced training (level II, and in limited cases, level III) could include but are not limited to (in alphabetical order) advanced dialysis care including new technologies, critical care, glomerular diseases, hypertension, inherited kidney diseases/genetics, interventional nephrology, onco-nephrology, palliative care, prevention and population health (including social determinants of health), transplantation, and women''s health 35 , 36 • Individualized pathways must also include innovative approaches in other areas in which nephrology fellows may have specialized interest, such as medical education, public policy, business administration, and leadership 32 , 37 • Because the decline in the number of fellows interested in careers as researchers in nephrology is alarming, fellowship programs involved in research training must invest in developing mentorship and financial structures to support nephrology-focused researchers, including clinical trialists. It is likely that research-focused careers will require level III competency Recommendation 3: Emphasize personalized care. Nephrology must emphasize personalized care to optimize kidney health and increase patient choice, including early intervention, transplantation, and dialysis. • Supports the goals of US government policy outlined in the AAKH initiative 7 • Emphasizes that kidney transplantation is the optimal form of treatment for kidney failure and should be promoted as the first form of treatment if there is not an absolute contraindication • Recognizes home-based modalities for kidney replacement therapy are often preferred options • Expands nephrologists'' expertise and enhances fellowship training in counseling and managing patients on these therapies, which currently lags behind nephrologists'' comfort with in-center hemodialysis • Every fellow in every nephrology fellowship training program must be trained to level I competence in the prevention and early diagnosis of kidney diseases, kidney transplantation, and dialysis (particularly home therapies), which likely means strengthening the current ACGME Nephrology Program Requirements in these areas, especially home hemodialysis. 36 Areas of training must include ( 1 ) longitudinal management of kidney transplant recipients and people with kidney failure treated in the home, including competency in engaging patients on home therapies to ensure patient-centered care; ( 2 ) technical and regulatory aspects of home therapies; and ( 3 ) emerging technologies and treatments • Interested nephrology fellowship training programs may provide training to level III competence in kidney transplantation as is currently being performed • Interested nephrology fellowship training programs may provide more advanced training to levels II and III competence in home therapies • Consideration should be given to the formal recognition of centers of excellence for home therapies at which a third year of training can be performed. A potential model for such centers of excellence is the American Society of Hematology''s Hematology-Focused Fellowship Training Program 38 Recommendation 4: Reconsider expectations for training in procedures. All fellows must have the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes to make decisions about the indications for both performance and complications of placement of temporary vascular access for hemodialysis and percutaneous biopsy of both autologous and transplanted kidneys (level I competency). • Maximizes patient safety and aligns with the realities of current (and likely future) nephrology practice • Increases integrity in nephrology fellowship training because many fellows report not being trained in the placement of temporary vascular access for hemodialysis or percutaneous biopsy of both autologous and transplanted kidneys • Increases the likelihood that nephrology will embrace evolving procedures safely, appropriately, and more quickly • Recognizes the growing importance of POCUS in nephrology care and the POCUS skills of incoming fellows 39 , 40 • Fellows must provide effective counsel to people with kidney diseases about these procedures, be knowledgeable about the interpretation and clinical application of the results, and be prepared to manage any adverse events (level I competency) • The existing requirement for training in the placement of temporary vascular access for hemodialysis as well as percutaneous biopsy of both autologous and transplanted kidneys should be removed as follows: ( 1 ) ACGME Nephrology Program Requirements and ( 2 ) Requirements for ABIM Initial Certification in Nephrology • Nephrology fellowship programs may provide training to competence (level II) for fellows interested in placement of temporary vascular access for hemodialysis as well as percutaneous biopsy of both autologous and transplanted kidneys • Training to competence is not a requirement for all programs but is highly encouraged and provides a way for nephrology training programs to differentiate themselves and for fellowship applicants to select programs that align with their career goals • Nephrology fellowship training programs can require their fellows to achieve competency for these procedures if they so choose • Nephrology fellowship programs should be prepared to provide training opportunities in new and evolving procedures for interested trainees as they arise (such as placement of novel vascular access devices) • Nephrology fellowship training programs must offer higher-level training in nephrology-focused Point-of-Care Ultrasound (POCUS), such as the assessment of kidney structure, volume status, and dialysis access. However, POCUS should not be included in the ACGME Nephrology Program Requirements or as a requirement for ABIM Initial Certification in Nephrology 41 Recommendation 5: Close gaps in current nephrology training. Nephrology must provide additional education, or partner with other entities, to close currently identified gaps in nephrology training. • Improves the quality of health care for people with kidney diseases • Increases the overall value of nephrology • Helps address gaps in current fellowship training identified through surveys of fellows and focus groups of relevant stakeholders 30 • This additional training must support competency-based nephrology education (goal 1) and individualized pathways (goal 2) • Although all gaps in training must be eliminated, each nephrology fellowship training program can prioritize additional training differently, with potential opportunities being (but not limited to) ( 1 ) patient-centered care, including patient engagement, activation, and rehabilitation within the context of training; ( 2 ) kidney health disparities; ( 3 ) delivery of nephrology care, including regulatory aspects of dialysis care, financial operations of nephrology practice, telehealth, prevention and population health, including social determinants of health, quality improvement, and patient safety, including value-based care and mental health conditions; ( 4 ) clinical skills, including assessment of vascular and peritoneal access for dialysis therapies, women''s health, and kidney pathology; and ( 5 ) emerging technologies and treatments, including the concept of sustainability and the evolving area of “green nephrology” to address the climate effect of kidney care Recommendation 6: Promote the well-being of nephrology fellows. Nephrology must prioritize the well-being of nephrology fellows, which will improve the overall environment in academic settings and beyond. • Underscores that fellow well-being is essential to maximizing patient safety, quality of care, the health of fellows, and the attractiveness of nephrology as a career to future applicants • Helps address concerns about how the COVID-19 pandemic affected health care professionals, including trainees • Attempts to address other causes of burnout aside from the pandemic • All nephrology fellowship programs must ( 1 ) adhere fully to the ACGME Common Program Requirements to support a fellow''s “psychological, emotional, and physical well-being” and “proactive attention to life inside and outside of medicine” 27 and ( 2 ) sustain a learning and working environment that embodies a culture of respect and accountability for fellow well-being, adheres strictly to clinical and educational work hours, manages work intensity and the distribution of workload in ways consistent with maintaining fellow well-being, eliminates any form of harassment or mistreatment in the learning environment, and promotes a culture that allows safe reporting and provides tools for fellows to maintain well-being beyond fellowship training to their ongoing career in medicine to support the sustainability of the nephrology workforce • Maintaining an environment with these characteristics is essential to maximizing patient safety, quality of care, the health of fellows, and the attractiveness of nephrology to future applicants • Although this recommendation focuses on the well-being of fellows, the kidney community should also join with other medical specialties to improve the overall well-being of all physicians and health professionals, which has declined dramatically during the COVID-19 pandemic, including faculty and staff affiliated with nephrology fellowship training programs Recommendation 7: Prioritize diversity, equity, inclusion, and health care justice. Nephrology must prioritize diversity, equity, and inclusion as well as promote health care justice in all aspects of the specialty, especially fellowship training. • Attempts to address the fact that a disproportionate number of the more than 37 million people with kidney diseases in the United States are Black/African American, Hispanic/Latinx, Indigenous or Native American, Asian American, and Native Hawaiian or other Pacific Islanders and have a lower socioeconomic status 42 • To accomplish this recommendation, every nephrology fellowship training program must ○ Maintain an inclusive and equitable learning and work environment. ○ Ensure fellows have the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes to recognize and address issues of systemic racism and other injustices in health care, science, and kidney medicine. ○ Expand the reach of fellowship recruitment to broadly diverse applicants and provide the necessary support to ensure their success. ○ Provide additional support to fellows from backgrounds underrepresented in medicine. ○ Educate fellows to deliver culturally sensitive care and shared decision making. ○ Offer fellows opportunities to train in areas of population health and public policy to equip nephrologists with tools to address systemic racism. • Recognizes that the disparity in kidney health of these races and ethnicities is unacceptable, as is well documented • Underscores the fact that teams, medical specialties, and associations that embrace diversity, equity, and inclusion make better decisions, are more innovative, perform at a higher level, experience less turnover, are considered more satisfying workplaces, and are financially more profitable Recommendation 8: Ensure equal opportunities for all nephrologists. Nephrology must work toward ensuring all nephrologists—including allopathic , osteopathic , IMGs , and US-IMGs—have equal opportunities in the United States. • Recognizes that over the past 5 years, the number of graduates from osteopathic medical schools and US-IMGs (US citizens who graduated from medical schools outside the United States) who matched into nephrology fellowship positions has increased 16 • Acknowledges that IMGs are vital members of the physician workforce in the United States, particularly the nephrology workforce. 43 Currently, IMGs represent more than 25% of US physicians, 50% of US nephrologists, and 70% of fellows in US nephrology fellowship training programs 10 , 15 , 19 , 44 – 46 , 48 • Embraces that following a 5-year transition, ACGME, AOA, and AACOM transitioned to a single accreditation system for graduate medical education in the United States on June 30, 2022 48 • Recognizes, however, that the United States lacks comprehensive policies to ○ Provide IMGs equal opportunities to reach their full potential. ○ Support IMGs in their essential roles in patient care, research, education, and administration. ○ Address the fact that much of the world, including the United States, faces dire shortages of physicians and other health professionals. • Accepts that many nephrology fellows are on J-1 visas (Exchange Visitor Visas), and therefore, opportunities for level III training (or the possibility of a third year of fellowship training) are more limited for fellows on J-1 visas because they must return to their home countries when they complete ACGME-approved training • Understands that navigating the immigration system, especially visas, places an undue hardship on IMGs and their families • Nephrology must ensure equal opportunities for all fellows and nephrologists to pursue their desired career pathway without bias to their professional focus, location or type of training, visa status, or any other educational or professional experience • With input from AAMC, medical specialty societies (such as ASN), and other stakeholders, the US federal government should develop comprehensive policies that ensure equal opportunities for all nephrologists, including IMGs, to pursue meaningful careers in the United States or their country of preference. A global nephrology workforce is needed to meet the needs of the more than 850 million people worldwide living with kidney diseases 47 • ASN should work toward providing all nephrologists, including IMGs, opportunities to reach their full potential as clinicians, researchers, educators, and administrators by working toward policies that ○ Ensure the success of the joint accreditation program initiated by ACGME, AOA, and AACOM. ○ Broaden J-1 visa waiver programs to provide people in underserved areas of the United States with access to nephrologists. ○ Support nephrologists and their families by removing burdens to adjusting status and shortening pathways to US citizenship (if desired) for those providing valuable care to people in the United States. ○ Expand access to public and private research funding for IMGs who are nephrologists and other kidney investigators working in US institutions. Recommendation 9: Foster interprofessional and interdisciplinary practice. Nephrology must emphasize the importance of interprofessional a and interdisciplinary practice that involves all members of the health care team in the care of people with kidney diseases . • Recognizes the importance of team-based care, interdisciplinary practice, and seamless continuity of care that involves collaboration with other disciplines of medicine • Helps improve health care outcomes and quality of life for people with kidney diseases and increases the likelihood that nephrology will benefit from efforts to screen people at risk for kidney disease, intervene earlier, take advantage of new therapies, improve medication effectiveness and safety, improve the quality of care, and reduce costs • Results in higher job and career satisfaction • Nephrology fellows must learn the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes to effectively function as members of interprofessional and interdisciplinary teams and to actively engage in interprofessional and interdisciplinary collaborative practice • By fostering interprofessional practice, nephrology can accelerate efforts to fill current gaps in training, such as ○ Clinical skills. ○ Kidney health disparities. ○ Delivery of nephrology care. ○ Patient-centered care, including patient engagement, activation, and rehabilitation within the context of training. • Each nephrology fellowship training program will likely prioritize potential interdisciplinary collaborations differently, with potential opportunities, including but not limited to (in alphabetical order) cardiology, critical care, endocrinology, family medicine, general internal medicine, obstetrics and gynecology, palliative care, pathology, pediatrics, radiology, rheumatology, and surgery Recommendation 10: Inspire lifelong learning. Nephrology must advance the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes of fellows over time, preparing them to deliver the highest quality of care possible for people with kidney diseases throughout their careers. • Advances the care of people with kidney diseases • Helps ensure nephrology is a successful field in the future • Increases personal and professional satisfaction, enhances individual development, and enhances active citizenship • All nephrology fellowship training programs must provide training in the knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes needed to embrace and support lifelong learning • ASN and other organizations that represent health care professionals must provide opportunities to ○ Support all members of the kidney care team. ○ Update and attain new competencies for members of the kidney care team. ○ Recognize these competencies. Open in a new tab ASN, American Society of Nephrology; ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; AAKH, Advancing American Kidney Health; POCUS, point-of-care ultrasound; ABIM, American Board of Internal Medicine; COVID-19, coronavirus 2019; IMG, international medical graduates; AAMC, Association of American Medical Colleges; AOA, American Osteopathic Association; AACOM, American Association of Colleges of Osteopathic Medicine. a The interprofessional kidney care team should include advanced practice providers, care managers, community health workers, dietitians, nephrologists, nurses, occupational therapists, pharmacists, physical therapists, physician assistants, psychologists, researchers, social workers, and others as appropriate to the needs of the patient and family. Next Steps The ASN Task Force on the Future of Nephrology''s 10 recommendations is the result of a detailed process of engaging the kidney community and other stakeholders. These recommendations provide a strategic foundation for reimagining nephrology fellowship training to best prepare fellows for the future of nephrology, while simultaneously envisioning this future outlined by the three guiding principles described earlier. Finally, the recommendations attempt to align with public and private efforts to restructure kidney care in this country, including the federal government''s 2019 AAKH initiative and recent investments by the private sector. 7 The process, approach, and results of developing the recommendations allowed ASN to effectively respond to the requests from ABIM and ACGME for input on the future of nephrology fellowship training and certification. The report of the task force, which this manuscript summarizes, was sent to both ABIM and ACGME as a response to their initial requests. Of course, as fully independent educational regulators entrusted by the medical profession, ABIM and ACGME will make the final decisions on the initial certification of nephrologists and the requirements for accreditation of nephrology fellowship training programs, respectively. The ASN Council (the society''s governing body) voted unanimously to approve the task force''s final report (and 10 recommendations), committing ASN to achieving these goals, ideally with the broader kidney community. Now that the task force has outlined the strategy for reimagining nephrology fellowship education to envision the future of nephrology, the ASN Council and staff will work through the society''s committees, task forces, and panels as well as partner with other members of the kidney community and other stakeholders to implement the 10 recommendations. Clearly, accomplishing the first five recommendations will require a major investment from ASN and a realignment of the society''s priorities, especially related to undergraduate, graduate, and continuing education. At the same time, implementing recommendations six through 10 will require ASN to partner with other members of the kidney community and organizations representing other medical specialties, such as primary care, cardiology, and endocrinology. At a more granular level, ASN will need to define a consistent taxonomy, lexicon, and terminology in nephrology; inventory and publicize current offerings for individualized pathways at ACGME-accredited training programs; ensure the updated fellowship training program requirements meet the objectives for level 1 competency (Table 2 ); and coordinate with existing initiatives (by ASN, such as the society''s Home Dialysis Task Force, or by other members of the kidney community) to develop necessary curricula for high-priority gaps in nephrology. To implement the recommendations, ASN and other stakeholders will need to work with nephrology fellowship training program directors and other academic leaders to overcome significant challenges, such as required time and support for program directors, 49 especially for smaller fellowship programs or programs located in underserved areas; availability of faculty expertise; and limitations of the patient population served by some training institutions. Table 2. Competency levels for recommendation 1 Competency Description Level I • Expected knowledge, skills, values, and attitudes of every graduating nephrology fellow • Accomplished during a standard 2-year training program in nephrology, often the focus of the first 12 months of training Level II • Training beyond general nephrology that provides fellows with opportunities to perform advanced procedures or clinical care • Level II can be achieved during the standard 2-year nephrology fellowship program, depending on the fellow''s career goals and use of elective periods Level III • A higher degree of proficiency that offers distinct career opportunities in specialized areas of nephrology • Level III cannot generally be achieved during the standard 2-year nephrology fellowship and requires advanced training in a specialized program, often during a third year of training • The use of a third year of training will be limited to very specific educational programs ( e.g. , transplant nephrology) Open in a new tab Providing opportunities for research is critical for the future of nephrology, and the task force did not intend to minimize the importance of research pathways. Recommendation 2 emphasizes the importance of individualized pathways and relevant support for research. However, the task force concluded that addressing concerns about the research workforce in nephrology will require an entirely separate, dedicated, and well-resourced effort. ASN should pursue such an undertaking as soon as possible, perhaps working directly with organizations such as the Association of American Physicians, American Society for Clinical Investigation, and the American Physician Scientists Association. In addition to making recommendations regarding nephrology fellowship education, the task force felt it was important for ASN—as the major professional society for the specialty, the home of nephrology fellowship training program directors (through the society''s Workforce and Training Committee), the home of nephrology fellows (through complimentary membership and other programs), and the sponsor of the nephrology match (through the National Resident Matching Program''s Medicine and Pediatric Specialties Match), and whose input was sought by both ACGME and ABIM—to define metrics for success and a process for evaluating, modifying, and ensuring accountability. As part of this important process, ASN needs to continue to work closely with nephrology fellowship training programs to assist with meeting new program requirements from ACGME and ABIM and build consensus regarding the implementation of the recommendations. The implementation process will likely force ASN to consider adjusting some of the recommendations to meet the realities of a changing environment in health care, medicine, science, and medical education. Although it is possible to foresee some of these realities (such as more physicians becoming employed), other changes are difficult to predict (such as which therapies currently in the developmental pipeline will reach the market). As such, ASN must embrace adaptability, flexibility, and humility. Limitations In agreeing to unanimously approve its final report and recommending that ASN implement the report''s 10 recommendations, the ASN Task Force on the Future of Nephrology acknowledges several limitations of this important effort. The task force was launched in response to requests from ABIM and ACGME. Because ABIM and ACGME certify adult nephrologists and accredit physician training, respectively, the task force focused on only adult nephrologists and not other health professionals, the broader system, pediatric nephrology, or patients. The task force also understood that it could only focus on what is known today and what can be reasonably predicted in the future. In other words, ASN and the rest of the kidney community will need to be adaptable in trying to implement these recommendations moving forward. Along these lines, the task force appreciated that nephrology, as a medical specialty, operates within a broader context that includes other medical specialties, other disciplines of health professionals, a time of greater patient-centeredness, and health care systems that are evolving daily. Finally, the task force focused on the “why” and the “what” of its recommendations, recognizing that the “how” will take even more effort. The American Association of Kidney Patients has declared the 2020s as the “Decade of the Kidney.” 50 As further evidence of that fact, the task force hopes that ASN and the rest of the kidney community will spend the remainder of this decade and beyond implementing these 10 recommendations to redefine the future of nephrology. Acknowledgments The ASN Task Force on the Future of Nephrology included Sharon Anderson, MD, FASN, Portland, Oregon; Samira S. Farouk, MS, MD, FASN, New York, New York; Keisha Gibson, MPH, MD, FASN, Chapel Hill, North Carolina