INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3225,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11387023','American Society of Nephrology Kidney Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars (TREKS) Program: A 10-Year Interim Analysis.','Rubin M, Lecker SH, Ramkumar N, Sozio SM, Hoover RS Jr, Zeidel ML, Ko BS.','2024','tutored looming tutored yearlong desert mdibl uchicago slated mdibl uchicago weeklong mdibl uchicago hikes kayaking acadia mdibl boat cruise baseball uchicago tutored preidentified finishes uchicago tutored mdibl uchicago mdibl uchicago mdibl desert uchicago praised tutored mdibl shocked mdibl camaraderie firsthand acadia amazing cherish amazing amazing uchicago fantastic fence reinspired amazing amazing consulate glimpse awesome mdibl desert tutored uchicago directories postresidency subspecialize postresidency subspecialize forgiveness weeklong slate advertise deploys emailing subspecializing mdibl precourse promisingly uchicago admittedly r25 t35 u2c tl1 r25 t35 u2c tl1 uchicago maursetter mdibl uchicago bryce mciver tishena jsn e664 lecker zeidel molly molly molly lecker nirupama ramkumar molly zeidel jsn e663 mdibl lederer lebowitz molitoris molitoris caskey ashoor weidemann elenberg maursetter publicationsbooks 2015_acgme_databook_document publicationsbooks 2016_acgme_databook_document_locked publicationsbooks 2017_acgme_databook_document publicationsbooks 2018_acgme_databook_document publicationsbooks 2019_acgme_databook_document publicationsbooks 2020_acgme_databook_document publicationsbooks 2021_acgme__databook_document publicationsbooks 2022_acgme__databook_document publicationsbooks 2023_acgme_databook_document romeo lash gabbay dgaa134 aberg ciad416 miloslavsky marston baudy thakar milewicz dermody brass rescuing jci84170 furth propagating ketchum rebuilding mattana bayliss sfz108 u2c 20u2c 2dtl1 20program 20or 20by 20the 20niddk 2fkuh kdsap translatable kdsap jcq janech','Abstract In response to decreasing numbers of individuals entering into nephrology fellowships, the American Society of Nephrology launched Kidney Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars (TREKS) to stimulate interest in nephrology among medical students, graduate students, and postdoctoral fellows. The program combines a 1-week intensive exposure to kidney physiology with a longitudinal mentorship program at the participants'' home institutions. Ten years in, an analysis was conducted to assess its effectiveness. We surveyed participants to assess their opinions regarding nephrology before and after the course and followed them longitudinally to determine their career choices. TREKS applicants who were not selected to participate were used as a comparison group. Three hundred eighty-one people participated in the program, and 242 completed the survey. After TREKS, both medical students and graduate students showed increased interest in nephrology, with rank scores of 5.6±0.2 before to 7.5±0.1 after the course for medical students (mean±SD, n =189, P = 0.001) and 7.3±0.3 to 8.7±0.3 ( n =53, P = 0.001) for graduate students. In long-term follow-up, TREKS medical students chose a nephrology pipeline residency at a higher rate than medical students overall (57% versus 31%, P = 0.01) and TREKS applicants who did not participate (47% versus 31%, P = 0.04). Nephrology fellowship rates for these groups exceeded the general population but did not significantly differ between TREKS participants and applicants. Doctor of Philosophy students and postdoctoral TREKS participants had a higher rate of participation in nephrology research compared with TREKS applicants (66% versus 30%, P = 0.01). In summary, the American Society of Nephrology Kidney TREKS program has demonstrated that it can increase interest in nephrology in the short term and increase the number of individuals going into nephrology careers. This long-term effect is most evident in Doctor of Philosophy students and postdoctoral participants. Further study is needed to assess the impact of TREKS on enrollment in nephrology fellowship programs. Keywords: nephrology, medical education Introduction The decline in individuals choosing careers in nephrology has been well documented over the past several years. 1 – 3 From 2002 to 2011, the number of US medical graduates entering into an adult nephrology fellowship declined by 26%, and there have been fewer fellowship applicants than positions since 2013. 4 , 5 At the same time, the need for nephrologists is increasing worldwide. 6 Pediatric nephrology has seen similar waning interest and also faces a looming workforce shortage. 7 In response, the then–American Society of Nephrology (ASN) Workforce Committee introduced a program intended to reverse declining interest in nephrology careers among medical and graduate students. ASN Kidney Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars (TREKS) combines a 1-week intensive exposure to kidney physiology with a yearlong longitudinal mentorship program at the participants'' home institutions. 8 Since then, the program has expanded to include two program sites: Mount Desert Island Biological Laboratory (MDIBL), started in 2013, and the University of Chicago (UChicago), added in 2017. Both programs are ongoing and are slated to continue in 2024. Ten years into the program, we undertook an analysis to assess its effectiveness. Specifically, we surveyed participants in the program to assess their opinions regarding nephrology both before and after the course and followed them longitudinally to determine their long-term career choices. We report the results of this analysis. Kidney TREKS Program Design Program Description Because students often select their career paths early in their training, the Kidney TREKS program targets medical students and graduate students (Figure 1 ). Students travel and stay at either the MDIBL or UChicago campus for the weeklong program. The program consists of 3-and-a-half-day–long modules, followed by a half day of a social activity with program faculty. At MDIBL, the focus of the modules is kidney physiology, gaining in-depth and hands-on understanding by replicating the classic experiments of renal physiology and combining them with modern ones. 8 At UChicago, the modules focus on aspects of clinical nephrology, such as clinical cases, physiology, transplantation, and health disparities. Both programs are highly interactive, with active student participation and presentations. Faculty interaction at TREKS comes in the form of social events that take place throughout the week: hikes and kayaking through and around the Acadia National Park while at MDIBL, or an escape room, architecture boat cruise, or baseball game at UChicago. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Schema of typical medical student and graduate training timelines and points of contact in the TREKS program. TREKS, Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars. After the program, participants are matched with a nephrology mentor at their home institution. These mentors are either a preidentified mentor (in the case of many graduate students) or identified in consultation with the nephrology fellowship training program director at their home institution. ASN then contacts the mentor–mentee pairs quarterly during the first year of the mentorship with discussion questions and resources designed to foster engagement. After the first year, ASN asks that the mentors serve in the role until the student finishes school and that they meet at least three times a year. TREKS participants are also invited to participate in the ASN Kidney Students and Residents (STARS) program. The STARS program provides onsite mentoring along with tailored events and networking for students and residents at the Annual Meeting at ASN Kidney Week. Participant Follow-Up Immediately after their participation in the TREKS program, participants were surveyed about their interests and their experiences in the program (survey instrument listed in supplement). They were also followed longitudinally to determine the effect of participation in the program on their future careers. These surveys, studies, and analysis were classified as a quality improvement project of an educational initiative by the UChicago Institutional Review Board and, therefore, exempt from Institutional Review Board approval as human subjects research. Statistical Analyses Paired t test, chi-square test, and ANOVA analysis were conducted to assess statistical significance between groups using SPSS. A P value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results of the Kidney TREKS Program Participant Demographics Between 2013 and 2022, we received 1103 applicants to the TREKS program for 444 positions. Table 1 shows the self-identified sex and ethnicity of TREKS participants at both sites as well as applicants who did not participate in the program. In general, both applicants (53% female) and participants (52% female) were more likely to identify as female ( P = 0.03 for all). Medical students (76%) predominated over graduate students (20%) and postdoctoral students (3%) ( P = 0.03). There were no significant differences between applicants and participants for sex, race or ethnic background, trainee type, or geographic region. Table 1. Demographics of Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars participants and applicants Demographic MDIBL Participants UChicago Participants All Participants Applicants—MDIBL Applicants—UChicago Applicants—All Sex, n (%) Female 147 (51) 88 (57) 235 (53) a 191 (48) 149 (56) 340 (52) a Male 118 (41) 63 (41) 181 (41) 147 (37) 103 (39) 250 (38) Other/did not disclose 25 (8) 3 (2) 28 (6) 57 (15) 12 (45) 69 (10) Ethnicity, n (%) Asian 74 (26) 49 (32) 123 (28) 91 (23) 67 (25) 158 (24) Black 24 (8) 13 (9) 37 (8) 19 (5) 19 (7) 38 (6) Hispanic, Latino, or Spanish origin 27 (9) 16 (10) 43 (10) 25 (6) 32 (12) 57 (9) Multiracial 6 (2) 5 (3) 11 (3) 7 (2) 12 (5) 19 (3) Other 17 (6) 9 (6) 26 (6) 19 (5) 12 (5) 31 (5) White 117 (40) 49 (32) 166 (37) 183 (46) 96 (36) 279 (42) Did not disclose 25 (8) 13 (8) 38 (9) 50 (13) 26 (10) 76 (11) Trainee type, n (%) Graduate student 67 (23) 24 (16) 91 (20) 58 (15) 12 (4) 70 (11) Medical student 210 (72) 126 (81) 336 (76) a 286 (72) 243 (92) 529 (80) a Postdoctoral fellow 11 (4) 4 (3) 15 (3) 21 (5) 4 (2) 25 (4) Other 2 (1) 0 (0) 2 (1) 30 (8) 5 (2) 35 (5) Region, n (%) Northeast 60 (21) 25 (16) 85 (22) 125 (19) Midwest 78 (27) 21 (14) 99 (26) 136 (20) South 98 (34) 38 (25) 136 (35) 189 (29) West 22 (7) 28 (18) 50 (13) 65 (10) Other 2 (1) 12 (7) 14 (4) 144 (22) Open in a new tab MDIBL, Mount Desert Island Biological Laboratory; UChicago, University of Chicago. a P = 0.03. TREKS Participants Attitudes Toward TREKS and Nephrology After the course concluded, 242 participants (63%) from a total of 381 participants completed the postcourse survey asking about the TREKS program and their nephrology interest ( Supplement 1 and Supplement 2 ). The survey was designed and piloted to assess specific elements of the program and the construct of interest in nephrology. Members of the ASN Workforce and Training Committee, many of whom are well-known educators and educational researchers, contributed to its development. TREKS participants were asked to review the modules in which they participated and provide comments about the course overall. Comments about the course praised the activities, but notably, faculty–student interaction and student–student interaction (Table 2 ). Table 2. Selected verbatim comments about the Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars program Site Comments MDIBL “I thought this event was a perfect way to get people involved in nephrology. As a scientist, I was so shocked at how cool MDIBL and kidney-related research were” “Everything about this program is unique. Getting to perform research at this historical lab improved the contextual understanding of the work. The physiology was very well taught. I feel the strongest benefit is the networking and camaraderie of the group” “One of the most rewarding parts of the TREKS program for me was getting to interact so much in a casual environment with the faculty and staff. I got to hear about the nephrology lifestyle firsthand and develop close relationships with the clinicians and researchers….And of course, all the outdoor activities in Acadia were simply amazing. This was an invaluable experience and one I will cherish for years to come” “I believe that programs like these are amazing in promoting nephrology as a career choice. More than anything, creating this community and seeing the amazing people within it truly makes me want to stay within the nephrology field. Similarly, I believe that strong nephrology mentors at each student''s home institution helps promote nephrology as a career choice” UChicago “I had a fantastic learning experience at TREKS and am so happy I was able to attend the program. Prior to TREKS, I was on the fence about pursuing nephrology…. However, being in this program with like-minded individuals who are also interested in changing the field of nephrology/impacting the lives of those with various kidney diseases reinspired me to pursue the field. This cohort of people specifically and the activities scheduled within the program reminded me of how much I love renal physiology and pathology” “It was an amazing experience—had it not been for this program, we would not have been exposed to nephrology at all. At least now it''s on my radar and I am considering a career in medicine more” “This was the most amazing academic activity I''ve ever been a part of. I''m beyond grateful for the opportunity. I learned a lot about the kidneys, I enjoyed speaking with the physicians (they seemed happy, motivational, encouraging), and this makes me more interested in nephrology. I LOVED going to the Mexican consulate, and I loved that the MDs let us run some of the consults” “Programs like TREKS are exactly that kind of experience that gives people a glimpse at nephrology and how awesome the people in nephrology are” Open in a new tab MDIBL, Mount Desert Island Biological Laboratory; TREKS, Tutored Research and Education for Kidney Scholars; UChicago, University of Chicago. Within the survey, participants were asked to rate their interest in pursuing a career in nephrology on a standard Likert scale of 1–10 (1 low, 10 high) before and after the course. 9 Scores were analyzed in aggregate and expressed as mean±SD. Among medical students, interest started at a rank of 5.6±0.2 and increased to 7.5±0.1 after the course ( n =189, P < 0.001). Interest in doctoral and postdoctoral participants started higher at 7.3±0.3 but similarly increased to 8.7±0.3 after the course ( n =53, P < 0.001). Medical Student Residency Enrollment We followed TREKS participants to see how participation in TREKS may have affected their future career plans. After each match day for the National Resident Matching Program Main Residency and Medicine and Pediatrics Specialties Matches, medical student participants who were estimated to be eligible to match to a residency or fellowship that year were contacted through email by ASN staff to self-report their match results using the survey. Response rates for these surveys were low, so we assessed residency status for most of the medical students by reviewing residency and fellowship directories, Doximity, LinkedIn, public match lists posted by institutions, and the National Provider Identifier database. A total of 206 participants entered residency between 2014 and 2022. Of these, 57% (117 individuals) entered an initial residency compatible with a future career in nephrology, defined as internal medicine (93), medicine-pediatrics (14), pediatrics (5), or pathology (5). Internal medicine, surgery, and pediatrics were the most common specialties for TREKS participants. Those not selected for TREKS and who started a residency between 2014 and 2022 were also followed (285 individuals). Among these, 47% of this cohort (134 individuals) entered into a nephrology pipeline residency, an internal medicine (98), medicine-pediatrics (5), pathology (5), or pediatrics (26) residency program. 10 – 18 On the basis of data from the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Data Resource Books, an average of 31% of all active full-time residents were participating in a nephrology pipeline specialty between 2014 and 2022. Both TREKS cohorts (participants and comparison group) entered into a nephrology pipeline specialty at a higher rate than overall medical student enrollment (57% versus 31%, P = 0.01). Notably, enrollment in a nephrology pipeline specialty was statistically higher in those individuals who participated in TREKS compared with applicants (47% versus 31%, P = 0.04) (Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Percent enrollment in nephrology pipeline residency of TREKS participants (blue) and applicants (orange) and nationally (red) (* P = 0.01 compared with national, # P = 0.05 compared with TREKS applicants). Medical Student Fellowship Enrollment We tracked the 117 TREKS participants (using a similar methodology to that discussed above) who entered into a nephrology pipeline specialty as they progressed to fellowship eligibility. Forty-four TREKS participants entered this stage in their training in 2022 or earlier. The postresidency career paths for all 44 individuals of this cohort have been identified, with ten (23%) going into a nephrology fellowship (Figure 3 ). Eighteen (41%) did not subspecialize. The 134 applicants from the comparison group who entered into a nephrology pipeline specialty were also tracked as they progressed to fellowship eligibility, with 64 members of the TREKS control group entering this stage in their training in 2022 or earlier. Postresidency career paths for all 64 individuals of this cohort have been identified, with 12 (19%) entering a nephrology fellowship (Figure 3 ). Twenty (31%) did not subspecialize. Both TREKS participants and applicants were more likely to enter a nephrology fellowship compared with national data. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Percent nephrology fellowship enrollment of TREKS participants (blue) and applicants (orange) and nationally (red) (* P = 0.01 compared with national). Graduate Student Research Fields We also tracked the careers of 38 graduate student participants, using the means described above as well as Google Scholar and Open Researcher and Contributor ID to determine employment and research focus. Two thirds (25 individuals) were active in nephrology research at the time they were tracked, significantly more than the 30% (seven participants) of the comparison group of 23 graduate student applicants not selected to participate in the program ( P = 0.04) (Figure 4 ). An additional three individuals in the TREKS group (8%) were determined to be active in research that is related to the field of nephrology (these fields include endocrinology, metabolism, and lipid research and diabetes), while an additional four individuals in the applicant group (17%) were determined to be active in research that is related to the field of nephrology (these four fields include immunology, hypertension, diabetes, and metabolic syndrome). Statistical significance remains when research in these related fields is considered ( P = 0.04). Figure 4. Open in a new tab Percent nephrology research of graduate program TREKS participants (blue) and applicants (orange) (* P = 0.05 when compared with TREKS applicants). Insights The decline in fellowship applicants in recent years is not unique to nephrology. Similar trends are being seen in infectious disease and endocrinology, among others. 18 – 20 Multiple approaches, including advocacy for student loan forgiveness, medical school mentorship, and higher salaries, have been proposed, but to our knowledge, no summer program similar to ASN Kidney TREKS is in operation. 19 – 21 In a study of medical students who do choose nephrology, one cohort cited interest in the subject (92%), work–life balance (73%), mentorship (70%), and subject exposure (66%) as key elements, and another identified nephrology faculty interaction with medical students, clinical exposure to nephrology, use of novel educational modalities, and early exposure to nephrology. 22 , 23 While no similar studies exist for Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) students, experts have suggested strong mentorship is key. 24 – 26 These findings match well with the TREKS program''s weeklong slate of science, clinical medicine, and close faculty interaction, and so our hope is that this approach will show promise in helping solve the nephrology workforce issue, especially because some 20–40% of nephrology fellows chose nephrology while in medical school. 4 , 27 Our analysis at 10 years since program inception demonstrates promising results for the TREKS program. To advertise the program each year, ASN deploys a marketing campaign through social media and email. ASN has a robust emailing list of ASN student members, nephrology training program directors, nephrology division chiefs, other kidney educators, medical and graduate student organizations, student special interest groups, and school officials (such as the deans of academic affairs) who are contacted each year. In addition, TREKS alumni from recent years are informed about each program cycle''s application opening and encouraged to spread the word about the program among their peers. This campaign has resulted in a number of applicants for TREKS that has far exceeded the number of offered positions. While this is promising, given that nephrology ranks among the least recognized specialties among medical students, more work needs to be done to raise the profile of nephrology among medical students. 28 In the short term, TREKS shows an effect on increasing interest in nephrology and kidney research among participants, with pre- and postcourse surveys showing a significant increase in interest in nephrology as a career. Among medical students, however, this increase in interest has yet to translate into increased numbers of nephrologists. A trend of increased nephrology fellowship enrollment in TREKS participants versus applicants did not meet statistical significance at the 10-year analysis mark. However, both groups greatly exceed the average nephrology enrollment among residents in general. On the basis of the annual ACGME Data Resource Book, between 2017 and 2022, an average of 3% of all active full-time fellows in a nephrology or pediatric nephrology pipeline specialty were subspecializing into either nephrology or pediatric nephrology. 12 – 16 While TREKS participants'' enrollment rates into a nephrology fellowship show no statistical difference when TREKS applicants are used as a comparator, PhD student and postdoctoral participants did choose nephrology-related work at a higher rate than TREKS applicants. Recruitment of these scientists has been tremendously successful, with two thirds of participants in this category active in nephrology in long-term follow-up. The underlying reasons for these differences in success are worthy of further study. Certainly, the appeal of the MDIBL program to PhD scientists is very strong and likely has a lot to do with TREKS’ success in PhD student recruitment, but analysis of precourse interest in nephrology suggests that recruiting medical students into nephrology may be a bigger lift. Entering the course, PhD students and postdoctoral participants rated their interest in nephrology as 7.3±0.3, while medical students rated their interest as 5.6±0.2 ( P < 0.001). This may indicate a greater level of differentiation toward a kidney-related field among the PhD group, perhaps resulting in higher rates of selecting a kidney-related career. While both groups significantly increased their interest by the end of the week, perhaps this lower starting point played a role in our observed trend. Alternatively, it may be simply too soon to see meaningful trends in medical student recruitment. Between 2014 and 2022, the ACGME Data Resource Book reported an average of 31% of all active full-time residents participating in an internal medicine, medicine-pediatrics, pathology, or pediatrics residency program. 10 – 18 Both TREKS participants and applicants enrolled in internal medicine or similar pipeline residencies at a higher rate compared with the general population of medical students, and promisingly, TREKS participants have a statistically higher rate compared with applicants. Only about half of these individuals have progressed to fellowship. In addition, only a very small number of participants from the UChicago program have reached the point of fellowship differentiation. This study is limited largely by the limitations of longitudinal follow-up. Medical and graduate student participants and applicants are tracked when they are estimated to have completed their Doctor of Medicine (MD), Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO), or PhD programs. Among PhD students, individuals in both cohorts were still in postdoctoral fellowship positions at the time of tracking and will be followed up in the future as they progress through their career. While LinkedIn, Google Scholar, and Research Gate have proved particularly helpful in following these individuals, our study is limited to publicly available information, which is by its nature incomplete and may not adequately encompass all nephrology-related work, such as online blogs, society work, and podcasts. In addition, our data of MD–PhD candidates is limited. The TREKS application does not have a separate category of MD–PhD under which to apply, and applicants generally pick either medical or graduate student as their applicant type at the time of application. While we will work to capture this information in the future, this information is not currently available, and therefore, we do not represent MD–PhD as a separate candidate and analyze their progress on the basis of their indicated applicant type. Another limitation is the lack of an assessment for the quality of mentoring at their home institution. While the TREKS participants and their mentors are asked to complete a feedback survey after 1 year of the relationship, this is admittedly a difficult area to quantify, but given the influence of a mentor on a student, an important one. In addition, one of the goals of the TREKS program is to ensure the nephrology workforce is representative of the US population and individuals with kidney diseases, of whom minoritized populations and those underrepresented in medicine ( i.e ., Black/African American, Latin/o/a/x, and Native Americans/Indigenous People) are at higher risk of progressing to kidney failure. TREKS participants are representative of these goals. However, at this time, the number of participants having reached differentiation precludes small group analysis. TREKS is not alone in its efforts to build the nephrology workforce. The National Institutes of Health has an R25-NIDDK Funded Summer Research Fellowship, T35, and U2C/TL1 programs aimed at enhancing interest in nephrology-related fields among undergraduates in the case of the R25, and medical and graduate students in the case of the T35 and U2C/TL1 programs. 26 , 29 These programs use strategies such as strong mentoring along with early and sustained exposure to nephrology research in an effort to attract and retain researchers in kidney-related fields. The closest parallels to the TREKS program are the Kidney Disease Screening and Awareness Program, an undergraduate student organization, and the Kidney Mentoring and Assessment Program for Students, a medical student program that parallels the structure of the Kidney Disease Screening and Awareness Program. 30 , 31 These programs are primarily targeted at raising community awareness and screening for kidney diseases but have demonstrated success in increasing awareness of nephrology and enhancing recruitment into nephrology at the undergraduate and individual medical school levels. 32 These programs have a strong community outreach component that the more clinical UChicago TREKS program has integrated into its curriculum. In conclusion, at its 10-year mark, the ASN Kidney TREKS program has demonstrated that it can improve interest in nephrology in the short term and substantially increase the number of individuals going into nephrology careers. This long-term effect is evident in PhD students and postdoctoral participants. Further study is needed to assess the effect of TREKS on nephrology fellowship enrollment and its effect on recruitment of individuals who are underrepresented in medicine. Supplementary Material jasn-35-1284-s001.pdf (1.4MB, pdf) Open in a new tab jasn-35-1284-s002.pdf (151.6KB, pdf) Open in a new tab Acknowledgments The authors gratefully acknowledge the contributions of the following individuals who helped develop and support this program: Laura McCann (ASN Vice President for Education and Career Development); Drs. Laura Maursetter, Mark Parker, and Scott Gilbert, the members of the ASN Workforce Committee; the faculty and staff at the MDIBL and UChicago sites, especially Bryce McIver and Tishena Wilson; and mentors at students'' home institutions. Disclosures Disclosure forms, as provided by each author, are available with the online version of the article at http://links.lww.com/JSN/E664 . Funding This work was supported by American Society of Nephrology. Author Contributions Conceptualization: Benjamin Ko, Stewart H. Lecker, Mark L. Zeidel. Data curation: Benjamin Ko. Formal analysis: Molly Rubin. Investigation: Benjamin Ko. Methodology: Benjamin Ko. Project administration: Molly Rubin. Writing – original draft: Benjamin Ko, Molly Rubin. Writing – review & editing: Robert S. Hoover, Benjamin Ko, Stewart H. Lecker, Nirupama Ramkumar, Molly Rubin, Stephen M. Sozio, Mark L. Zeidel. Supplemental Material This article contains the following supplemental material online at http://links.lww.com/JSN/E663 . Supplement 1 . 2023 ASN Kidney TREKS postcourse survey (MDIBL). Supplement 2 . 2023 ASN Kidney TREKS postcourse survey (University of Chicago). References 1. Lederer E, Lebowitz J. Current state of the workforce in nephrology. Adv Chronic Kidney Dis. 2020;27(4):281–290.e1. doi: 10.1053/j.ackd.2020.07.007 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Parker MG, Sozio SM. The future nephrology workforce: there will Be one. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol. 2021;16(11):1752–1754. doi: 10.2215/CJN.05040421 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Parker MG, Ibrahim T, Shaffer R, Rosner MH, Molitoris BA. The future nephrology workforce: will there be one? Clin J Am Soc Nephrol. 2011;6(6):1501–1506. doi: 10.2215/CJN.01290211 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Parker MG, Pivert KA, Ibrahim T, Molitoris BA. Recruiting the next generation of nephrologists. Adv Chronic Kidney Dis. 2013;20(4):326–335. doi: 10.1053/j.ackd.2013.03.004 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shaikh A, Patel N, Nair D, Campbell KN. Current paradigms and emerging opportunities in nephrology training. Adv Chronic Kidney Dis. 2020;27(4):291–296.e1. doi: 10.1053/j.ackd.2020.05.011 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Sozio SM, Pivert KA, Caskey FJ, Levin A. The state of the global nephrology workforce: a joint ASN-ERA-EDTA-ISN investigation. Kidney Int. 2021;100(5):995–1000. doi: 10.1016/j.kint.2021.07.029 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Ashoor I Weidemann D Elenberg E, et al. The pediatric nephrology workforce crisis: a call to action. J Pediatr. 2021;239:5–10.e4. doi: 10.1016/j.jpeds.2021.03.033 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Maursetter LJ Stern LD Sozio SM, et al. Enhancing nephrology career interest through the ASN kidney TREKS program. J Am Soc Nephrol. 2016;27(6):1604–1607. doi: 10.1681/ASN.2015101086 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Likert 