INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3233,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4953796','Diabetes and renal disease: who does what?','Jones RK, Hampton D, O''Sullivan DJ, Phillips AO.','2013','diabetologists diabetologists qof qof qof egfrs qof normoalbuminuria qof qof aneurin diabetologist progressors diabetologist diabetologist diabetologist progressors progressors progressors progressor progressors progressors progressor proteinuira diabetologists progressors progressors repatriation progressors progressors progressors progressors progressor progressors progressors diabetologists diabetologists diabetologist progressor progressors boag innes qjmed hcl052 nejmhpr041294 rychlik contrib vupputuri dc11 ckdguide tomson qjmed hcp030 gullion meran qjmed hcq151 skupien warram','ABSTRACT Care of patients with diabetes and chronic kidney disease (CKD) in the UK is divided between primary care, diabetologists and nephrology. In a retrospective analysis, we examined the distribution of care provision for patients with diabetes and CKD. Nephrology services see a minority of diabetic patients with CKD, but they see the majority of those with an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) of <30 ml/min. Of those followed in nephrology, 70% showed no evidence of progressive renal dysfunction. The nephrology cohort were significantly younger that those seen by primary care physicians or diabetologists. Half of the patients with diabetes and CKD seen in either the primary care and diabetology cohorts, with no nephrology input, had a rate of fall of eGFR of >5 ml/min/yr. This suggests that older age might deter referral to nephrology, which is based predominantly on CKD stage. This results in a significant proportion of patients with stable renal function being seen by nephrology, and in the under-referral of a large cohort of patients with progressive CKD. KEY WORDS: Chronic kidney disease, CKD, diabetes, epidemiology, nephropathy Introduction Classification of chronic kidney disease (CKD) on the basis of estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) and automated reporting of eGFR have focused attention on the prevalence of chronic kidney disease (CKD). 1 This has been further complemented in the UK by changes in primary care working patterns. In April 2004, a new contract for general practice, the General Medical Services (GMS) contract, was introduced, in which a significant proportion of practice income is derived from performance against targets in a new Quality and Outcomes Framework (QOF). The intended consequences of the new contractual arrangements were to reward quality of care rather than number of registered patients, to improve data capture and care processes, and to improve patient outcomes and doctors’ working conditions. 2 In 2006, in response to the heightened awareness of the increasing prevalence of renal disease, the revised QOF of the GMS contract added CKD to the previously identified domains of chronic disease. This revised QOF now requires general practitioners (GPs) to keep a renal register of patients with CKD who have eGFRs below 60 ml/min/1.73m 2 . The rationale behind the alterations in the reporting of renal function, and that underlying the recognition of patients with CKD in general practice disease registers, is to enable the early identification of CKD. The introduction of the CKD domain into QOF has therefore changed the level of emphasis on CKD patient care by highlighting the requirement to see and care for early and moderate CKD predominantly within primary care. The most common cause of renal failure in the western world is now diabetes mellitus, which accounts for 20–50% of all patients requiring renal replacement therapy. 3 Progression of diabetic nephropathy in the pre-dialysis phase from normoalbuminuria to overt proteinuria, as well as the provision of renal replacement therapy, is associated with increased medical care costs. 4 General practice QOF in the UK include not only CKD but also domains relating to diabetes, which, like the CKD QOF domains, reward primary care practices for completeness of processes and agreed clinical indicators. As a result, the majority of patients with the combination of diabetes and CKD are likely to be managed in primary care. Nevertheless, guidelines suggest that specialist care is appropriate for those patients with evidence of disease progression and the development of significant complications such as nephropathy. 5,6 As a result, the responsibility for the care of patients with diabetes and CKD in the UK is divided between primary care, diabetology and nephrology services. Specific guidelines have been published on how to manage the interface between these different healthcare settings in terms of patient referral. At present, very little data exist on the caseload of each of these areas of healthcare in managing patients with both diabetes and CKD. In this manuscript, we describe the characteristics of patients followed up in each setting. In particular, we describe the age, CKD stage and proportion of patients with CKD and with progressive CKD in each care setting. Methods Data were collected from the records of the clinical biochemistry department serving the whole of the Aneurin Bevan NHS Health Board, which covers five primary healthcare Local Health Boards and a total population of 560,000 people. Using the data for the year 2009, 6.5% of CKD patients were identified as having a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus, either by a positive glucose tolerance test or by having Haemoglobin A1c >48 mmol/mol. We were not able to differentiate between type I and type II diabetes, nor between diabetic nephropathy and non-diabetic CKD in diabetic patients. Following the initial screen, patients with a diagnosis of diabetes in 2009, a recorded eGFR of <60 ml/min, and follow-up biochemical data covering at least one year were included in the analysis. Patients were separated into those whose clinical care during 2009 was delivered by a primary care physician only, those seen additionally by a diabetologist and those seen by a nephrologist. Using all available biochemical data between January 2009 and April 2012 (and at least 12 months of follow-up data), the rate of decline of renal function was calculated as change in eGFR ml/min/year. Patients were defined as ‘progressors’ if the rate of reduction in their eGFR was >3 ml/min/year, stable if the change in eGFR was between −3 ml/min/year and +3 ml/min/year and improving if their eGFR rose by >3 ml/min/year. Albumin:creatine ratio (ACR) results were collected from the nearest time point to the date of the reported eGFR in 2009 that was the trigger for inclusion of the patient in the analysis. Statistical analysis was carried out by Students’ t-test and the Chi-squared (χ 2 ) test as appropriate, and p < 0.05 deemed statistically significant. Results Identification of patients with CKD From the initial screen of 26,759 CKD patients with a known diagnosis of diabetes in 2009, we identified 2,710 patients with an eGFR <60 ml/min and at least one year of biochemical follow-up data. The proportion of the diabetic patients with CKD (DM-CKD) were seen only in primary care (42.7%) was significantly larger than that seen by a diabetologist (36.0%, p < 0.0001). An even smaller proportion of DM-CKD patients were seen in nephrology (21.6%, p < 0.0001). Patients followed up in primary care only, were significantly older the patients who were also seen by a diabetologist or a nephrologist. Similarly, the patients who were seen by a nephrologist were significantly younger than those cared for in primary care alone or those who saw a diabetologist but no nephrologist. The cohort followed up by nephrology had the lowest eGFR (Table 1 ). The nephrology cohort had a significantly higher proportion of patients with stage 4–5 CKD (eGFR <30 ml/min), and these patients were significantly younger than the patients with stage 4–5 CKD in both the primary care and diabetes cohorts. Table 1. Basic characteristics and distribution of patients with diabetes and CKD between primary care, diabetes and nephrology services. Open in a new tab Analysis of the progression of CKD The change in the renal function of DM-CKD patients within each follow-up cohort is shown in Table 2 . The proportion of patients with progressive DM-CKD was no different in those seen in primary care, diabetes or nephrology. The patients followed up in primary care with progressive DM-CKD were, however, significantly older than the progressors in the diabetes and nephrology cohorts. In both the primary care and diabetes cohorts, 97% of those with progressive DM-CKD had stage 3 CKD. In the nephrology cohort, almost a third of the patients with progressive DM-CKD had stage 4–5 CKD. Table 2. Characteristics and distribution of patients with diabetes and CKD according to rate of progression of their renal disease, and according to their eGFR, between primary care, diabetes and nephrology services. Open in a new tab Patients with progressive DM-CKD were further sub-divided into mild, moderate and severe progressors on the basis of the rate of deterioration in eGFR with ‘mild’ defined as >3 but ≤5 ml/min/year, ‘moderate’ as >5 but ≤10, and ‘severe’ as >10 ml/min/year (Table 3 ). The smallest proportion of severe progressors was seen in the primary care cohort, although in absolute terms, the number of severe-progressor patients seen by primary carers alone was comparable that seen by nephrology. The severe progressors group in primary care were significantly older those followed up by diabetes or nephrology. The proportion of patients classified as severe progressors was no different between the diabetes and nephrology cohorts, and there was no difference in the ages of these two severe progressor groups. Table 3. Distribution of patients with diabetes, CKD and progressive renal impairment (defined as a fall in eGFR of ≥3 ml/min/yr) according to the rate of progression (categorised as mild, moderate and severe) between primary care, diabetes and nephrology services. Open in a new tab Proteinuira and follow-up characteristics The results of ACR for each of the patient cohorts are shown in Table 4 . ACR was highest in the patient cohort followed up by nephrology, and significantly higher in the patient cohort followed up by diabetes than in those seen by primary care alone. The same pattern was also seen within the sub-group of patients with progressive renal disease; patients with progressive renal disease who were followed up by nephrology had the highest ACR, and those seen by diabetologists had a higher ACR than those in seen by primary carers alone. Table 4. Analysis of albumin:creatinine ratio in patients with diabetes and CKD. Open in a new tab Discussion Changes in working practice have moved the emphasis of care for patients with diabetes and CKD away from specialist services towards primary care. 7 For most patients with the combination of CKD and diabetes, it is therefore appropriate for management to be coordinated within primary care, where the focus is on cardiovascular risk management. Nevertheless, patients with more advanced diabetes and associated complications, might benefit from joint care with specialist services. Patients with either advanced or progressive renal disease should ideally be under the care of nephrology specialist services. A smooth transition of patient care through the appropriate specialist services, requires an awareness of the significance of the markers of disease progression, which should trigger referral. In response to the perceived shift in focus for the care of diabetic patients, in this study we determined the distribution of care of patients with diabetes and CKD between primary care, diabetes and nephrology services, and sought to identify patient- and disease-associated characteristics of those followed up in each setting. From this analysis it is clear, as would be expected, that the nephrology service sees only a minority of patients with the combination of diabetes and CKD. From the patient characteristics, it would seem that age, stage of CKD and quantitation of proteinuria are the driving forces for referral; the patients followed up by nephrologists are younger, have a lower eGFR and have the highest levels of proteinuria or albuminuria. However, nephrologists currently do see the majority of the patients with stage 4 or 5 CKD as well as diabetes. These data, therefore, suggest that the characteristics of the nephrology cohort reflect current referral guidelines that emphasise an absolute eGFR and CKD stage as the trigger for the referral of patients with CKD to a nephrologist. A striking feature of the majority of the patients followed by nephrologists is that they have stable stage 3 CKD, with the stable (or improving) group of CKD patients representing roughly two-thirds of all the patients seen. It is unlikely that this high proportion of non-progressors relates to a difference in management, as a similar proportion of non-progressors was seen in the primary care and diabetic cohorts. This is, however, likely to reflect the relatively elderly population with the combination of diabetes and CKD in our study; it is known that in the elderly CKD may be a more important marker of cardiovascular risk than of risk of progression to end-stage renal disease. 8,9 Is this an appropriate use of resources? Might these patients be better served by having their cardiovascular risk managed by their primary care physician? The idea of an active programme of discharge of patients with significant renal impairment might be viewed as rash and might not be routine practice within nephrology. We have previously examined the impact of a programme of active and aggressive discharge of patients with non-progressive CKD, supported by advice on monitoring and re-referral parameters. 7 − 10 In this context, we have shown that discharge of the appropriate CKD patients from secondary care was followed by adequate monitoring of renal function in primary care, and that this was safe for patients. We suggest, therefore, that repatriation of stable patients with diabetes and CKD should be considered in order to increase capacity within existing clinical resources. Recent studies have highlighted that early decline in renal function in patients with diabetes is a predictor of the risk of end-stage renal disease, with the rate of progression for any individual being linear. 11 In the current study, it would appear that deterioration of renal function does not influence patterns of referral. From our data, the same proportion of patients classified as progressors was seen in each of the three follow-up groups (primary care, diabetes and nephrology). Therefore, although nephrology services see the majority of those with advanced renal disease (stages 4 and 5 CKD), only a third of progressors are followed up in the nephrology clinic. Roughly half of the patient group classified as progressors in the primary care and diabetes cohorts were classified as moderate or severe progressors, having a rate of deterioration in their eGFR of ≥5 ml/min/year. A striking feature within the progressor cohort is that the age of the patients seen only by their primary care physicians is significantly older than those seen by a nephrologist. In addition, this disparity in age is exaggerated in those in which the rate of decline in renal function is greatest. This would suggest that age seemingly influences the decisions of primary care physicians. This may well be appropriate in that some elderly patients might not be suitable for long-term renal replacement therapy. Nevertheless, when patients, following suitable immersion and education, make a decision to have conservative management of end-stage renal disease (a decision that of itself needs interaction and discussion), active management of anaemia, of renal bone disease and of end-of-life care are an important part of the role of the nephrologist and the wider nephrology multidisciplinary team. Age therefore should not in itself be seen as a barrier to referral. As in the patient cohort followed in primary care, there is a significant proportion of patients in the diabetes cohort who have progressive renal disease. A greater share of moderate progressors and an equivalent proportion of severe progressors are seen by diabetologists when compared to those seen by nephrologists. This reflects the larger absolute number of patients in these two categories seen by diabetologists than are seen by nephrologists. Age does not seem to be a factor influencing decision making for those seeing a diabetologist as there their age does not differ from that of the progressor subgroup seen in nephrology. The key difference between the diabetes and nephrology patients (which also applies to the primary care cohort) relates to eGFR, which is higher in the diabetes cohort than in the nephrology cohort. This would suggest that for patients with diabetes, absolute eGFR or CKD stage seems to dictate referral patterns rather than rate of progression. Current guidelines, 6 although emphasising referral based on eGFR and CKD stage, also highlight deteriorating renal function as an important criteria that should prompt referral, but our data suggest that this does not influence decision making. Adoption of an approach in which rate of decline of renal function forms an important determinant of the decision to refer would, however, result in a large increase in new patient referrals to nephrology. In this study, in which half of the patients being followed up by either primary care or diabetology were classified as moderate or severe progressors, this would amount to a four-fold increase in the number of patients with moderate progression (≥5 but <10 ml/min/year) who would need to be accommodated within nephrology clinics, and roughly a doubling of patients with severe progression (≥10 ml/min/year). Conclusion We have demonstrated that using current guidelines based on referral according to CKD stage, the majority of patients with DM-CKD in nephrology clinics do not have progressive renal disease. Furthermore, there is a large cohort of patients who are not currently referred to nephrology but who have significant progressive renal impairment. At least in primary care, patient age seems to influence referral patterns. References 1. MacGregor MS, Boag DE, Innes A. Chronic kidney disease: evolving strategies for detection and management of impaired renal function. QJM 2006;99:365–75. 10.1093/qjmed/hcl052 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Roland M. Linking physicians’ pay to the quality of care – a major experiment in the United Kingdom. N Engl J Med 2004;351:1448–54. 10.1056/NEJMhpr041294 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ritz E, Zeng XX, Rychlik I. Clinical manifestation and natural history of diabetic nephropathy. Contrib Nephrol 2011;170:19–27. 10.1159/000324939 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Nichols GA, Vupputuri S, Lau H. Medical care costs associated with progression of diabetic nephropathy. Diabetes Care 2011;34:2374–8. 10.2337/dc11-0475 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Joint Speciality Committee of the Royal College of Physicians and Renal Association The UK CKD guidelines, 2007. www.renal.org/CKDguide/ckd.html [Accessed 7 August 2013]. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Burden R, Tomson C. Identification, management and referral of adults with chronic kidney disease: concise guidelines. Clin Med 2005;5:635–42. 10.7861/clinmedicine.5-6-635 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Phillips LA, Donovan KL, Phillips AO. Renal quality outcomes framework and eGFR: impact on secondary care. QJM 2009;102:415–23. 10.1093/qjmed/hcp030 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Keith DS, Nichols GA, Gullion CM, et al. Longitudinal follow-up and outcomes among a population with chronic kidney disease in a large managed care organization. Arch Int Med 2004;164:659–63. 10.1001/archinte.164.6.659 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Foley RN, Murray AM, Li S, et al. Chronic kidney disease and the risk for cardiovascular disease, renal replacement, and death in the United States Medicare population, 1998 to 1999. J Am Soc Nephrol 2005;16:489–95. 10.1681/ASN.2004030203 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Meran S, Don K, Shah N, et al. Impact of chronic kidney disease management in primary care. QJM 2011;104:27–34. 10.1093/qjmed/hcq151 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Skupien J, Warram JH, Smiles AM, et al. The early decline in renal function in patients with type 1 diabetes and proteinuria predicts the risk of end-stage renal disease. Kidney Int 2012;82:589–97. 10.1038/ki.2012.189 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',8,'2025-10-15 05:54:30','2025-10-15 05:54:30');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3234,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11321740','A National Survey of Pregnancy and Parenthood among Nephrology Trainees: A Focus on Nephrology Fellowship.','Dixon A, Bansal N, Nicholas SB, Ostrow A, Kendrick J.','2024','subsidized 30s strenuous strenuous fmla allowance supersedes exhausting fmla strenuous conveniently refrigerator subsidized fishe breastfed breastfeed breastfed conveniently nanny subsidized discounted juggling exhausting feat misinterpret fmla allowance fmla untrained prolongs clause deems apprehensive moonlight moonlighting overstretching conveniently refrigerator subsidized subsidized subsidized subsidized b907 dk007135 angelina susanne ostrow angelina angelina ostrow angelina susanne nisha angelina nisha angelina susanne ostrow nisha angelina susanne ostrow angelina nisha angelina susanne ostrow b906 cfd natality fastats osterman stentz burgner dunson salihu shumpert slay s0029 simchen yinon sivan paulson s0015 vandermeer khurana nandi hajizadeh koski strumpf heymann e1001985 winegarden bracy ruhm s0167 f28 burtle bezruchka healthcare4020030 aboi abom acfgm rigler scholarlycommons caselrev vol45 iss2 hochbaum kegels infosihat infosihat artikelhp bahanrujukan he_dan_teori 20model wellons hartig 0b013e3181d2cb5b dalgaard 1_7 upton wics koven haberer kwolek overdue nejmp2215288 heymann mcneill earle uoce famli fml famli fml malinzak schwenk genhosppsych aaronson backes anzia hofe meeks behbehani tulandi s1701 klevan dabrow mozurkewich avni s0029 stocker macklon cheong bewley varcoe vanoverberghe chernoby pettit e10504 magudia mcphillips pallant stapleton e596 e602 hipp kawwass s10995 mwakyanjala cowart maniaci e012137','Visual Abstract Open in a new tab Keywords: clinical nephrology, medical education Abstract Key Points Parental leave policies for physician trainees are inconsistent. Nephrology fellows are largely unaware of parental leave policies and pregnancy accommodations in their programs. Individual nephrology programs should improve awareness about national and local program policies among trainees. Background National and international policies on parental leave for physician trainees are inconsistent. Physician trainees, including nephrology fellows, may be at higher risk of pregnancy complications. Physician trainees face barriers in meeting their breastfeeding goals and in finding childcare because of nontraditional work hours with extended or unpredictable shifts. We examine awareness of current policies in US nephrology fellowship programs regarding parental leave, pregnancy/breastfeeding accommodations, and fellows'' perspectives on family planning. Methods An anonymous, online survey of US nephrology fellows was undertaken from June 9 to August 24, 2023. Results One hundred twenty nephrology fellows submitted the survey. Most of the fellow respondents were unaware of parental leave policies of their training programs (63%), the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (75%), and/or the American Board of Medical Specialties (75%). Forty-two percent were unaware of the duration of parental leave at their program. Nearly 45% of all respondents were unsure if their program limited night shifts or shifts >24 hours for pregnant trainees. Forty-three percent reported they were unsure of lactation accommodations, and 40% were unsure of access to subsidized childcare. When fellows received work accommodations for pregnancy or parenthood, their work obligations were largely covered by co-fellows (60%) or attendings (38%). Over 60% of fellows agreed or strongly agreed that they would avoid a pregnancy in fellowship because of concern that they would have to extend their training. Of the 40 fellows who chose to pursue pregnancy or parenthood during medical training, 75% did not change their career plans as a result. Conclusions Most nephrology fellows were unaware of parental leave policies and pregnancy/lactation accommodations. While the topic itself has a broad effect to all physician trainees, there is a need for improved awareness about national and local program policies among trainees across individual nephrology programs. Introduction In the United States, the average age of a first-time mother is 27 years while the average age of a first-time physician mother is 30 years. 1 – 3 In the United States, 39% of adult nephrology fellows and 71% of pediatric nephrology fellows are women, with a mean age of 33 years. 4 Women who choose to pursue pregnancy in their 30s or later may struggle with infertility or pregnancy complications, and this may be exacerbated by strenuous working conditions. 5 – 10 Physician trainees are able to work 24 consecutive hours (with extension up to 28 hours) up to twice per week and 80 total hours per week, when averaged over 4 weeks. 11 There are no Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) policies regarding limitation of strenuous work during pregnancy and thus is up to the individual training program. Adequate parental leave has been demonstrated to optimize the well-being of newborns. 12 – 16 However, the duration of leave that physician trainees may take is limited to graduate on time. 17 – 19 While the US Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) requires the allowance of 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected parental leave, the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) allows only 5 weeks of academic leave per year. 17 , 20 In 2021, the American Board of Medical Specialties (ABMS), which supersedes the ABIM, passed a policy allowing a minimum of 6 weeks of leave on one occasion during training for purposes of parental, caregiver, or medical leave, without exhausting vacation or sick leave and without requiring an extension in training. 18 ACGME enacted a similar policy in 2022 mandating a minimum of six paid weeks off. 19 Still, there is a large gap between the 5–6 weeks allowed by training governing bodies and 12 weeks outlined by FMLA. We aimed to examine the current policies in the US nephrology fellowship programs regarding parental leave, pregnancy/breastfeeding accommodations, and access to childcare facilities. In addition, we examined nephrology fellows'' perspectives on family planning. Methods Study Population The US nephrology fellows (including adult, pediatric, and medicine–pediatric fellows) with and without children were invited to complete an online anonymous survey administered between June 9 and August 24, 2023. Participants were recruited through emails sent to training program directors (PDs) found through the American Society of Nephrology Training Program Directory. This directory includes 163 adult nephrology PDs and 50 pediatric nephrology PDs. PDs were asked to forward the recruitment email that included the survey link along to nephrology fellows. A reminder email was sent out 4 weeks after the initial survey distribution. The survey was completely anonymously, and data were collected and managed through Research Electronic Data Capture. 21 , 22 The study protocol was deemed exempt by the Colorado Multiple Institutional Review Board, and consent was implied through voluntary return of the survey. Participants received a $10 gift card for participating. The standardized reporting framework Checklist for Reporting Results of Internet E-Surveys was used. 23 Survey Details All recruited fellows were invited to complete an 88-item online survey ( Supplemental Information ). The survey consisted of questions regarding parental leave, pregnancy/breastfeeding accommodations, access to childcare facilities, and fellows'' perspectives on family planning. Female nephrology fellows who had a pregnancy during residency or fellowship were asked to self-report any pregnancy complications. Both residency and fellowship were included as strenuous work occurs during both of these training periods. The survey was modeled on the Health Belief Model 24 and a previous survey that examined how knowledge of fertility and perceived threat to career affects the decision to have children during residency. 25 Adaptive questioning was used. The modified survey underwent validation on a convenience sample of at least five physician trainees. Additional information on trainee demographics and geographic location of the training program were collected. Fellows also had the opportunity to answer open-ended questions to share their thoughts on the topic. Reasons for not participating were not ascertained. Analyses of Survey Results Descriptive statistics were provided summaries of the responses. For continuous variables, the Kruskal–Wallis rank-sum test 26 detected significant differences between groups (sex or subspecialty groups). For categorical variables, the Fisher exact test 27 determined whether sex or subspecialty group was independently associated with a given study variable. For comparisons between male and female participants, only 98 participants who completed the survey in its entirety, including demographic questions, were included. All analyses were performed using SAS statistical software. 28 Statistical significance was set at P < 0.05. Thematic analysis was used for the open-ended questions. The study team independently coded the answers to identify major themes and categories. Results One hundred twenty-one nephrology fellows submitted the survey, 98 fellows completed the survey in its entirety. As the survey was distributed to PDs with a request to forward to nephrology fellows, we do not have a calculated response rate. Table 1 presents the demographics of the respondents who completed all questions ( N =98), as demographic questions were at the end. Sixty-two percent of respondents were female, 38% were male, 68% were married, and the majority were participating in adult nephrology fellowship programs (72%). Table 1. Characteristics of survey participants Characteristic Overall a , N =98 Adult Nephrology, N =71 Pediatric Nephrology, N =24 Med-Peds Nephrology, N =3 Age in years, mean (SD) 33 (4) 34 (4) 32 (2) 33 (1) Sex, N (%) Female 61 (62) 41 (58) 18 (75) 2 (67) Male 37 (38) 30 (42) 6 (25) 1 (33) Race, N (%) American Indian or Native American 0 0 0 0 Asian 37 (38) 33 (46) 4 (17) 0 Black 1 (1) 1 (1) 0 0 Native Hawaiian or other Pacific Islander 0 0 0 0 White 46 (47) 26 (37) 18 (75) 2 (67) Other 12 (12) 10 (14) 2 (8) 0 Prefer not to disclose 2 (2) 1 (1) 0 1 (33) Ethnicity, N (%) Hispanic/Latinx 11 (11) 8 (11) 3 (13) 0 Not Hispanic/Latinx 87 (89) 63 (89) 21 (88) 3 (100) Fellowship location, N (%) Northeast 27 (28) 21 (30) 6 (25) 0 Midwest 31 (32) 21 (30) 7 (29) 3 (100) South 22 (22) 19 (27) 3 (13) 0 West 18 (18) 10 (14) 8 (33) 0 Fellows/year (mean) 4.0 (2.7) 4.8 (2.7) 2.3 (1.9) 1.7 (0.6) PGY, N (%) PGY-4 41 (42) 37 (52) 3 (13) 1 (33) PGY-5 41 (42) 28 (39) 13 (54) 0 PGY-6 7 (7) 2 (3) 4 (17) 1 (33) PGY-7 3 (3) 0 3 (13) 0 PGY-8 2 (2) 1 (1) 0 1 (33) PGY-9 2 (2) 1 (1) 1 (4) 0 PGY-10 2 (2) 2 (3) 0 0 Relationship status, N (%) Single 15 (15) 12 (17) 2 (8) 1 (33) Married 67 (68) 47 (66) 19 (79) 1 (33) Long-term relationship 16 (16) 12 (17) 3 (13) 1 (33) Divorced/separated 0 0 0 0 Sexual orientation, N (%) Heterosexual 88 (90) 62 (87) 23 (96) 3 (100) LGBTQ 6 (6) 5 (7) 1 (4) 0 Prefer not to disclose 4 (4) 4 (6) 0 0 Open in a new tab LGBTQ, lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender; PGY, Post-Graduate Year. a Only respondents who completed the entire survey, including demographic questions, are included in the overall number. Over one third of respondents had children (37%, N =36), and an additional 9% ( N =9) were currently pregnant (or their partner was pregnant) (Table 2 ). Significantly fewer female nephrology fellows had children during residency compared with male fellows (45% versus 75%, respectively, P = 0.04) (Table 2 ). Table 2. Family planning responses by nephrology trainees Characteristic Overall, N =98 Female, N =61 Male, N =37 P Value Have children, N (%) 0.30 Yes 36 (37) 20 (33) 16 (43) No 62 (63) 41 (67) 21 (57) Plan to have children, N (%) 0.22 Yes 45 (73) 30 (73) 15 (71) No 13 (21) 7 (17) 6 (29) Currently pregnant 4 (7) 4 (10) 0 Plan to have children during fellowship, N (%) 0.99 Yes 13 (29) 8 (27) 5 (33) No 32 (71) 22 (73) 10 (67) How many children, N (%) 0.69 One 17 (47) 9 (45) 8 (50) Two 16 (44) 10 (50) 6 (38) Three or more 3 (8) 1 (5) 2 (13) When children born, N (%) Before training 4 (4) 1 (5) 3 (19) 0.15 Medical school 4 (4) 1 (5) 3 (19) 0.15 Residency 21 (21) 9 (45) 12 (75) 0.04 Fellowship 14 (14) 11 (55) 3 (19) 0.17 Between residency and fellowship 5 (5) 4 (20) 1 (6) 0.65 Plan to have more children, N (%) 0.31 Yes 14 (39) 4 (25) 10 (50) No 17 (47) 10 (63) 7 (35) Currently pregnant 5 (14) 2 (13) 3 (15) Plan to have more children during fellowship, N (%) 0.99 Yes 3 (21) 1 (25) 2 (20) No 11 (79) 3 (75) 8 (80) Open in a new tab Of the female nephrology fellow respondents who had children during residency or fellowship ( N =21), 38% ( N =8) experienced self-reported pregnancy complications (Figure 1 ). The most common complication was miscarriage (24%, N =5). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Self-reported pregnancy complications by female nephrology trainees who had a pregnancy during residency or fellowship ( N =21) . Parental Leave Policies Regarding parental leave policies, fellows were largely unaware of the national regulations and their programs'' policies. Seventy-five percent of fellows were unaware of the ABMS and ACGME statements on parental leave (missing responses=21). Sixty-three percent of fellows were unaware of their programs'' policy for parental leave, and 42% were unsure about the duration of parental leave at their program. Table 3 presents comparisons between male and female fellows regarding parental leave. Compared with male fellows, more female fellows knew the duration of parental leave at their program, but these differences did not reach statistical significance. Seventeen percent of fellows reported receiving <6 weeks of full-paid parental leave. Numerically more female compared with male fellows reported that their training would be prolonged if more than 6 weeks of parental leave was taken (39% versus 24%, respectively, P = 0.24), but these differences did not reach statistical significance (Table 3 ). Table 3. Parental leave policies and leave duration responses by male and female nephrology trainees Survey Question Overall a , N =98 Female, N =61 Male, N =37 P Value b Awareness of parental leave policies Aware of ABMS policy on parental leave, N (%) 0.61 Yes 20 (20) 14 (23) 6 (16) No 78 (80) 47 (77) 31 (84) Aware of ACGME policy on parental leave, N (%) 0.34 Yes 25 (26) 18 (30) 7 (19) No 73 (75) 43 (71) 30 (81) Aware of your fellowship program''s parental leave policy, N (%) 0.14 Yes 36 (37) 26 (43) 10 (27) No 62 (63) 35 (57) 27 (73) Fellowship program parental leave duration No. of weeks your program offers for full-paid parental leave, N (%) 0.13 <6 17 (17) 12 (20) 5 (14) 6–8 23 (24) 18 (30) 5 (14) 8–10 7 (7) 5 (8) 2 (5) 10–12 9 (9) 6 (10) 3 (8) >12 0 0 (0) 0 (0) Unsure 42 (43) 20 (33) 22 (60) Receive additional full-paid vacation time outside of parental leave, N (%) 0.58 Yes 46 (47) 27 (44) 19 (51) No 15 (15) 10 (16) 5 (14) Unsure 37 (38) 24 (39) 13 (35) Receive additional paid sick time outside of parental leave, N (%) 0.86 Yes 34 (35) 21 (34) 13 (35) No 19 (19) 12 (20) 7 (19) Unsure 45 (45) 28 (46) 17 (46) Training is prolonged if >6 wk of parental leave taken, N (%) 0.24 Yes 33 (33) 24 (39) 9 (24) No 17 (17) 11 (18) 6 (16) Unsure 48 (49) 26 (43) 22 (60) Open in a new tab ABMS, American Board of Medical Specialties; ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education. a Only respondents who completed the entire survey, including demographic questions, are included in the overall number. b Fisher exact test used to compare responses between male and female fellows. Pregnancy Accommodations When surveyed about scheduling and work accommodations during pregnancy, 37% ( N =45) reported they were unsure whether their program limited call shifts (or shifts >24 hours) during pregnancy and 39% ( N =47) were unsure whether their program limited fixed night shifts during pregnancy (missing responses=20). Table 4 presents responses regarding accommodations during pregnancy/parenthood between male and female fellows. A similar number of male and female fellows were unsure whether their program limited call or night shifts. Over half of all respondents reported that programs enabled avoidance of patients with infections that posed a potential for fetal transmission or adverse outcomes in pregnancy (including coronavirus disease 2019, cytomegalovirus, varicella, zoster, parvovirus), and an additional 37% were unsure whether these accommodations were made. Fifty-nine respondents (60%) reported that programs provided time for doctor''s appointments during work hours, and 30% ( N =30) were unsure whether their program provided this resource (Table 4 ). Significantly more male fellows reported time to accompany their partner to appointments than female fellows (46% versus 21%, respectively, P = 0.01). Table 4. Pregnancy, lactation, and childcare accommodations response by male and female nephrology trainees Survey Question Overall a , N =98 Female, N =61 Male, N =37 P Value b Pregnancy accommodations Call shifts or shifts > 24 h limited, N (%) 0.99 Yes 25 (26) 15 (25) 10 (27) No 30 (31) 19 (31) 11 (30) Unsure 43 (44) 27 (44) 16 (43) Night shifts limited, N (%) 0.52 Yes 19 (19) 10 (16) 9 (24) No 35 (36) 24 (39) 11 (30) Unsure 44 (45) 27 (44) 17 (46) Avoidance of patients with infection that pose risk, N (%) 0.61 Yes 53 (54) 35 (57) 18 (49) No 9 (9) 6 (10) 3 (8) Unsure 36 (37) 20 (33) 16 (43) Program provides time for doctor appointments during work hours, N (%) 0.99 Yes 59 (60.2) 37 (61) 22 (60) No 10 (10.2) 6 (10) 4 (11) Unsure 29 (29.6) 18 (30) 11 (30) Lactation accommodations Program provides breaks to express breast milk, N (%) 0.04 Yes 46 (47) 33 (54) 13 (35) No 10 (10) 8 (13) 2 (5) Unsure 42 (43) 20 (33) 22 (60) Lactation room conveniently located, N (%) 0.13 Yes 23 (24) 14 (23) 9 (24) No 21 (21) 17 (28) 4 (11) Unsure 54 (55) 30 (49) 24 (65) Lactation room available when needed, N (%) 0.86 Yes 31 (32) 20 (33) 11 (30) No 10 (10) 7 (12) 3 (8) Unsure 57 (58) 34 (56) 23 (62) Equipped with an electric pump, N (%) 0.83 Yes 16 (16) 10 (16) 6 (16) No 15 (15) 12 (20) 3 (8) Unsure 67 (68) 39 (64) 28 (76) Equipped with a refrigerator, N (%) 0.46 Yes 21 (21) 14 (23) 7 (19) No 15 (15) 10 (16) 5 (14) Unsure 62 (63) 37 (61) 25 (68) Equipped with a workstation and phone, N (%) 0.11 Yes 19 (19) 12 (20) 7 (19) No 18 (18) 15 (25) 3 (8) Unsure 61 (62) 34 (56) 27 (73) Childcare accommodations Childcare subsidized, N (%) 0.35 Yes 18 (18) 10 (16) 8 (22) No 41 (42) 29 (48) 12 (32) Unsure 39 (40) 22 (36) 17 (46) Hospital system offers childcare, N (%) 0.55 Yes 32 (33) 20 (33) 12 (32) No 32 (33) 22 (36) 10 (27) Unsure 34 (35) 19 (31) 15 (41) Access to childcare with extended hours (before 7 am , after 6 pm ), N (%) 0.22 Yes 11 (11) 5 (8) 6 (16) No 36 (37) 26 (43) 10 (27) Unsure 51 (52) 30 (49) 21 (57) Access to childcare with weekend hours, N (%) 0.20 Yes 6 (6) 2 (3) 4 (11) No 40 (41) 28 (46) 12 (32) Unsure 52 (53) 31 (51) 21 (58) Access to childcare with emergency/on-call hours, N (%) 0.23 Yes 6 (6) 3 (5) 3 (8) No 37 (38) 27 (44) 10 (27) Unsure 55 (56) 31 (51) 23 (64.9) Access to sick childcare, N (%) 0.002 Yes 18 (18) 5 (8) 13 (35) No 31 (32) 24 (39) 7 (19) Unsure 49 (50) 32 (53) 17 (46) Open in a new tab a Only respondents who completed the entire survey, including demographic questions, are included in the overall number. b Fishe exact test used to compare responses between male and female fellows. When fellows received work accommodations for pregnancy or parenthood, 60% reported their work obligations were largely covered by co-fellows, 38% by attendings, and 12% by advanced practice providers (APPs) (missing responses=20). Of the 72 respondents who reported work obligations were covered by co-fellows, 28% reported that co-fellows sometimes volunteered to cover the extra work, but 44% reported co-fellows were more often scheduled for the additional work. Seventeen percent reported that co-fellows were paid to cover these work obligations. Breastfeeding Accommodations Of the female fellows with children ( N =20), 55% reported they breastfed their children and an additional 40% were breastfeeding at the time of the survey. Of those breastfeeding at the time of the survey ( N =8), 63% planned to breastfeed for more than 6 months. Of those who had completed breastfeeding ( N =11), 27% breastfed for more than 6 months and just over 50% met their breastfeeding goals. Table 4 presents responses regarding breastfeeding accommodations between male and female fellows. Of all the fellows ( N =98), 47% reported their program provided breaks to express breast milk, but 43% were unsure this was provided. More male fellows were unsure their program provided breaks to express breast milk than female fellows. Most fellows were unsure whether lactation rooms were available when needed, were conveniently located, or were equipped. Childcare Accommodations Of the nephrology fellows with children ( N =36), 36% had a stay-at-home spouse, 22% had an extended family member who cared for their children, 44% used childcare, and 11% had a nanny or au pair. Fifteen percent ( N =18) of fellows reported they had access to subsidized or discounted childcare through their institution and 32% ( N =39) were unsure (missing responses=26). Comparison of responses to childcare by sex is presented in Table 4 . More male fellows than female fellows reported access to sick childcare (35% versus 8%, respectively, P = 0.002). Of the 13 male fellows who responded they had access to sick childcare, eight (62%) currently had children. Of those eight, 63% reported they had a stay-at-home spouse and 25% had an extended family member who cared for their child. Eleven percent of fellows reported access to childcare with extended hours, and 6% reported access to childcare with weekend or emergency/on-call hours. Most of the fellows were unsure of available childcare resources (Table 4 ). Family Planning in Fellowship Of the fellows without children ( N =62), 73% planned to have children. However, the majority did not want to have children during fellowship (Table 2 ). The surveyed fellows reported the average age at which they desired (or had) their first child was 32 (SD, 10) years, and the age at which they desired (or had) their last child was 36 (SD, 10) years. In an open-ended question regarding family planning and having children during fellowship, two major themes emerged: lack of time and increased stress/reduced quality of life (Table 5 ). One female participant stated their reason for not having children during fellowship as “not enough time off and not enough support while working long hours.” Another was concerned, “I won''t be able to take care of myself due to the demands of fellowship.” Other responses are presented in Table 5 . Table 5. Themes identified as reasons to defer a pregnancy during fellowship Theme Quotations Lack of time “Challenging in research track—need to publish and get work done quickly to get a position where I will have protected research time” (female fellow) “High stress, not enough time” (female fellow) “Not enough time off and not enough support while working long hours” (female fellow) “No time to make a baby” (female fellow) “I feel that fellowship would be extremely difficult with children. I don''t know if I could balance everything well, and feel like I would be both an absent/bad parent and an absent/bad fellow as I could not devote the proper time to either” (male fellow) “Too less of maternity leave duration” (female fellow) Increased stress/reduced quality of life “Lifestyle change and responsibility of taking care of another life is very difficult during medical training and increases stress” (male fellow) “It may affect my studying” (female fellow) “The difficulty of juggling a newborn with training” (male fellow) “I won''t be able to take care of myself due to the demands of fellowship” (female fellow) “Fellowship is exhausting, and it seems like a large feat to have a baby” (female fellow) Open in a new tab Knowledge of Fertility Fifty-four percent of fellows ( N =65) reported they were aware of the age when fertility declines in women but were unaware of the age when fertility declines in men (missing responses=15). When asked at what age there is a slight decline in a woman’s ability to become pregnant, fellows’ responses averaged 33 (SD, 4) years, and when asked at what age there is a marked decline in a woman’s ability to become pregnant, fellows’ responses averaged 39 (SD, 4) years (missing responses=59). There were no significant differences between male and female fellow responses. Fertility Resources Table 6 presents male and female fellow responses regarding fertility resources and fertility preservation. Most of the fellows reported that their program or Graduate Medical Education (GME) had not discussed fertility or fertility preservation and were unsure whether the program or GME covered fertility treatments. There was no difference in the number of male and female fellows who experienced infertility or had considered fertility preservation. Table 6. Fertility resources and fertility preservation responses by nephrology trainees Survey Question Overall a , N =98 Female, N =61 Male, N =37 P Value b Fellowship program or GME has discussed fertility/fertility preservation, N (%) 0.06 Yes 9 (9) 8 (13) 1 (3) No 78 (80) 49 (80) 29 (78) Unsure 11 (11) 4 (7) 7 (19) Fellowship program or GME covers fertility appointments and treatments, N (%) 0.40 Yes 18 (18) 13 (21) 5 (14) No 18 (18) 13 (21) 5 (14) Unsure 62 (63) 35 (57) 27 (73) Personally experienced infertility, N (%) 0.60 Yes 4 (4) 2 (3) 2 (5) No 94 (96) 59 (97) 35 (95) Considered fertility preservation, N (%) 0.13 Yes 25 (26) 18 (30) 7 (19) No 73 (75) 43 (71) 30 (81) Completed fertility preservation, N (%) 0.90 Yes 3 (3) 2 (3) 1 (3) No 22 (22) 16 (26) 6 (16) Missing responses ( N ) 73 (75) 43 (70) 30 (81) Open in a new tab GME, Graduate Medical Education. a Only respondents who completed the entire survey, including demographic questions, are included in the overall number. b Fisher exact test used to compare responses between male and female fellows. Perceived Threat to Pregnancy or Career Figure 2 shows reasons fellows would defer pregnancy during fellowship by sex. Sixty-one fellows (62%) agreed/strongly agreed that they would avoid a pregnancy in fellowship because of concern they would have to extend their training because of parental leave. More female fellows than male fellows reported they would defer a pregnancy during fellowship because they perceived negative emotions from their program toward those who take parental leave (31% versus 11%, respectively, P = 0.02). More female fellows than male fellows reported they perceived negative emotions from co-fellows toward those who take parental leave, but it did not reach statistical significance (25% versus 14%, respectively, P = 0.38). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Reasons to defer a pregnancy during nephrology fellowship . Career Outcomes Of the fellows who have children or were pregnant ( N =40), 75% reported pregnancy/parenthood changed their career plans and 90% reported it did not affect their pursuit of an additional degree. Sixty-seven percent of fellows who were performing research ( N =30) reported pregnancy or parenthood did not affect their research productivity. Discussion To our knowledge, this is the first in-depth study examining pregnancy and parental leave policies in the US nephrology fellowship training programs. Most of the fellows were unsure of their training programs'' parental leave policies and ACGME and/or ABIM parental leave policies. While we highlight the experiences of US nephrology fellows, we recognize that these issues are likely inherent to all US medical trainees and potentially international medical trainees. Other studies have noted that training programs may not be fully aware of or may misinterpret parental leave policies established by certifying boards, and our data further highlights a lack of awareness, but at the individual fellow, not program level. 29 The theme of lack of awareness further extends to pregnancy and lactation accommodations because over one third of respondents were unaware of accommodations their program provided. While this lack of knowledge is not specific to nephrology fellows, individual nephrology programs have the ability to improve fellows'' knowledge on program policies. Programs should ensure that their program handbook/website/orientation includes information on their individual as well as national parental leave policies and pregnancy/lactation accommodations. In the United States, FMLA requires the allowance of 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected parental leave. 20 The United States is the only high-income country and one of eight countries in the world that does not mandate paid parental leave. 30 In fact, of 186 countries examined in a 2013 report (including Canada, Mexico, Europe, Asia, South America, and Africa), 96% provide some form of pay during parental leave. 30 Some states, such as Massachusetts or Colorado, have limited paid parental leave policies. 29 , 31 Still, the US physician trainees are in a unique position because they are both paid workers and trainees who need to graduate to work in their field of choice. For US medical subspecialty fellows, ABIM allows 5 weeks of parental leave while the ABMS and ACGME allow 6 weeks of such leave. 11 , 17 , 18 With these variations, there is no easy way to create a fellowship program parental leave policy. Most of the fellows reported program policies with 6–8 weeks of parental leave. Trainees may qualify for longer, unpaid leave with FMLA; however, physician trainees can be categorized as untrained personnel who do not qualify for this benefit. 29 In addition, more than 5–6 weeks of leave may require an extension of training. In our study, one third of fellows reported their training is prolonged for taking more than 6 weeks of parental leave, and 60% reported that they would avoid a pregnancy during fellowship because they did not want to extend training. An extension in training prolongs the time until trainees make a higher salary, delays board certification, and results in difficulty matching into secondary fellowships. 32 The ABIM has a “deficiencies in required training time” clause which specifies that, as long as a PD deems a trainees to be competent, they do not have to extend training. 29 Ultimately, it is up to the individual training program to advocate for fellows. However, physician trainees may be apprehensive to request additional leave because of fear of future career consequences or stigma as PDs are responsible for their letters of recommendation for future positions. 33 We found that one third of fellows reported their program did not provide time for doctors'' appointments during working hours. As with parental leave, this may be because of lack of awareness of ACGME policies and informing fellows of these policies may result in improvements. However, requesting time for doctors'' visits often requires disclosure of health concerns and private health information to their PD. Studies have found that despite physician trainees feeling they require health services, few actually seek care. 34 While the studies performed have mainly assessed mental health, trainees may also be hesitant to disclose pregnancy or family planning issues to their program. Some training programs have adopted wellness days that are built into the trainees schedule. 35 , 36 This allows trainees to schedule visits without having to ask for time away from work. Individual nephrology programs should consider adding built-in time off into their fellows schedules. In our study, we found over one third of female nephrology fellows who had a child during residency or fellowship experienced adverse pregnancy outcomes. This is not exclusive to US nephrology fellows because this rate is similar to previous studies showing pregnancy complication rates between 33% and 36% in all physician trainees. 37 , 38 It has been shown that pregnant women who work rotating shifts, fixed night shifts, or more than 40 hours per week have an increased risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes. 10 , 39 – 41 Some training programs have adopted policies that preclude pregnant trainees from working overnight shifts or call shifts during pregnancy. 42 , 43 Smaller nephrology fellowships may have more difficulties finding coverage for their pregnant trainees. In our program, we found allowing co-fellows the opportunity to moonlight and volunteer to cover shifts results in improved fellow satisfaction and less fellow burden. Providing covering co-fellows extra days off if moonlighting pay is not possible is another option. Studies suggest APPs or attendings cover such shifts to prevent overstretching physician trainees. 29 In some institutions, attending physicians and APPs receive extra pay for doing additional work. A large number of nephrology fellows were unsure of lactation accommodations at their program. Previous studies have found lack of necessary lactation accommodations for physician trainees. 44 There is opportunity for nephrology programs to advocate for institutional improvement of lactation accommodations that will benefit all physician trainees. Individual programs can make improvements in lactation accommodations including increasing awareness of and access to lactation rooms that are conveniently located; available when needed; and equipped with a pump, refrigerator, workstation, and phone. In 2022, an American Medical Association (AMA) policy was passed to encourage the provision of on-site or subsidized childcare for medical students, residents, and fellows. 45 At the 2022 AMA Meeting, it was acknowledged that medical trainees have nontraditional work hours and extended or unpredictable shifts, so on-site childcare, with extended or flexible hours, could provide useful childcare accommodations. In a survey of pediatric department chairs, 59% reported on-site childcare was available at their institution, but demand for childcare always or almost always exceeded availability. 46 In our study, only a small proportion of nephrology fellows reported knowledge of access to subsidized childcare. The AMA highlights the importance of subsidized childcare because pay is low for physician trainees and trainees have limited negotiating ability given the residency and fellowship match process. 45 Training programs do have the opportunity to increase trainee awareness and advocate for priority enrollment and subsidized childcare for their trainees. Involving their local GME office and other PDs may contribute to trainee policy changes. Most of the nephrology fellows preferred to wait until after fellowship to have children (or more children). One reason they may be comfortable waiting is that they perceive a longer fertile period than actually exists. This is similar to prior studies where nearly half of obstetrics and gynecology residents overestimate the age at which fertility markedly declines, despite this being a fundamental part of their residency training. 47 A larger proportion of female compared with male nephrology fellows reported negative emotions from their training program as reasons to defer pregnancy during fellowship. This belief among women in the potential for pregnancy to threaten careers was similar to studies in medical residents. 25 It is important to highlight that nephrology fellows who choose to pursue pregnancy or parenthood during fellowship are quite professionally successful. Most of the nephrology fellows reported that pregnancy or parenthood did not change their career plans or affect their pursuit of an additional degree and did not affect their research productivity. This is encouraging as studies of cardiology fellows found that physician parents had difficulty completing research during fellowship because of pregnancy or parenthood. 48 Our study has several limitations. First, we were unable to determine a response rate because we were unable directly survey nephrology fellows. Second, there may be respondent bias in those who have responded versus those who did not respond. We are unable to determine any causal relationships in this observational study. In addition, we do not have information beyond the survey. Awareness surrounding parental leave, pregnancy, and lactation policies may be similar to awareness of other institutional policies. We did not collect information on reasons for not participating. Finally, the study responses represent fellows'' knowledge of program characteristics instead of the actual characteristics of programs because PDs or institution GME officials were not included. Our study highlights lack of awareness of parental leave policies, pregnancy, lactation, and childcare accommodations among US nephrology fellows. While the topic itself has a broad effect as it pertains to all physician trainees, individual nephrology programs are able to implement changes to improve awareness about both national and local program policies among trainees. Subsequent studies will be necessary to understand the effect of such changes. Supplementary Material cjasn-19-0984-s001.pdf (1.4MB, pdf) Open in a new tab cjasn-19-0984-s002.pdf (187.5KB, pdf) Open in a new tab Acknowledgments This work is a product of the Women in Nephrology, Li-Li Hsiao Nephrology Leadership Fellowship. Because J. Kendrick is an editor of the CJASN , she was not involved in the peer-review process for this manuscript. Another editor oversaw the peer-review and decision-making process for this manuscript. Footnotes See related editorial, “Supporting Fellows during Pregnancy: Improvement Is Needed,” on pages 944–945. Disclosures Disclosure forms, as provided by each author, are available with the online version of the article at http://links.lww.com/CJN/B907 . Funding A. Dixon: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (T32 DK007135). Author Contributions Conceptualization: Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick, Susanne B. Nicholas, Anna Ostrow. Data curation: Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick. Formal analysis: Angelina Dixon, Anna Ostrow. Funding acquisition: Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick, Susanne B. Nicholas. Investigation: Nisha Bansal, Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick. Methodology: Nisha Bansal, Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick, Susanne B. Nicholas, Anna Ostrow. Project administration: Nisha Bansal, Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick. Resources: Jessica Kendrick, Susanne B. Nicholas. Software: Anna Ostrow. Supervision: Jessica Kendrick. Writing – original draft: Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick. Writing – review & editing: Nisha Bansal, Angelina Dixon, Jessica Kendrick, Susanne B. Nicholas, Anna Ostrow. Data Sharing Statement All data is included in the manuscript and/or supporting information. Supplemental Material This article contains the following supplemental material online at http://links.lww.com/CJN/B906 . Survey Questions References 1. Control CfD. Fast Stats—Birth and Natality; 2024. Accessed May 23, 2024. https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/births.htm [ Google Scholar ] 2. Martin JA, Hamilton BE, Osterman MJ, Driscoll AK, Drake P. Births: final data for 2016. Natl Vital Stat Rep. 2018;67(1):1–55. PMID: 29775434 [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Stentz NC, Griffith KA, Perkins E, Jones RD, Jagsi R. Fertility and childbearing among American female physicians. J Womens Health. 2016;25(10):1059–1065. doi: 10.1089/jwh.2015.5638 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Pivert KA Burgner AM Chan L, et al. On Deck: Results from the 2022 ASN