INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3238,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11772766','Evaluating AI performance in nephrology triage and subspecialty referrals.','Koirala P, Thongprayoon C, Miao J, Garcia Valencia OA, Sheikh MS, Suppadungsuk S, Mao MA, Pham JH, Craici IM, Cheungpasitporn W.','2025','terabytes onconephrology onconephrology onconephrology top_p top_k configured top_p top_k onconephrology onconephrology onconephrology onconephrology routing uncommonly misdirected mistriage encryption baa baa unauthorized encryption enforceable baas interdependencies retrieves shap shapley adapts gapped encryption cyber deployable reallocate probabilistically hallucinate negating arbiters augmentative contends 310kb jadoul aoun masimango imani e342 e343 kovesdy kisu hafez fdgth temel athaluri frai rosoł gasior łaba korzeniewski młynczak uddin yew bozza wilder russe herbold hautli janisz heuer kikteva trautsch nastasi courtright brin futterer breithaupt retell toek masanneck untrained e53297 bhattaram shinde khumujam bellocchio fneph manzoor mazhar rashwan ouanes farhah s10916 franc hertelendy s43678 jeyaraman wjm v13 baa baa thongprayoon suppadungsuk cheungpasitporn kaunas medicina60030445 kapsali tombstone e54759 e48009 khullar e2210309 beets e40337 bajwa munir nori e188 e194 fhj blezek missert korfiatis healthcare10122493 alowais hendrix veenstra verguet jval pauling keck baumbach e16866 310kb','Abstract Artificial intelligence (AI) has shown promise in revolutionizing medical triage, particularly in the context of the rising prevalence of kidney-related conditions with the aging global population. This study evaluates the utility of ChatGPT, a large language model, in triaging nephrology cases through simulated real-world scenarios. Two nephrologists created 100 patient cases that encompassed various aspects of nephrology. ChatGPT’s performance in determining the appropriateness of nephrology consultations and identifying suitable nephrology subspecialties was assessed. The results demonstrated high accuracy; ChatGPT correctly determined the need for nephrology in 99–100% of cases, and it accurately identified the most suitable nephrology subspecialty triage in 96–99% of cases across two evaluation rounds. The agreement between the two rounds was 97%. While ChatGPT showed promise in improving medical triage efficiency and accuracy, the study also identified areas for refinement. This included the need for better integration of multidisciplinary care for patients with complex, intersecting medical conditions. This study’s findings highlight the potential of AI in enhancing decision-making processes in clinical workflow, and it can inform the development of AI-assisted triage systems tailored to institution-specific practices including multidisciplinary approaches. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1038/s41598-025-88074-5. Keywords: Artificial Intelligence, ChatGPT, Nephrology triage, Medical triage, Identify subspecialty Subject terms: Computational biology and bioinformatics, Health care, Nephrology, Engineering, Mathematics and computing, Evolution of language Introduction The global healthcare landscape faces mounting challenges due to the increasing prevalence of kidney-related conditions in the setting of an aging population 1 , 2 . As the demand for nephrological care continues to rise, healthcare systems are under immense pressure to optimize patient referral and triage processes. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a promising tool to revolutionize medical workflow by offering the potential to enhance decision-making efficiency and accuracy 3 , 4 . ChatGPT, a state-of-the-art AI language model developed by OpenAI, has garnered significant attention for its impressive capabilities in natural language processing and knowledge synthesis 5 – 17 . Its potential applications in healthcare have sparked excitement among researchers and clinicians alike. However, despite the growing interest in AI-assisted medical triage, empirical evidence on the effectiveness of AI models like ChatGPT in complex medical specialties such as nephrology remains limited 18 . Nephrology is a multifaceted discipline that encompasses a wide range of conditions affecting the kidneys and their related systems. The classification of nephrological cases often requires careful consideration of multiple complex factors, including patient history, comorbidities, and intersecting subspecialties 19 . Efficient and accurate triage is crucial in ensuring timely access to appropriate care and optimizing patient outcomes 20 . Traditional triage processes, however, can be time-consuming and prone to human error, particularly in high-volume healthcare settings 21 . The integration of AI in nephrological triage processes represents a significant opportunity to address these challenges. By utilizing the extensive parameters and processing capabilities of AI models like ChatGPT, which are trained on terabytes of data, healthcare providers could potentially streamline the triage process, reduce decision-making time and staff burden, and improve the accuracy of referrals. These AI models use pattern recognition and contextual analysis derived from their training datasets to provide clinically relevant recommendations 22 . These AI-assisted triage systems could independently analyze patient data, identify relevant clinical factors, and provide evidence-based recommendations for appropriate nephrological care. The intended user cases for this tool include primary care physicians in outpatient settings, where timely and accurate nephrology referrals are critical for optimizing patient outcomes. The tool could be implemented as an integrated feature within electronic health records (EHRs) to provide seamless, context-aware decision support during patient encounters or as a standalone application for use in diverse healthcare settings. Such implementation pathways would allow healthcare providers to utilize AI capabilities effectively while aligning with their existing workflows and technological infrastructure. Despite the growing interest in AI-assisted medical triage, there is a significant gap in knowledge regarding the effectiveness of AI models like ChatGPT in complex medical specialties such as nephrology 23 . While the potential benefits of AI-assisted triage in nephrology are promising, empirical evidence supporting these claims is limited. Before AI can be confidently deployed in real-world clinical settings, it is crucial to rigorously evaluate its performance and identify potential limitations. This proof-of-concept study systematically evaluates the capabilities of ChatGPT-4.0, a proprietary generative large language model (LLM) developed by OpenAI, in triaging nephrology cases and referring patients to appropriate nephrology subspecialties. Given the increasing demand for nephrological care in the context of a globally aging population, this study provides critical insights into the feasibility of deploying AI to enhance decision-making processes in clinical nephrology workflows. Through the utilization of a simulated dataset of 100 cases created by nephrologists, we explore ChatGPT’s potential to optimize referral accuracy and efficiency, thereby laying the groundwork for future AI-assisted triage systems tailored to nephrology practices. By examining ChatGPT’s accuracy in identifying the need for nephrology consultations and its ability to recommend suitable nephrology subspecialties, we seek to gain insights into the strengths and weaknesses of AI-assisted triage in this complex medical domain. Method This study systematically evaluates the capability of ChatGPT 4.0, an advanced AI language model, in assisting with the triage and referral of nephrology patients through a series of simulated case scenarios. Development of simulated cases Two board-certified nephrologists collaboratively developed a set of 100 focused nephrology cases (“ Online supplementary ”). These cases were designed to reflect a wide array of clinical conditions commonly encountered in nephrology practice. Each case was meticulously developed to include patient history, presenting symptoms, lab results, and imaging findings mimicking real-world clinical situations. The two nephrologists discussed and agreed on (1) whether the simulated case was appropriate for nephrology consultation and (2) what the nephrology subspecialty the simulated case should be triaged to. The nephrology subspecialty clinics included acute kidney injury, chronic kidney disease, hypertension, diabetic kidney disease, glomerular disease, kidney stone, electrolyte disorders, cystic kidney disease, onconephrology, and kidney transplantation. The simulated cases were distributed across the major nephrology subspecialties to reflect a comprehensive spectrum of clinical scenarios. The distribution included Acute Kidney Injury (15 cases), Chronic Kidney Disease (20 cases), Hypertension (10 cases), Diabetic Kidney Disease (15 cases), Glomerular Diseases (10 cases), Kidney Stones (5 cases), Acid-Base and Electrolyte Disorders (10 cases), Cystic Kidney Diseases (5 cases), Onconephrology (5 cases), and Kidney Transplantation (5 cases). While this distribution does not directly reflect the incidence or prevalence of these conditions in the general U.S. adult population, it was designed to include a broad range of clinical scenarios commonly encountered in nephrology practice. This approach ensured a diverse and representative evaluation of the AI tool’s capability across key subspecialties. Prompt templates and configuration parameters To evaluate ChatGPT’s performance in nephrology triage, we developed specific prompt templates and configuration settings tailored to the study objectives. The prompts instructed ChatGPT to determine whether nephrology consultation was required and, if appropriate, to recommend the most suitable nephrology subspecialty clinic. Special Instructions: ChatGPT was instructed to provide clear and concise responses for each case. Specifically: Answer ‘Yes’ or ‘No’ to whether nephrology consultation was required. If nephrology consultation was appropriate, recommend the most suitable subspecialty clinic from the following options: Glomerular Diseases, Chronic Kidney Disease, Diabetic Kidney Disease, Electrolyte Disorders, Hypertension, Onconephrology, Kidney Stones, Kidney Transplantation, Acute Kidney Injury, or Cystic Kidney Diseases. If nephrology consultation was not appropriate, respond with ‘Not Nephrology Related’ and suggest an alternative specialty. Temperature, Top_p, and Top_k Settings: The following parameters were configured to optimize ChatGPT’s clinical performance: Temperature: 0.3, to ensure deterministic and consistent responses. Top_p: 0.9, to maintain a balance between precision and diversity in token selection. Top_k: 50, to focus on the top-ranked tokens for relevant outputs. These settings were chosen to minimize variability and maintain accuracy in medical decision-making tasks. Evaluation procedure Each case scenario was entered into ChatGPT in the narrative form. ChatGPT was then queried (1) whether the case was appropriate for nephrology consult, and (2) what was the most suitable nephrology subspecialty clinic for the case. The ChatGPT-generated response was recorded. The evaluation of ChatGPT’s performance in the same 100 simulated cases was independently conducted in two rounds (each in separate months of March and April 2024) to test for consistency and reliability of ChatGPT’s performance over time. The simulated cases were built by W.C. and C.T., and the consensus agreement on the triage of each case was reached by P.K., C.T., and W.C., who were masked from ChatGPT’s responses. The accuracy of ChatGPT for determining the appropriateness of nephrology consult and identifying suitable nephrology subspecialties was determined by comparison to the consensus established by P.K., C.T., and W.C. Statistical analysis The accuracy between the first and the second round of ChatGPT’s response was compared using McNemar’s test. The agreement between ChatGPT’s responses in the first and second rounds was calculated using percentage agreement and Cohen’s kappa coefficient to assess the consistency of the AI’s performance across the two rounds. Statistical analyses were performed using the JMP statistical software (version 17.0, Cary, NC). Results In the first round, ChatGPT correctly determined the need for nephrology consultation in all (100%) simulated cases, and it correctly triaged to the most suitable nephrology subspecialty in 99 (99%) cases. The one incorrect nephrology subspecialty triage involved ChatGPT recommending an electrolyte disorder clinic instead of onconephrology for a patient with metastatic prostate cancer, worsening kidney function, and hypercalcemia. In the second round, ChatGPT correctly determined the need for a nephrology consult in 99 (99%) simulated cases, and it correctly triaged to the most suitable nephrology subspecialty in 96 (96%) cases (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Accuracy of ChatGPT’s Recommendations for Nephrology Consult and Subspecialty. Referral in two rounds of simulation The four incorrect nephrology subspecialty triage involved ChatGPT recommending: (1) against nephrology referral instead of cystic kidney disease clinic for a patient with simple cysts, (2) kidney transplantation clinic instead of onconephrology for a patient with kidney failure following bone marrow transplant, (3) chronic kidney disease instead of onconephrology for a breast cancer patient that developed kidney injury, and (4) electrolyte disorder clinic instead of onconephrology for a patient with metastatic prostate cancer, worsening kidney function, and hypercalcemia (same case as the first round). There was no statistical difference in accuracy between the first and second round of ChatGPT’s responses ( p = 0.25). The agreement between the first and second rounds of ChatGPT’s response was 97%, with a Cohen’s kappa coefficient of 0.94. Discussion Our study found that ChatGPT achieved an overall accuracy of 99.5% in determining the need for nephrology consultation across 100 simulated cases. The AI performed with a high degree of clinical accuracy, appropriately identifying the necessity for nephrology referrals. However, it exhibited some difficulty in accurately routing patients to the most appropriate nephrology subspecialties, particularly in complex cases involving multiple comorbidities. When this uncommonly occurred, the AI’s recommendations misdirected patients to less appropriate subspecialties, such as recommending an Electrolyte Disorders clinic for a cancer patient experiencing renal electrolyte complications. These errors highlight the potential gaps in AI’s understanding of the nuances in clinical scenarios where patient conditions may span multiple specialty areas. Literature on AI in medical triage, especially in complex specialties like nephrology, is sparse. Previous studies have predominantly focused on AI in broader clinical decision support systems, with few exploring the specific context of nephrology 24 – 26 . Our findings of AI’s high accuracy in identifying the need for nephrology consultation align with the promising results seen in other specialties, but its potential mistriage to nephrology subspeciality clinics underscore AI’s unique challenges in nephrology due to the high complexity and interplay of various subspecialties. The high accuracy of ChatGPT in basic triage decisions can be attributed to its extensive training in a wide range of medical texts and guidelines 27 . However, the errors in some subspecialty referrals likely stem from limitations in the training dataset’s coverage of complex, intersecting conditions and the inherent challenge of simulating the depth of human specialist expertise 28 . The integration of AI-assisted triage systems into clinical workflows presents significant ethical and medicolegal challenges that warrant careful consideration. A primary concern is algorithmic bias, which could lead to disparities in care recommendations. This risk arises from the possibility that AI models, including ChatGPT, may be trained on datasets that do not adequately represent diverse patient populations or clinical scenarios. To mitigate this, it is essential to continuously refine training datasets to ensure inclusivity and fairness, incorporating data from a wide range of demographics, clinical contexts, and geographic regions. Patient privacy and data security are additional concerns. AI systems must adhere to strict regulatory standards, such as Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) in the United States, to safeguard sensitive patient information. Transparent data handling practices and robust encryption protocols should be implemented to address these concerns. Furthermore, clear delineation of accountability is crucial. In cases where AI-generated recommendations result in adverse outcomes, there must be a framework to identify whether responsibility lies with the AI developers, healthcare providers, or the institutions deploying the tool. Developing guidelines and regulatory policies to clarify accountability and liability will be vital to fostering trust in these systems. The use of AI systems, including LLMs, in healthcare workflows raises important concerns about data privacy and compliance with regulations such as the HIPAA. Transmitting protected health information (PHI) or personally identifiable information (PII) to an LLM without a business associate agreement (BAA) constitutes a HIPAA violation. While OpenAI is eligible to serve as a BAA under specific conditions 29 , limitations in its scope necessitate a careful review of vendor claims and contractual agreements. Additionally, data breaches involving major healthcare entities highlight the risks of unauthorized access to sensitive patient data and the importance of robust safeguards in AI implementation 30 . Even anonymized datasets can be re-identified when combined with other data sources, a risk that is amplified in the era of advanced models like GPT-4. This underscores the need for secure data-handling practices, including encryption, data masking, and strict access controls, when deploying AI systems for clinical applications 30 . In this study, we ensured compliance with HIPAA and data privacy standards by exclusively using simulated case scenarios that did not contain real patient data. However, we acknowledge that real-world applications require institutions to establish enforceable BAAs and implement rigorous data protection measures. These include regular audits, validation of vendor compliance, and transparent communication with clinicians and patients regarding the use of AI in clinical workflows. To enhance the utility of AI in nephrology triage, several recommendations are proposed. One, increased specialization awareness can be achieved by training machine learning models on the specific interdependencies and symptomatology related to nephrology and its subspecialties. Two, integration of multidisciplinary data, including insights from related fields such as oncology, would allow the AI to develop a more nuanced understanding of complex cases. Three, customization for respective institutional practices, with AI systems tailored to match local referral patterns and guidelines, is essential for maximizing the relevance and effectiveness of AI recommendations. Beyond aligning with referral patterns and guidelines, these AI systems should also consider the availability of nephrologists and the specific capacities of nephrology service lines. Incorporating these factors ensures that the recommendations are not only clinically appropriate but also logistically feasible, addressing the unique constraints and resources of each healthcare institution. These recommendations could be effectively addressed by employing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), an advanced AI approach that integrates external knowledge sources into the generative process 31 . RAG dynamically retrieves contextually relevant information from structured or unstructured databases during inference, enabling the AI to generate outputs that reflect the latest clinical guidelines, multidisciplinary insights, and institutional protocols. This capability would not only enhance the model’s specialization awareness but also improve its adaptability to local practices and evolving clinical standards, ensuring more accurate and context-specific recommendations in nephrology triage. Ethical considerations surrounding the use of AI in triage of nephrology consultation and subspecialty referral warrant further discussion. The potential for bias in AI algorithms is a significant concern, as it may lead to unintentional disparities in care recommendations and exacerbate existing healthcare inequities 28 . Incorporating robust monitoring mechanisms and real-time accuracy alerts into deployed AI systems is critical to ensure their safety and reliability in clinical practice. Such mechanisms can identify deviations in performance early, allowing timely interventions and maintaining trust in the system’s outputs 32 . However, achieving transparency with LLMs poses unique challenges due to their ‘black box’ nature. The architecture of LLMs includes billions of parameters, hidden layers with weights and biases, and complex mechanisms such as activation functions and dropouts, which collectively obscure the pathways through which these models generate outputs. Existing tools for explainability, such as Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations (LIME), have limited applicability to deep learning models like LLMs, as they are designed for simpler machine learning algorithms. Despite these challenges, transparency is essential, particularly in clinical applications where accountability and patient safety are critical. Ongoing research efforts are focused on developing advanced explainability techniques tailored to LLMs, such as SHAP (Shapley Additive Explanations) and model-specific visualization methods, to provide interpretable insights into the factors influencing AI-generated recommendations, enabling clinicians and patients to better understand and evaluate the rationale behind decisions. In the meantime, implementing robust validation processes, prospective studies in real-world settings, and continuous monitoring can help mitigate the risks associated with the opacity of LLMs. Furthermore, emphasizing human oversight and intervention ensures that AI tools augment rather than replace clinical judgment. Educating both clinicians and patients about the capabilities, limitations, and reliability of AI systems is essential to fostering trust and informed decision-making while acknowledging the complexities inherent in achieving full transparency 33 . To manage the ethical and medicolegal risks associated with AI-assisted triage systems, a multidisciplinary approach is essential. One strategy is the establishment of human-AI collaboration frameworks that emphasize the role of clinicians as final decision-makers. AI tools like ChatGPT should augment, rather than replace, clinical judgment, and their recommendations should be subject to clinician oversight. This approach ensures that AI serves as a decision-support tool rather than an autonomous decision-maker. Robust validation processes are another critical component. These should include prospective studies in real-world clinical settings to evaluate AI performance across diverse patient populations and healthcare environments. Continuous monitoring and feedback loops are essential to ensure that the AI system adapts to new clinical evidence and evolving medical practices. Transparency is also key; AI systems must provide explainable outputs that enable clinicians and patients to understand the rationale behind recommendations. This can foster trust and acceptance while enabling users to identify and address potential errors or biases in the AI’s reasoning. Finally, clear communication with patients regarding the role of AI in their care is vital. Educating patients about the capabilities and limitations of AI systems can alleviate concerns and enhance their confidence in the technology. Transparent reporting of AI’s accuracy, error rates, and validation processes can further support informed decision-making by both patients and clinicians. Patient perspectives on AI-assisted nephrology referral triage and subspecialty referrals are another important aspect to consider. While AI has the potential to improve efficiency and accuracy of triage processes in nephrology, it is crucial to understand patients’ attitudes and concerns regarding AI involvement in their care. Patients may have reservations about the privacy and security of their medical data when used by AI systems for nephrology triage 34 , 35 . They may also question the quality and reliability of AI-generated recommendations for nephrology consultations and subspecialty referrals compared to those provided by human healthcare professionals 36 . Building trust and transparency around the use of AI in nephrology triage is essential for fostering patient acceptance and optimizing the benefits of these technologies. Engaging patients in the development and evaluation of AI-assisted nephrology triage systems can help address these concerns and ensure that the systems prioritize patient-centered care. This study’s limitations include its reliance on simulated case scenarios, which may not capture all the nuances of real-world clinical contexts in nephrology. Additionally, the evaluation was limited to a single AI model and conducted by a small number of nephrologists, which may affect the generalizability of the results to other nephrology settings. Continuous learning and feedback mechanisms from real-world performance in nephrology triage may enable AI to further adapt and improve over time. Expanded validation studies across different demographic groups and nephrology settings would help confirm AI’s utility and attempt to catalog existing biases. Recognizing and addressing these biases is critical to improving the fairness and reliability of AI-assisted triage systems, ensuring that they serve diverse patient populations equitably. Establishing robust ethical and safety oversight is crucial, ensuring that AI recommendations augment rather than replace clinical judgment in nephrology referral triage and subspecialty referrals 37 . Optimization of the user interface for AI-assisted triage systems, whether as a standalone application or integrated within EHRs, would enhance ease of use for nephrologists and other healthcare providers. Such optimization would support the understanding and evaluation of AI-driven advice in nephrology triage, helping to make the system’s outputs more accessible and aligned with existing clinical workflows. The integration of AI-assisted triage systems into existing clinical workflows in nephrology is another critical consideration. Successful implementation requires careful planning and coordination to ensure seamless integration with current nephrology practices and minimize disruptions. Nephrologists and other healthcare providers using AI-assisted triage systems will need appropriate training and support to incorporate AI’s recommendations effectively. Collaboration among stakeholders, including health systems, vendors, and regulatory agencies, will be important to provide and fund training. Proficiency evaluation may involve standardized metrics developed by organizations like American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM), vendors, or third-party bodies to ensure confidence and competence in using these tools. The impact on workload and time management should also be evaluated, as the introduction of AI tools may initially require additional time for adaptation and verification of results in nephrology triage. While this study provides a proof-of-concept for the use of AI in nephrology triage, we acknowledge that implementing such tools within a health system’s EHR presents significant technical and ethical challenges. Deployment in real-world clinical settings would require robust infrastructure designed to protect sensitive patient data, including near air-gapped systems, advanced encryption protocols, and multi-layered security measures to ensure compliance with regulations such as HIPAA and to safeguard against potential cyber threats. Comprehensive validation within the intended healthcare environment would be critical to ensure that the tool functions reliably and securely under diverse operational conditions. Integration into an EHR would necessitate collaboration between clinicians, data scientists, and IT security professionals to establish a secure and seamless workflow. Institutions would need to perform rigorous risk assessments, conduct regular security audits, and implement continuous monitoring systems to address evolving threats. Beyond technical considerations, ethical oversight is crucial to ensure transparency, fairness, and accountability in the deployment of AI tools, addressing potential limitations and risks associated with their use in clinical decision support while reinforcing trust among healthcare providers and patients. As clinicians, we conducted this study as a proof-of-concept using simulated cases to evaluate the potential of ChatGPT in improving nephrology triage processes. While the findings demonstrate promise, they represent an intellectual exploration rather than an immediately deployable solution. Future research should focus on addressing the infrastructure and security requirements for real-world implementation, including pilot studies in controlled healthcare environments to validate the tool’s safety, reliability, and efficacy. Furthermore, the necessary infrastructure, such as secure data storage and processing capabilities, must be in place to support the deployment of AI-assisted triage systems in nephrology settings 38 . Ongoing quality assurance and review as the field of AI and medicine changes over time will be needed. Addressing these practical aspects of integration is crucial for the successful adoption and utilization of AI in nephrology referral triage and subspecialty referrals. The economic implications of implementing AI-assisted triage systems in nephrology should be explored. Developing, deploying, and maintaining these systems requires significant financial investments, including costs associated with data acquisition, model training, infrastructure setup, and ongoing technical support specific to nephrology 39 . However, the potential benefits of AI-assisted triage in nephrology, such as improved efficiency, reduced wait times, the ability to reallocate healthcare staff time, and decreasing healthcare staff burden and burnout, may contribute to long-term cost savings for healthcare systems 39 , 40 . Conducting thorough economic evaluations, considering both the direct and indirect benefits and costs in nephrology settings, can help inform decision-making and resource allocation strategies for AI adoption in nephrology triage 41 . Understanding the economic viability and sustainability of AI-assisted triage systems is essential for their widespread implementation and long-term success in nephrology 42 . It is important to acknowledge the non-deterministic nature of LLMs such as ChatGPT. These models generate responses probabilistically, which can result in slight variability in outputs even when identical inputs are provided. While the configuration of parameters such as temperature can reduce response variability, this inherent characteristic remains a limitation of LLM-based systems. In this study, we conducted two rounds of evaluation using identical simulated cases to assess consistency and reliability, finding high agreement across rounds. However, these measures do not fully address the variability inherent to LLMs. Another recognized limitation of LLMs is their predilection to hallucinate or generate false information, particularly in scenarios where prompts are ambiguous or incomplete. This risk underscores the importance of comprehensive prompt design and rigorous validation processes in clinical applications. To mitigate these risks, human oversight remains essential, as clinicians must critically evaluate AI-generated outputs before incorporating them into decision-making processes. Future research should focus on refining LLMs through domain-specific training, implementing mechanisms to detect and flag potentially inaccurate responses, and enhancing model explainability to ensure the reliability and safety of LLM-based tools in healthcare settings. Future research should focus on expanding the AI’s training dataset to include more complex nephrology cases and integrating feedback loops from actual clinical use in nephrology triage to refine its algorithms. Studies should also explore the integration of AI with other digital health tools to enhance multidisciplinary care coordination in nephrology. While this study used a curated set of synthetic cases as a primary test cohort to evaluate the performance of ChatGPT in nephrology triage, we acknowledge the importance of validating these findings on larger and more diverse datasets. A second validation cohort comprising historical real-world referrals or prospective data would provide a more representative evaluation of the model’s applicability in clinical practice. Such an approach would allow for testing across a broader range of clinical scenarios, further assessing the model’s generalizability, accuracy, and ability to address variability in referral patterns. Additionally, this study focused exclusively on true positive cases to evaluate ChatGPT’s accuracy and reliability in determining the appropriateness of nephrology consultations and subspecialty triage. While this approach provided valuable insights into the model’s ability to correctly identify appropriate nephrology referrals, it did not assess its performance in distinguishing between true positive and true negative cases. Incorporating true negative cases—those that do not require nephrology consultation—would allow for a more comprehensive evaluation of the model’s discrimination performance, including metrics such as recall, specificity, area under the curve (AUC), and F1 score. The generalizability of AI-assisted triage systems for identifying nephrology subspecialties is influenced by the availability of subspecialists and subspecialty clinics, which can vary widely across healthcare institutions. While this approach aims to enhance precision in subspecialty referrals, the primary goal in many clinical settings should be to facilitate timely nephrology consultations. This is particularly relevant in institutions where nephrology services are consolidated, and subspecialty distinctions are less practical due to limited resources. Future iterations of AI systems should be designed to accommodate varying institutional capabilities, allowing flexibility to recommend either nephrology consultations broadly or specific subspecialty referrals based on local availability. This adaptability ensures that the tool remains useful and applicable in diverse healthcare environments, ranging from large academic centers to smaller community hospitals. In addition, while this study focuses on the performance of ChatGPT in nephrology triage, it is important to contextualize the findings by comparing them to other AI models and existing triage methods used in nephrology. A comparative analysis can help identify the strengths and limitations of ChatGPT in relation to other approaches in nephrology triage. For example, some AI models may excel in specific aspects of nephrology triage, such as identifying urgent cases or predicting patient outcomes, while others may have a more comprehensive understanding of multidisciplinary care considerations in nephrology. Similarly, comparing ChatGPT’s performance to traditional triage methods used in nephrology can highlight areas where AI can offer significant improvements, as well as scenarios that require human expertise and clinical judgment due to the complexity of cases or the need for individualized care. By conducting a comparative analysis, researchers can gain a more nuanced understanding of ChatGPT’s capabilities and potential contributions to nephrology triage, inform future development and refinement efforts, and allow customization of AI systems to distinct medical systems. In conclusion, ChatGPT has demonstrated considerable potential to enhance nephrology triage processes, achieving high accuracy in identifying the need for nephrology consultations. However, the study also highlighted critical areas for improvement, particularly in the AI’s ability to integrate multidisciplinary care approaches and handle complex patient presentations in nephrology. The next steps in the development of AI-assisted triage systems for nephrology involve exploring their role as an initial screening tool designed to enhance process efficiency without negating human involvement in clinical decision-making. Such systems could provide preliminary recommendations regarding nephrology consultations and subspecialty referrals, with clinicians serving as the final arbiters of decision-making. This approach ensures that AI functions as an augmentative tool, supporting rather than replacing human judgment. By addressing these areas, future AI-assisted triage systems can be better aligned with nephrology practices and the multifaceted nature of medical care, paving the way for broader adoption in nephrology settings. This study lays a foundational step towards realizing the transformative potential of AI in improving medical triage, particularly in nephrology, a specialty that contends with high medical complexity. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (310KB, pdf) Acknowledgements None. Author contributions Conceptualization: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Data curation: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., and W.C.;Formal analysis: P.K., C.T., J.M., and W.C.;Investigation: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Methodology: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Project administration: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Resource: C.T., J.M., and W.C.;Software: C.T.;Supervision: C.T., J.M., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Validation: P.K., C.T., J.M., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Visualization: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Writing – original draft: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.;Writing – review and editing: P.K., C.T., J.M., O.A.V., M.S.S., S.S., M.A.M., J.H.P., I.M.C., and W.C.All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. Data availability The data used to support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author reasonable request. Declarations Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Jadoul, M., Aoun, M. & Masimango Imani, M. The major global burden of chronic kidney disease. Lancet Glob Health . 12 , e342–e343. 10.1016/s2214-109x(24)00050-0 (2024). [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Kovesdy, C. P. Epidemiology of chronic kidney disease: An update 2022. Kidney Int. Suppl. 12 , 7–11 (2011). 10.1016/j.kisu.2021.11.003 (2022). [ D