 Scholar ] 23. Fowler KJ. Advancing American kidney health (AAKH): catalyst for Investment in Kidney Diseases Clinical Trials and Precision Medicine: an opportunity to advance upstream interventions and the importance of nephrology. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol. 2020;15(12):1689–1691. doi: 10.2215/CJN.03660320. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Recommendations | Chronic kidney disease: assessment and management | Guidance | NICE. Accessed June 8, 2022. https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng203/chapter/Recommendations#risk-assessment-referral-criteria-and-shared-care 25. Bhachu HK, Cockwell P, Subramanian A, et al. Impact of using risk-based stratification on referral of patients with chronic kidney Disease from primary care to specialist Care in the United Kingdom. Kidney Int Rep. 2021;6(8):2189–2199. doi: 10.1016/j.ekir.2021.05.031. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Wang M, Peter SS, Chu CD, Tuot DS, Chen JH. Analysis of specialty nephrology care among patients with chronic kidney Disease and high risk of Disease progression. JAMA Netw Open. 2022;5(8):e2225797. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2022.25797. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. College of American Pathologists Clinical Chemistry Committee. Kidney biomarkers: the kidney profile order, urine albumin-creatinine ratio (uACR), and estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR). Published online 2020. Accessed October 2, 2022. https://documents.cap.org/documents/2020-a-kidney-biomarkers.pdf Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 12875_2022_1910_MOESM1_ESM.docx (55.4KB, docx) Additional file 1. Supplementary Material: Albuminuria Testing and Nephrology Care among Insured US Adults with Chronic Kidney Disease: A Missed Opportunity. Table S1. Diagnosis and Procedure Codes for Comorbidities. Fig. S1. Proportion of Patients Receiving Nephrology Care Stratified by eGFR, Albuminuria, and CVD Status Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from Optum Labs but restrictions apply to the availability of these data, which were used under license for the current study, and so are not publicly available. Data are however available from the authors upon reasonable request and with permission of Optum Labs.',579,'2025-10-15 05:56:28','2025-10-15 05:56:28');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3243,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10843340','Performance of ChatGPT on Nephrology Test Questions.','Miao J, Thongprayoon C, Garcia Valencia OA, Krisanapan P, Sheikh MS, Davis PW, Mekraksakit P, Suarez MG, Craici IM, Cheungpasitporn W.','2023','dislike verifiable nephsap ksap renew refresh retry reattempt copying pasting visuals nephsap ksap nephsap ksap kbsd ticd parasitologists excelling deepmind medpalm medpalm pubmedqa medmcqa multitask hallucinate dislike dislike inputting dependability kurtis heidi winkel bogacz slagle furnishing cheungpasitporn craici mekraksakit krisanapan aura com7 dohome jmt sabina kalbe genexine apexcela zuellig wisit cheungpasitporn charat thongprayoon pajaree krisanapan poemlarp mekraksakit wisit cheungpasitporn charat thongprayoon wisit cheungpasitporn iasmina craici pajaree krisanapan poemlarp mekraksakit charat thongprayoon wisit cheungpasitporn pajaree krisanapan poemlarp mekraksakit charat thongprayoon wisit cheungpasitporn wisit cheungpasitporn wisit cheungpasitporn charat thongprayoon wisit cheungpasitporn iasmina craici pajaree krisanapan poemlarp mekraksakit charat thongprayoon b817 examinee krisanapan tangpanithandee thongprayoon pattharanitima cheungpasitporn jcm12083018 thongprayoon kaewput kovvuru jcm9041107 thongprayoon vaitla jadlowiec e221286 e46885 sallam healthcare11060887 safranek e45312 cheatham medenilla e0000198 pdig zuluaga greenblatt hvad058 hoffer thirunavukarasu trialling e46599 thongprayoon cheungpasitporn astr rosol gasior łaba korzeniewski młynczak deebel terlecki mihalache muni suchman trindade jeehp jcma giannos delardas bmat tmua lnat e47737 takagi watari erabi sakaguchi e48002 bhayana bleakney e230582 nori carignan horvitz gottweis anil firat suppadungsuk thongprayoon krisanapan jcm12175550 suppadungsuk thongprayoon jpm13091363','Visual Abstract Open in a new tab Keywords: clinical nephrology, kidney disease, medical education Abstract Background ChatGPT is a novel tool that allows people to engage in conversations with an advanced machine learning model. ChatGPT''s performance in the US Medical Licensing Examination is comparable with a successful candidate''s performance. However, its performance in the nephrology field remains undetermined. This study assessed ChatGPT''s capabilities in answering nephrology test questions. Methods Questions sourced from Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and Kidney Self-Assessment Program were used, each with multiple-choice single-answer questions. Questions containing visual elements were excluded. Each question bank was run twice using GPT-3.5 and GPT-4. Total accuracy rate, defined as the percentage of correct answers obtained by ChatGPT in either the first or second run, and the total concordance, defined as the percentage of identical answers provided by ChatGPT during both runs, regardless of their correctness, were used to assess its performance. Results A comprehensive assessment was conducted on a set of 975 questions, comprising 508 questions from Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and 467 from Kidney Self-Assessment Program. GPT-3.5 resulted in a total accuracy rate of 51%. Notably, the employment of Nephrology Self-Assessment Program yielded a higher accuracy rate compared with Kidney Self-Assessment Program (58% versus 44%; P < 0.001). The total concordance rate across all questions was 78%, with correct answers exhibiting a higher concordance rate (84%) compared with incorrect answers (73%) ( P < 0.001). When examining various nephrology subfields, the total accuracy rates were relatively lower in electrolyte and acid-base disorder, glomerular disease, and kidney-related bone and stone disorders. The total accuracy rate of GPT-4''s response was 74%, higher than GPT-3.5 ( P < 0.001) but remained below the passing threshold and average scores of nephrology examinees (77%). Conclusions ChatGPT exhibited limitations regarding accuracy and repeatability when addressing nephrology-related questions. Variations in performance were evident across various subfields. Introduction The significant advancements in the field of machine learning, which is a prominent subset of artificial intelligence (AI), have provided us with a powerful tool for the automated analysis of extensive datasets. This transformative technology has already demonstrated its efficacy in the realm of clinical medicine, such as distinct subgroup identification in kidney transplant patients and CKD management. 1 – 3 Introduced by OpenAI in 2022, ChatGPT is a language model fostering meaningful human–AI interactions. 4 ChatGPT''s training involved reinforcement learning with human feedback, supervised fine-tuning, and later, proximal policy optimization. ChatGPT, a vast large language model, can understand, interpret, and respond similarly to humans. Users can like or dislike ChatGPT''s answers, crucial feedback avenues that enhance its capabilities. 4 Using advanced machine learning, ChatGPT offers insightful responses across medical research, health care, and education. 5 , 6 Remarkably, ChatGPT exhibits superior capabilities in medical knowledge. 7 , 8 This is evidenced by its performance, which has been demonstrated to be on par with that of a successful candidate in major assessments such as the US Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE). 7 , 8 This achievement underscores the model''s deep understanding and highlights its potential to assist users in the medical field. It is crucial to emphasize that ChatGPT is a general purpose model that was not explicitly fine-tuned for the medical domain. 7 , 8 At present, two versions of ChatGPT are available. The first version, GPT-3.5, was released in November 2022 and is offered as a free version. The second version, GPT-4, was introduced in March 2023 and is available as a paid version. 4 While deep learning models identify patterns and predict, they do not always match human reasoning or accuracy. 1 – 3 This is most evident when they are presented with complex, nuanced, or subjective information, where their lack of genuine comprehension can lead to inaccuracies or inconsistencies. They also lack the ability to critically analyze or question the information they are presented with, a key aspect of human cognition. 9 Further illustrating this point, different users may receive inconsistent responses to identical prompts, something that human cognition would typically not be subject to. 9 Moreover, these models may not always provide verifiable references to substantiate an answer and, in some instances, may even cite fabricated sources. 6 , 10 In addition, an observational study using the General Practitioners Applied Knowledge Test revealed that ChatGPT''s overall performance was 60%, falling below the average passing threshold (70%) of the previous 2 years. 11 In our previous investigation, ChatGPT demonstrated a limited performance, with an accuracy of 45% and an agreement rate of 74% when responding to questions related to glomerular disease, a specialized area within nephrology. 12 Therefore, it is important to delineate the difference between these capabilities and true human understanding. To gain a deeper understanding of ChatGPT''s capabilities and limitations, we performed a comprehensive study with a specific focus on assessing its performance in nephrology test questions. By examining ChatGPT''s responses in this specific domain, we aimed to shed light on its expertise, accuracy, and suitability for addressing medical inquiries pertaining to this crucial area of health care. Our anticipation is to demonstrate that ChatGPT may possess a certain level of performance in addressing medical questions within the field of nephrology. Methods Nephrology Test Question Bank Used in This Study To conduct our assessment, we used the Nephrology Self-Assessment Program (NephSAP) and Kidney Self-Assessment Program (KSAP) tests, both administered by the American Society of Nephrology (ASN), which use multiple-choice single-answer questions. Nephrology Self-Assessment Program provides a learning vehicle for health care professionals to renew and refresh their clinical knowledge and diagnostic and therapeutic skills, whereas Kidney Self-Assessment Program is mainly designed for the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) nephrology board preparation. In the case of Kidney Self-Assessment Program modules, participants are granted two attempts to answer each question, and the system offers immediate feedback on the accuracy of their answers, allowing them to gauge their performance in real time. They can either choose to attempt the question again immediately after giving an incorrect answer and then move on to the next one or they can complete all questions in the first round and use the second chance to retry all questions. Similarly, Nephrology Self-Assessment Program also allows participants two attempts to pass the examination. However, in Nephrology Self-Assessment Program, participants are expected to complete all questions in the initial round, and during the test, they remain unaware of the correctness of their answers. If they do not pass, they are provided with the chance to reattempt all the questions at their own discretion. To earn continuing medical education credits and maintenance of certification points, participants need to achieve a passing score of 75% for Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and 76% for Kidney Self-Assessment Program or higher. Nephrology Test Questions and Subfields Examined in This Study Each issue of Nephrology Self-Assessment Program focuses on a particular area of clinical nephrology and encompasses 30 self-assessment questions. For our assessment, we tested the recent 17 Nephrology Self-Assessment Program question banks ( Supplemental Table 1 ), which cover eight subfields of nephrology, including glomerular disease, hypertension, kidney transplantation, CKD, kidney failure, AKI, electrolytes and acid-base disorders, and kidney-related bone and stone disorders. Each module within Kidney Self-Assessment Program comprises 75 questions, incorporating content from all ten ABIM nephrology blueprint categories. Eight of these categories align with those covered in Nephrology Self-Assessment Program while the remaining two categories are dedicated to pharmacology and tubulointerstitial and cystic disorders, respectively. For our assessment, we examined the recent seven Kidney Self-Assessment Program question banks ( Supplemental Table 2 ). Study Design We opted to use GPT-3.5, a free version of ChatGPT, because it is easily accessible to the general population, including health care professionals and patients. In the interest of robustness and authenticity in our assessment, we chose to allow ChatGPT to answer each question only once. To ensure a comprehensive assessment, we conducted two separate runs of each question bank, within a 12-day interval (March 13 version used on the first run and March 23 version used on the second run). This methodology was designed to precisely evaluate ChatGPT''s performance in a real-world scenario without the influence of feedback loops or repeated attempts on the same run. Although comparing ChatGPT''s performance directly to that of humans may not be entirely accurate, it allowed us to observe the repeatability of the model''s responses and understand the reliability of its performance. We have also evaluated the performance of GPT-4 (July 20 version), the latest version that requires a subscription and monthly payment. We integrated text-based questions by directly copying and pasting the content. However, because of the complex and diverse nature of medical images, modern AI language models, such as ChatGPT, encounter difficulties in accurately recognizing and analyzing these clinical visuals. As a result, questions that involved visual elements, such as clinical images, medical photographs, and graphs, were excluded from our assessment, following the approach taken by previous studies. 7 , 8 , 11 , 13 , 14 Assessment of ChatGPT''s Performance ChatGPT''s responses were recorded and compared against the correct answers provided by Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and Kidney Self-Assessment Program, respectively. The following metrics were used to assess its performance: Accuracy rate for each run, defined as the percentage of correct answers provided by ChatGPT in each run. Total accuracy rate, defined as the percentage of correct answers obtained by ChatGPT in either the first or second run. Concordance rate of correct answers, defined as the percentage of identical correct answers obtained by ChatGPT between both runs by comparing the results with those of the initial run. Concordance rate of incorrect answers, defined as the percentage of identical incorrect answers obtained by ChatGPT between both runs by comparing the results with those of the initial run. Total concordance rate, defined as the percentage of identical answers provided by ChatGPT during both runs, regardless of their correctness. Statistical Analysis We reported accuracy and concordance rate as counts with percentage. We compared accuracy rate of ChatGPT''s response between Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and Kidney Self-Assessment Program, between the first and second run, and between nephrology subfields using the Chi-squared test. A direct statistical comparison of ChatGPT''s performance with that of nephrology examinees was not feasible, given the summary format of the data provided by ASN for the Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and Kidney Self-Assessment Program tests. We compared accuracy and concordance rate between GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 using the McNemar test. We considered P value < 0.05 as statistically significant. We performed analyses using JMP statistical software (version 16; SAS institute, Cary, NC). Results Accuracy of GPT-3.5 in Answering Nephrology Test Questions This study encompassed a thorough analysis of a total of 975 questions, with 508 questions sourced from Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and 467 from Kidney Self-Assessment Program. The total accuracy rate of GPT-3.5''s response for all questions examined in the study was 51% (Table 1 ). Notably, the GPT-3.5''s response to Nephrology Self-Assessment Program yielded a higher total accuracy rate compared with Kidney Self-Assessment Program (58% versus 44% for Nephrology Self-Assessment Program and Kidney Self-Assessment Program, respectively; P < 0.001) (Figure 1 A). The total accuracy rate of GPT-3.5 was comparable between the first and second run (46% versus 44% in the first and second run, respectively; P = 0.15) (Figure 1 B). Table 1. Performance of GPT-3.5 on nephrology test questions and by different nephrology subfields Questions by Subfields Nephrology Self-Assessment Program Kidney Self-Assessment Program Overall Correct Incorrect Correct Incorrect Correct Incorrect Nephrology questions, No. 508 467 975 1st run, No. (%) 270 (53) 238 (47) 174 (37) 293 (63) 444 (46) 531 (54) 2nd run, No. (%) 258 (51) 250 (49) 170 (36) 297 (64) 428 (44) 547 (56) Concordance between 2 runs, No. (%) 235 (87) 178 (75) 139 (80) 209 (71) 374 (84) 387 (73) Total concordance, No. (%) 413 (81) 348 (75) 761 (78) Total accuracy, No. (%) 294 (58) 205 (44) 499 (51) Glomerular disease, No. 90 33 123 1st run, No. (%) 38 (42) 52 (58) 13 (39) 20 (61) 51 (41) 72 (59) 2nd run, No. (%) 32 (36) 58 (65) 14 (42) 19 (58) 46 (37) 77 (63) Concordance between 2 runs, No. (%) 30 (79) 36 (69) 11 (85) 15 (75) 41 (79) 50 (70) Total concordance, No. (%) 66 (73) 26 (79) 92 (75) Total accuracy, No. (%) 40 (44) 16 (49) 56 (46) Hypertension, No. 60 42 102 1st run, No. (%) 37 (62) 23 (38) 14 (33) 28 (67) 51 (50) 51 (50) 2nd run, No. (%) 35 (58) 25 (42) 14 (33) 28 (67) 49 (48) 53 (52) Concordance between 2 runs, No. (%) 35 (95) 22 (96) 9 (64) 20 (71) 44 (86) 42 (82) Total concordance, No. (%) 57 (95) 29 (69) 86 (84) Total accuracy, No. (%) 37 (62) 19 (45) 56 (55) A