rather than making any claims about its impact on clinical outcomes. In our comprehensive assessment, it became evident that ChatGPT''s performance varied significantly across different subfields of nephrology. It appears to demonstrate competence when handling inquiries rooted in factual knowledge, excelling in delivering accurate responses to clear-cut queries. Yet, the AI model struggles with more complex tasks requiring advanced comprehension, analytical abilities, and precise calculations. As indicated by a number of studies, 16 , 20 – 22 ChatGPT''s limitations in handling scientific and mathematical applications, particularly those demanding high-level cognitive engagement, become evident. Fluctuations in accuracy may be linked to the nature of subfield questions, even without explicit categorization. This could affect ChatGPT''s performance in these subfields. For instance, in the field of nephrology, subfields related to electrolytes and acid-base disorders, as opposed to AKI, CKD/kidney failure, and kidney transplant, may demand more intricate calculations. Thus, ChatGPT presented a particular challenge in answering these questions, reflecting in lower accuracy rates. Low accuracy in glomerular disease may also be linked to the distinct nature of questions, requiring a nuanced interpretation of kidney anatomy, pathology, and pharmacology. Currently, relying on ChatGPT for question answering, particularly in nephrology fields, seems impractical, even in cases where the highest accuracy rate and concordance rate were achieved. Although ChatGPT shows potential in medical fields, its ability to handle complex tasks needs further research. As AI models evolve, understanding these limitations is key to optimizing their medical use. Recent studies show GPT-4 outperformed GPT-3.5 by 24%–30% in various medical examinations. 13 , 14 , 21 , 23 These findings indicate a significant enhancement in the model''s capabilities. However, a study using the American College of Gastroenterology Test found GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 had scores of 65% and 62%, respectively. 17 It is important to note that both models failed the test. 17 During our study, we observed that GPT-4 displayed a remarkable 23% higher accuracy when answering nephrology test questions compared with GPT-3.5. However, it is crucial to highlight that the total accuracy rate remains below the passing threshold mandated by ASN, and the total concordance rate falls short of 85%. Recently, Google and DeepMind introduced their large language model, named PaLM 2, alongside a medical domain–specific version called MedPaLM. An assessment of GPT-4 and MedPaLM 2 performance on various evaluations, including USMLE, PubMedQA, MedMCQA, and Massive Multitask Language Understanding, indicates striking similarity. 24 , 25 However, it is important to note that even with these improvements, the accuracy of GPT-4 remains below 80% and falls short of the average scores achieved by medical students or examinees. 14 , 21 We also obtained relevant data regarding the performance of nephrologists and/or nephrology trainees on the Kidney Self-Assessment Program and Nephrology Self-Assessment Program tests from ASN. The Kidney Self-Assessment Program accuracy rate was 80%, obviously higher than the passing threshold. As for the Nephrology Self-Assessment Program test, the accuracy rate was 69% for the first attempt and 77% for the second attempt. It is worth highlighting that for Nephrology Self-Assessment Program, examinees achieved significantly higher average scores on their second attempts, surpassing ASN''s passing threshold. By contrast, both GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 exhibited similar accuracy rates between their first and second attempts in both Kidney Self-Assessment Program and Nephrology Self-Assessment Program. Therefore, ChatGPT, including GPT-4, displays a difference in accuracy compared with human performance because these models generate responses on the basis of patterns learned from data, lacking the deep comprehension exhibited by humans. Thus, verifying ChatGPT''s responses is vital because models can sometimes hallucinate details. 26 It is also crucial to acknowledge that at present, human oversight remains essential at the end of the AI processing chain. Given ChatGPT''s current performance, it should not be the sole resource for medical queries, especially in nephrology. Ensuring high levels of accuracy is absolutely critical in the health care context because decisions made on the basis of this information significantly affect patient safety, the reliability of information, and the efficacy of decision-making processes. 27 An accuracy rate of 51%, improving to 74% with GPT-4, means a risk of incorrect information with potential adverse outcomes. As a result, AI models, such as ChatGPT, need to demonstrate substantially higher accuracy rates and must undergo stringent validation processes by medical professionals before they can be considered reliable and safe for use within a medical context. It is currently vital for all information obtained from ChatGPT or similar platforms to be further discussed and corroborated with health care professionals. In our assessment, we examined two commonly used nephrology test question banks, totaling nearly 1000 questions. However, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of our study. First, the testing strategy we implemented, which comprised two runs, paralleled the methodology used by ASN and was guided by the balance between computational feasibility and the usefulness of the output data. Although it showed consistent performance across both runs in our examination, we recognize that this approach may not completely capture the full breadth of ChatGPT''s repeatability. Moreover, because our study design did not incorporate a feedback mechanism for ChatGPT''s responses, additional testing might not substantially enhance the observed performance. Although our findings provide valuable insights into the performance of ChatGPT, it is crucial to interpret the results with an understanding of this limitation. Second, we cannot confirm whether ChatGPT had access to Nephrology Self-Assessment Program, Kidney Self-Assessment Program, or any other nephrology databases or question banks during its training by OpenAI. The accessibility of these nephrology-related question banks and training the model with these question banks or incorporating feedback on its responses could potentially enhance its performance, similar to how it performed on the USMLE tests. 23 In our study, we deliberately avoided providing ChatGPT with any feedback on its responses (such as like or why like the response and dislike or why dislike the response), even during the initial run. This was done to simulate the real-world application of AI systems, where they may not receive immediate feedback on their responses. In future studies, this limitation could be overcome by integrating a feedback loop into the testing process, allowing ChatGPT to receive and learn from its performance feedback. Moreover, continuous training of ChatGPT, particularly in complex domains such as medicine and under the guidance of AI experts, may further enhance its performance and accuracy rates. This approach, however, was beyond the scope of this study, which aimed to evaluate the stand-alone performance and repeatability of ChatGPT''s responses without the influence of iterative learning or immediate feedback. Finally, it is worth noting that all the questions tested in our study did not involve visual information. As of the present time, ChatGPT, including GPT-4, does not allow inputting images through the user interface. This is likely due to the primitive nature of GPT-4, which is still being trained and developed. The only way for users to attempt image entry to GPT-4 is through the developer application programming interface. Furthermore, no software or application, including AI tools or machine learning algorithms, has fully met the criteria for accurately recognizing or differentiating various forms of visual information in the medical domain. This includes medical images such as ultrasound, x-ray, computed tomography, magnetic resonance imaging, and positron emission tomography, as well as radiological and anatomical atlases, dermatological and ophthalmological images, surgical visualizations, and medical illustrations and diagrams. In addition, challenges persist in accurately handling pathology slides, which hold particular significance in nephrology. Despite ongoing research, this remains a challenging issue to address. In summary, ChatGPT''s performance in nephrology remains low. Furthermore, the performance of ChatGPT varied when assessing different nephrology subfields. As a consequence, it is advisable for individuals not to depend solely on ChatGPT for academic studies or question answering in the field of nephrology. This study underscores the depth of medical knowledge possessed by ChatGPT and its promising applications within the field of nephrology. Collaborations between AI companies and medical professionals have the potential to enhance the precision and dependability of AI in nephrology practice, spanning areas such as dataset administration, diagnosis, treatment strategy formulation, patient engagement and education, and advancements in medical research and education. In a prospective outlook, advancements in AI, including models like GPT-4 and beyond, hold great promise for the practice of nephrology. These forthcoming developments encompass a spectrum of applications, including, but not limited to, enhanced decision support, predictive analytics, personalized medicine, telemedicine applications, and research assistance. However, it is crucial to emphasize that AI should complement, not replace, the expertise of nephrologists. Human oversight should remain integral in guiding its implementation in medical practice. In addition, it should adhere to all relevant health care data privacy and security regulations to ensure patient confidentiality. Future studies should address the limitations identified in our research and work toward developing more advanced and reliable AI models that can effectively support medical professionals and enhance patient care in the field of nephrology. Supplementary Material cjasn-19-035-s001.pdf (531.8KB, pdf) Open in a new tab Acknowledgments We extend our sincere gratitude to Kurtis Pivert, Heidi Winkel Bogacz, and Nicole Kunz-Slagle for their invaluable contribution in furnishing us with the pertinent data pertaining to the performance evaluation of nephrologists and/or nephrology trainees on the Kidney Self-Assessment Program and Nephrology Self-Assessment Program tests administered by the American Society of Nephrology (ASN). Footnotes J.M. and C.T. contributed equally to this work. See related editorial, “Will ChatGPT Be the Next Nephrologist?,” on pages 2–4. Disclosures W. Cheungpasitporn, I.M. Craici, P. Mekraksakit, J. Miao, and M.S. Sheikh report employment with Mayo Clinic. M. Gonzalez Suarez reports employment with Mayo Clinic and research funding from Gilead. P. Krisanapan reports ownership interest in Thai stocks (AURA, CHASE, COM7, DOHOME, HL, JMT, M-CHAI, MEGA, PHG, SABINA); research funding from PT. Kalbe Genexine Biologics, Indonesia and Thai Otsuka Pharmaceutical; honoraria from Apexcela Co., Ltd; and speakers bureau for AstraZeneca, Boehringer Ingelheim, Novo Nordisk, Thai Otsuka Pharmaceutical, and Zuellig Pharma. All remaining authors have nothing to disclose. Funding None. Author Contributions Conceptualization: Wisit Cheungpasitporn, Jing Miao, Charat Thongprayoon. Data curation: Paul W. Davis, Pajaree Krisanapan, Poemlarp Mekraksakit, Jing Miao, Mohammad S. Sheikh, Oscar A. Garcia Valencia. Formal analysis: Wisit Cheungpasitporn, Jing Miao, Charat Thongprayoon. Investigation: Wisit Cheungpasitporn, Iasmina M. Craici, Paul W. Davis, Pajaree Krisanapan, Poemlarp Mekrak