INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3254,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11321736','Should Transplant Nephrology Pursue Recognition from the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)?','Singh N, Anand PM, Gupta G, Sawinski D, Fix O, Adey D, Akalin E, Zayas C, Dadhania D, Doshi M, Cibrik D, Gupta M, Parsons R, Leca N, Santos RD, Concepcion BP, Nishio Lucar AG, Ong S, Sridhar VS, Parajuli S, Zachariah M, Mehta S, Soliman K, Shawar S, Husain SA, Preczewski L, Friedewald J, Mohan S, Wiseman A, Samaniego M, Kumar V, Tanriover B, Bloom R.','2024','waitlisted optn cosponsored tnftap bylaws tnftap accredits postfellowship asndataanalytics aakhi reignited visas pecos nonbillable pecos ogme sanction nucc nucc ogme nonbillable aakhi aakhi nonstandard nonstandard nst nst nst nst nst nst nst reap h1b visas nst nst visas nontenured visas sticking unchallenged culminates accredit diplomats entice oren prerogative optn optn optn optn redistributing optn rolled downsides crafting deterrents tnftap adey allovir hansa natera optn akalin caredx immucor caredx immucor caredx immucor caredx immucor calliditas caredx calliditas caredx natera veloxis calliditas natera veloxis calliditas caredx natera veloxis caredx behring memo natera veloxis royalty cibrik caredx eledon concepcion guidepoint veloxis monogram dadhania allovir caredx allovir caredx behring memo inventor w02018187521a2 liveonny friedewald egenesis eurofins veloxis behring eurofins viracor hansa biopharma veloxis scripps caredx alexion caredx mallinckrodt natera veloxis alexion caredx veloxis kpop ctot revivicor apollo behring hansa memo arep tncc honararia honararia elsivier nephronet honararia honararia webmd councilor veloxis delpi envarsus ceot optn leca eurofins tgi angion caredx behring hansa memo natera verici caredx natera prospera hsag srtr etclc nihmd nibib parajuli caredx veloxis caredx eurofin aapl amzn bac csco msft hansa biopharma natera preczewski levenger publix supermarkets varsity banc berkshire hathaway pacwest rivian samaniego caredx veloxis sawinski caredx councilor caredx mallinckrodt natera veloxis kpcop optn mallinckrodt natera veloxis sridhar adventhealth hansa medeor nephrosant talaris veloxis veloxis zayas gaurav neeraj neeraj neeraj neeraj neeraj gaurav neeraj bekir tanriover adey enver akalin cibrik beatrice concepcion darshana dadhania rowena delos mona doshi oren friedewald gaurav mallika vineeta nicolae leca shikha sumit angie nishio lucar sandesh parajuli preczewski millie samaniego deirdre sawinski saed shawar neeraj vikas sridhar bekir tanriover mareena zachariah zayas concepcion alasfar levea akalin dadhania samaniego potluri 2023_fellow_survey linas doshi schold hailemariam kidneynews p14_11 chonchol charytan heher hricik hairy audacious kidneynews p8_3 nucc nucc nucc_taxonomy_code_request_form_v6_2021 giacoma ayvaci gaston tanriover nonbillable ecfmg ecfmg evsp crsreports if10960 138_interventionalpulmonology_10 aagaard caverzagie guidebook milestonesguidebook byspecialty fsmb palleti untold kidneynews p1_2 cprfellowship_2022v3 proposalreviewandcomment aasld announce aasld announce sweitzer e006139 circheartfailure aasldapp optn mpsc_brief_subspecialty_201612 20accredited 20programs 20are 20assessed 20each 20year refund','Abstract Kidney transplant is not only the best treatment for patients with advanced kidney disease but it also reduces health care expenditure. The management of transplant patients is complex as they require special care by transplant nephrologists who have expertise in assessing transplant candidates, understand immunology and organ rejection, have familiarity with perioperative complications, and have the ability to manage the long-term effects of chronic immunosuppression. This skill set at the intersection of multiple disciplines necessitates additional training in Transplant Nephrology. Currently, there are more than 250,000 patients with a functioning kidney allograft and over 100,000 waitlisted patients awaiting kidney transplant, with a burgeoning number added to the kidney transplant wait list every year. In 2022, more than 40,000 patients were added to the kidney wait list and more than 25,000 received a kidney transplant. The Advancing American Kidney Health Initiative, passed in 2019, is aiming to double the number of kidney transplants by 2030 creating a need for additional transplant nephrologists to help care for them. Over the past decade, there has been a decline in the Nephrology—as well Transplant Nephrology—workforce due to a multitude of reasons. The American Society of Transplantation Kidney Pancreas Community of Practice created a workgroup to discuss the Transplant Nephrology workforce shortage. In this article, we discuss the scope of the problem and how the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education recognition of Transplant Nephrology Fellowship could at least partly mitigate the Transplant Nephrology work force crisis. Keywords: medical education In 1998, the American Society of Transplantation (AST) and the American Society of Nephrology (ASN) established the fellowship training criteria and educational infrastructure required by training institutions in the United States and Canada, formally setting up Transplant Nephrology as a distinct area of expertise. The accreditation criteria were built on the existing requirements for certification of transplant physicians set by the Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network (OPTN) managed by United Network for Organ Sharing (UNOS). Until June 2013, the program was cosponsored by the ASN. In May 2014, the Transplant Nephrology Fellowship Training Accreditation Program (TNFTAP) was registered as a Limited Liability Company (LLC) and an operating agreement (that acts as the LLC''s bylaws) was developed between the LLC and the AST. 1 – 3 TNFTAP accredits transplant centers that have developed programs to provide specialty Transplant Nephrology training. The standard Transplant Nephrology fellowship is a 1-year clinical fellowship, designed for nephrologists who have successfully completed a standard Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)–accredited (or foreign equivalent for Canadian programs) Nephrology fellowship. Unfortunately, most Transplant Nephrology fellowship programs are not able to fill all their training positions every year. In 2008, there were 50 accredited Transplant Nephrology training programs which graduated only 29 fellows. 3 A recent survey of US transplant nephrology training programs found that, of responding programs, 62% did not fill their annual fellowship positions at least one time between 2017 and 2022. 4 The 2022 ASN Nephrology Fellow Survey indicated that only 10% ( N =37) of current Nephrology fellows were seeking additional subspecialty fellowship training in Transplant Nephrology (17 fellows), Nephrology–Critical Care (10), Interventional Nephrology (7), glomerular disease (2), or home dialysis (1 fellow) (Figure 1 ). Of the 117 respondents starting their first postfellowship job, only 5% of nephrology fellows were practicing both Nephrology and another subspecialty, such as critical care or Transplant Nephrology. 5 The reasons why so few Nephrology fellows pursue further training in Transplant are incompletely understood. This is affected in part by a declining interest in Nephrology as a specialty among internal medicine residents in the United States, 6 with a widening gap between offered and filled positions over the years (Figure 2 ). The salary and job satisfaction (possibly a few of the many factors affecting the decision to enter transplant) are not necessarily better after the transplant fellowship. 7 Figure 1. Open in a new tab Current nephrology fellows'' plans on completion of 2021–2022 training year (the 2022 ASN Nephrology Fellow Survey). ASN, American Society of Nephrology. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Number of offered and filled nephrology positions over years in the United States (ASN AY 2020 Nephrology Match Preliminary Analysis. Available at https://asndataanalytics.github.io/AY-2020-Nephrology-Match/ . Accessed on April 29, 2023). A shortage in the number of Transplant Nephrology trainees is a concern because it will lead to a limited workforce to care for the growing number of kidney transplant recipients (Figure 3 ). A current pool of 800–1000 transplant nephrologists plays a critical role in providing and coordinating care to over 250,000 kidney recipients currently living with a functioning graft and over 88,000 patients on the kidney transplant waiting lists in addition to patients being evaluated for listing. 8 , 9 A deficit in the Transplant Nephrology workforce will adversely affect the future of clinical transplantation and will be able to support the Advancing American Kidney Health Initiative (AAKHI) mission to double the number of kidneys available for transplantation by 2030. 10 The declining interest in Transplant Nephrology and the resultant workforce crisis has reignited a debate about the possible steps we need to take as a transplant community to increase trainees'' interest in transplant. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Annual and cumulative kidney transplants in the United States between 2010 and 2022 (UNOS STAR file as of December 31, 2022). UNOS, United Network for Organ Sharing. ACGME Accreditation as a Strategy to Secure the Transplant Nephrology Workforce The ACGME accreditation of Transplant Nephrology has been debated over the years but was not pursued in the past primarily to avoid adding a significant administrative burden to programs associated with certification requirements. ACGME is responsible for ensuring that the accredited programs meet certain standards for the training and education of residents and fellows in various medical specialties, including Internal Medicine. Table 1 summarizes some of the challenges faced by Transplant Nephrology and how ACGME accreditation could be beneficial to our field. We believe ACGME accreditation of Transplant Nephrology with its several advantages as discussed below could enhance the interest of trainees in Transplant Nephrology. Table 1. Current challenges to transplant nephrology and potential benefits with Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education accreditation Current Challenges Potential Benefits of ACGME Accreditation For trainees • Standardization of fellow recruitment • Work hour regulations • Uniformity of training across all centers • Potential utilization of NRMP • Avoid burn-out of fellows by limiting hours • Enhance the uniformity and quality of transplant training across institutions with basic standards For fellowship program directors • Source of funding fellows • Administrative time to dedicate to fellow training • Sponsoring visas for IMGs • Allow funding for transplant nephrology training through graduate medical education funds • Salary support required to devote 25%–50% FTE of nonclinical time to the administration of the program and education of fellows • Allow for a pathway for J1 fellows For workforce • Lack of CMS designation • CMS reimbursements are tied to the parent specialty “nephrology” • Complex and challenging patient population • Potentially PECOS designation • CMS could potentially establish specific reimbursement rates that reflect the unique expertise and resources required for providing care to kidney transplant recipients • CMS may be asked to recognize nonbillable work, which is a significant portion of Transplant Nephrology practice Open in a new tab ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; CMS, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services; FTE, full-time equivalent; IMG, international medical graduate; NRMP, National Resident Matching Program; PECOS, provider enrollment, chain, and ownership system. Recognition of Transplant Nephrology as a Distinct Medical Specialty A formal recognition of Transplant Nephrology by ACGME is desirable given the additional training and considerable differences in scope and practice versus General Nephrology. Having formal accreditation could help distinguish aspects of care of recipients that is transplant-specific, such as the evaluation of donor/recipient candidates, early post-transplant care, and the management of long-term consequences of immunosuppression. In the past few years, several other subspecialties, including Interventional Pulmonary and Correctional Medicine, have received ACGME accreditation with a goal of better recognition and attracting more trainees as mentioned in their initial application. 11 The ASN has also recently recognized the value of ACGME accreditation. 12 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services Designation The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) currently does not recognize Transplant Nephrology as a separate subspecialty. ACGME accreditation and/or American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) certification is required to obtain CMS Provider Enrollment, Chain, and Ownership System designation for any specialty. CMS reimbursement rates for medical services are often tied to specialty designations. The American Medical Association (AMA)''s Relative Value Scale Update Committee comprised representatives from various medical specialties, reviews, and makes recommendations to the CMS regarding creation of new current procedural terminology code and relative value units associated with the services and procedures specific to that specialty. Transplant Nephrology does not currently meet the criteria for CMS designation that are presented in Table 2 . 13 , 14 However, it would meet the criteria if it were to become ACGME or ABIM approved. Table 2. The criteria for specialty designation by the Center for Medicare & Medicaid Services Section C—New Taxonomy Code Requests—Physician Only One of the following must be met for new taxonomy code requests for MD or DO 1. The area of specialty is recognized for certification by the ABMS or the AOA certifying boards 2. There is an accredited residency/fellowship or specialty/subspecialty training program by the ACGME or AOA-Accredited OGME 3. If Sections C.1 or C.2 are not met, all of the following must be met • The training requirements demonstrate that the MD/DO has a recognized area of specialty/subspecialty, i.e ., separate and distinct from others • The training program is accredited by a third party • The third-party accrediting organization establishes training program requirements, conducts periodic review of the programs, and has the authority to sanction programs • A certification examination is required for graduates of the specialty/subspecialty training program to earn the designation The NUCC shall determine whether a request meets all requirements of Section C.3 Open in a new tab ABMS, American Board of Medical Specialties; ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; AOA, America Osteopathic Association; DO, Doctors of Osteopathy; MD, Medical Doctors; NUCC; National Uniform Claim Committee; OGME, Osteopathic Graduate Medical Education. Currently, all transplant medicine subspecialties (Transplant Hepatology, Transplant Cardiology, or Transplant Nephrology) bill using standard/generic E/M codes that are not specific to any specialty. Once Transplant Nephrology is ACGME-accredited and consequently AMA-recognized and CMS-recognized, it may be possible to negotiate with AMA and CMS to create specific current procedural terminology codes with higher relative value units not only for Transplant Nephrology but for all the other transplant medicine subspecialties to capture the nonbillable work as well as the complex and value-based care provided. 15 CMS designation will ensure that the patients referred to transplant nephrologists for a new kidney transplant evaluation by their Nephrology colleagues from the same practice are billed as a new office visit instead of a follow-up visit. The Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation payment model launched concurrent with the release of the AAKHI in 2019 provides incentives for increasing the number of kidney transplants and improving their longevity; however, there is no provision for separate payment to transplant nephrologists primarily due to the inability of CMS to separately identify transplant nephrologists from general nephrologists. CMS recognition will better identify transplant nephrologists and their role in value-based payment structure and the future success of AAKHI. Change in Oversight of Nonstandard Training Programs Nonstandard training (NST) programs are advanced clinical subspecialty disciplines or paths for which ACGME accreditation and/or American Board of Medical Specialties Member Board certification is unavailable. 16 On January 7, 2023, oversight of NST programs (for example, Transplant Nephrology, Transplant Infectious Disease) was shifted to ACGME. 16 However, ACGME will confer NST recognition to sponsoring institutions with NST programs and not upon individual NST subspecialties. If the NST programs or specialties at ACGME-accredited are now required to comply with the same requirements as for those of ACGME-accredited programs regarding trainees'' clinical and educational hours, annual assessment, opportunity to raise concerns, and provide feedback, it is logical for Transplant Nephrology to transition from NST status to ACGME accreditation since it will be bound by these regulations regardless but can only reap the other benefits of accreditation if accreditation is pursued. Change in Program Requirement for J1 Visa Sponsorship As per the Association of American Medical Colleges Physician Specialty Data Report, 66% of the US Nephrology fellows were international medical graduates (IMGs) in 2021, a percentage higher than in any other ACGME-accredited specialty. 17 Similarly, most Transplant Nephrology fellowship applicants are IMGs who need either J1 or H1B visas during their transplant training. Starting July 1, 2023, ACGME-accredited institutions wishing to host J1 physicians in NST programs have been required to obtain ACGME NST recognition, 16 without which they would not be able to hire transplant fellows on J1 visas. Obtaining this recognition process involves a substantial application process with significant costs for the program, efforts that would not be required if ACGME accreditation for Transplant Nephrology were secured, thereby maintaining access to training programs for most applicants and consequently securing the Transplant Nephrology workforce pipeline. Federal Funding for Transplant Nephrology Medicare Graduate Medical Education (GME) funding, which began in 1965, has been a primary source of funding for the teaching hospitals for training residents and fellows in ACGME-accredited programs, 18 although Medicare sets a cap on this funding, known as the “Medicare GME cap.” Most hospitals train more residents and fellows than Medicare funded. As transplant fellowships are not ACGME-accredited, their funding largely depends on the transplant centers or hospitals, and sometimes in the absence of adequate funding, the programs may face difficulties in hiring interested candidates for their programs. The accreditation by ACGME would make the Transplant Nephrology training programs eligible for GME funds. In addition, ACGME accreditation would allow Transplant Nephrology fellows to be hired under the GME program, in contrast to current practice at most transplant centers, where fellows are hired as nontenured faculty (mainly as an instructor), thereby inadvertently limiting training opportunities to US citizens or permanent residents, and specifically precluding the participation of trainees on J1 visas. Protected Time for Fellowship Program Directors Current ACGME program requirements require at least 0.2 full-time equivalent protected time for program directors of any sized internal medicine subspecialty fellowship program, including the small programs like Interventional Pulmonology that may only have 1–2 fellows per year. 19 This has been a sticking point for many sponsoring institutions, and implementation may be variable, but this is a relatively new requirement from ACGME (July 2023) that would likely be applied to a transplant nephrology fellowship if it came into existence. This will offset some of the increased administrative burden resulting from the need to comply with ACGME continuous accreditation. Enhanced Quality in Transplant Training ACGME accreditation will enhance the quality of transplant training across institutions due to emphasis on competency-based medical education articulated through subspecialty specific milestones. 20 , 21 Easing of State Licensing Requirements for Exceptionally Qualified Candidates The annual ASN Nephrology Fellow Survey data from 2019 to 2022 show that the percentage of exceptionally qualified fellows (as presented in Table 3 ), 22 IMGs who entered Nephrology fellowship without prior US internal medicine residency, has increased from 1.5% to 7%. 5 Similarly, a significant number of Transplant Nephrology fellowship positions are believed to be filled by these exceptionally qualified trainees, although the exact figures are unknown. Exceptionally qualified fellows may find it difficult to find employment after graduation due to restrictions on licenses put in place by several states for fellows trained in non–ACGME-accredited programs. Most states require 3 years of ACGME training for licensure. 24 Although a 3-year Nephrology fellowship track (with 1 year of Nephrology followed by 2 years of research/Transplant Nephrology) is available at some programs, a 2-year Nephrology training is standard at most programs, and exceptionally qualified graduated fellows would therefore not meet state licensing requirements. After graduation, most exceptionally qualified fellows either attempt to match into an internal medicine residency program which is becoming increasingly difficult for IMGs or try to seek a full-time faculty position at an accredited US or Canadian academic institution with an academic/institutional license for a minimum of 3 years under Special Consideration Pathway A of ABIM (as presented in Table 3 ) 23 before becoming eligible for the ABIM Internal Medicine or subspecialty board. 23 , 25 Of note, as per the 2022 ASN Nephrology Fellow Survey, 4% ( N =15) of the graduating Nephrology fellows who had not yet completed a US-based residency required for board eligibility planned to enter internal medicine. 5 The Special Consideration pathway A ABIM positions are also limited and require special hospital approval. The ACGME accreditation of Transplant Nephrology will allow the graduated Transplant fellows who previously received internal medicine residency abroad but completed 2 years of Nephrology in the United States to meet the state licensure requirements and hence help increase the workforce. This strategy may also attract IMGs to pursue Transplant Nephrology fellowship. Table 3. (A) Criteria for Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education defined exceptionally qualified applicant. 22 (B) American Board of Internal Medicine special consideration pathway A to enable International Trained Physicians to Obtain American Board of Internal Medicine Board Certification 23 A. Exceptionally qualified applicant as defined by the ACGME is someone who has ○ Completed a non–ACGME-accredited residency program in the core specialty ○ Demonstrated clinical excellence, in comparison with peers, throughout their training ○ Additional evidence of exceptional qualifications, which are required, may include one of the following: (a) participation in additional clinical or research training in the specialty or subspecialty, (b) demonstration of scholarship in the specialty, (c) demonstration of leadership abilities during or after training, or (d) completion of an ACGME-I–accredited residency program B. ABIM pathway A: IMGs who are full-time US or Canadian faculty A full-time faculty member at an LCME-accredited or Canadian-accredited medical school, or at an ACGME-accredited or Canadian-accredited residency or fellowship program, who has successfully completed training in internal medicine and/or a subspecialty abroad may become eligible to achieve ABIM board certification in internal medicine and/or a subspecialty as a candidate for special consideration The candidate may not propose themselves for consideration in this pathway but must be proposed by the Chair of the Department of medicine, or the internal medicine and/or subspecialty program director at the institution where the candidate holds a current full-time faculty appointment Who is eligible? Eligible faculty will have ○ Completed three or more years of verified graduate medical education training in internal medicine and/or a subspecialty abroad ○ An academic rank of Assistant Professor or higher at the time of application ○ A full-time faculty appointment for a minimum of three (3) immediately prior and consecutive years at the same institution ○ Full-time faculty members are those who supervise and teach trainees (students, residents, or fellows) in clinical settings that include direct patient care ○ The appointment must be at an LCME-accredited or Canadian-accredited medical school or at an ACGME-accredited or Canadian-accredited internal medicine residency or subspecialty fellowship training program ○ Certification in internal medicine from ABIM for certification in a subspecialty ○ A valid, unrestricted, and unchallenged medical license to practice medicine in the United States, its territories or Canada Open in a new tab ABIM, American Board of Internal Medicine; ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; ACGME-I, ACGME-International; IMG, international medical graduate; LCME, Liaison Committee on Medical Education. Common Questions Regarding ACGME Accreditation for Transplant Nephrology Training Several additional questions and concerns regarding ACGME accreditation of Transplant Nephrology training warrant additional consideration. Are ACGME-Accredited Specialties Required to Have ABIM Board Certification? ACGME does not certify individuals and would not require a certification examination. An expressed purpose of ACGME accreditation is to support fellowship training that culminates in certification, 26 but there are exceptions, and this need not be the case nor should it be a factor in deciding whether ACGME should accredit a new transplant nephrology specialty. As an example, Interventional Pulmonology was granted ACGME accreditation this year, without the mandate for ABIM certification, although the American Association for Bronchoscopy and Interventional Pulmonology has been conducting a board-certifying examination in Interventional pulmonology since 2014, with 423 current diplomats. 27 Will ACGME Accreditation Deter Nephrology Fellows from Pursuing Further Training in Transplant due to the Need to Take an Additional Examination? The exact effect ACGME accreditation would have on recruitment of Transplant Nephrology fellows is uncertain, although it may entice more trainees, especially IMGs to pursue further education in transplant due to more formal recognition, the CMS designation, federal funding for Transplant Nephrology training, ACGME required fellows'' work hour limits/time off, and easing of state licensing requirements for some IMGs as discussed above. For example, the effect of ACGME accreditation on Transplant Hepatology and Advanced Heart Failure/Transplant Cardiology has been overall positive despite the need for a certification examination in both these specialties. Since ACGME accreditation of Transplant Hepatology in 2006, the number of ACGME‐accredited transplant hepatology fellowship programs has increased from 21 in 2007–2008 to 66 in 2023–2024 and annual fellowship graduates have grown, 28 despite about half of all available fellowship slots going unfilled each year (Oren Fix, personal communication, July 18, 2023). The 3-year combined gastroenterology/Transplant Hepatology pathway was initiated as a pilot program in 2012 and involved over 2/3 of all the accredited transplant hepatology fellowship programs in the United States. 29 The pilot was successful and was approved as a formal dual certification pathway by the ABIM in 2019. A survey done by The International Society of Heart and Lung Transplantation in 2019 30 concluded that in the United States, recognition of Advanced Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology as a subspecialty with ACGME-accredited training and board certification has contributed to increased professional identity, with an attendant growth in the number of heart failure professionals. However, in 2023, at least one-third of Advanced Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology fellowship positions went unfilled per National Resident Matching Program (NRMP) data which may be due to factors unrelated to ACGME accreditation. Are ACGME-Accredited Programs Required to Participate in the NRMP, Commonly Referred to as “The Match,” for their Residency and Fellowship Positions? The decision to participate and how many positions to offer in “the Match” is a program''s prerogative. 31 Whereas most ACGME-accredited programs or specialties participate in the NRMP to standardize the selection process, some opt not to. Currently, Advanced Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology and Interventional Pulmonology participate in the NRMP. Transplant hepatology does not participate in NRMP but participated in an American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases sponsored match for the first time in 2023. 32 Will OPTN Require Completion of ACGME-Accredited Transplant Nephrology Fellowship for Eligibility to be a Primary Physician of a Kidney/Pancreas Transplant Program? The OPTN currently requires either Transplant fellowship or experience or an alternate pathway to be recognized as a primary physician of a transplant program. This requirement is not expected to change with ACGME certification. For example, OPTN does not require Transplant Hepatology certification to be eligible as a primary liver transplant physician as long as the experience is documented. 33 Would CMS and OPTN/UNOS Mandate Transplant Patients to be Seen Only by Transplant Nephrologists If Our Specialty Gets ACGME Accredited? Accreditation is about acknowledgment of a specific and unique skill set and not about redistributing patients or deciding who is “allowed” to see certain patients. Each hospital grants privileges to providers/practitioners to see patients on the basis of their training and experience. CMS and OPTN/UNOS do not dictate medical care. For example, Transplant Hepatology received ACGME accreditation in 2006, but it had no significant effect on clinical care in terms of who is managing transplant patients. The general gastroenterologists without sufficient transplant training or experience who were managing transplant patients before 2006 continued to manage them without any stigma from the community. Will General Nephrologists be Less Inclined to Resume Care of their Transplant Patients after ACGME Accreditation of Transplant Nephrology? With a growing number of transplant recipients and the new CMS quality measures and value-based care models being rolled out, there is a need for better care coordination between transplant and general nephrologists as well as the need for more robust training in Transplant during the General Nephrology years. In practice, most general nephrologists regularly coordinate the care of their transplant patients with transplant nephrologists or refer transplant recipients back to the transplant centers when transplant-related complications arise. Potential Pitfalls of ACGME Accreditation for Transplant Nephrology Given that obtaining ACGME accreditation is likely to be an irreversible change, potential downsides to accreditation must be carefully considered. These factors include. Potential for Decreasing Transplant Exposure during General Nephrology Fellowship Separating out Transplant Nephrology from General Nephrology could reduce the exposure to transplant during Nephrology fellowship and subsequent readiness of nephrologists to accept patients with transplants. Potential Loss of Training Flexibility and Expertise Availability The current lack of formal ACGME accreditation offers training programs greater flexibility in crafting their curriculum to better align with the unique demands and strengths of individual institutions. In addition, the major changes to Common Program requirements are typically instituted by ACGME at 10-year intervals, 34 and given the fast rate of change of transplant science, the formal accreditation risks the timely inclusion of new requirements. There were 153,843 residents and fellows in ACGME-accredited programs in the 2021–2022 academic year, 35 and Transplant Nephrology fellows would therefore represent <0.1% of their trainees, providing little incentive to prioritize the needs of transplant training programs. Administrative and Cost Burden The ACGME requires each accredited program to pay an annual fee to the ACGME and additionally has the authority to impose other fees. 36 For example, the 2023 fee schedule includes a $7200 initial fee, a $5000 per program fee for programs with ≤5 residents, a $3200 fee for canceled site visits, and a fee for appealing ACGME decisions of $10,000 plus appeals panel member expenses and administrative costs. This financial burden may serve as deterrents to opening new training programs. The counter argument is that even TNFTAP requires an application fee of $3,000, although modest, for initial accreditation of the Transplant Nephrology training programs. The Potential Need for a Certification Examination As mentioned above, other medical subspecialties fulfill the need for a certification examination through either ABIM examination or an examination written by the specialty organization. For Transplant Nephrology, the quality and scope of ABIM examination (if implemented) may be limited by a lack of strong evidence for many transplant medicine practices. Furthermore, ABIM examination fees would be an additional burden on trainees, as current initial certification examination fees for Transplant Hepatology and Advanced Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology are each $2995. 37 This cost and the time burden of the long-term participation in the ABIM''s maintenance of certification program may deter potential trainees and contribute to job dissatisfaction and burnout. An alternative assessment program administered by the AST would require the development of a new institutional infrastructure to prepare and administer the examination. Risk of Care Fragmentation and Skill Dilution Introducing further subspecialization could fragment patient care. A separate accreditation might result in general nephrologists becoming less proficient in providing transplant care, potentially weakening the collective expertise of the Nephrology sector. We believe that the overall known benefits of ACGME accreditation outweigh many of the aforementioned hypothetical concerns. Establishing ACGME accreditation of Transplant Nephrology is crucial for recognizing it as a separate and distinct specialty. For the applicants and fellows, it will make the fellowship more appealing due to formal recognition, CMS Provider Enrollment, Chain, and Ownership System designation with possibly better reimbursement benefits post-training, providing comprehensive and structured training, securing GME funding, and facilitating J1 visa sponsorship for the non-US applicants. For the program directors, it will ensure they receive protected time for administrative responsibilities. An ACGME-accredited Transplant Nephrology fellowship program would benefit the transplant community and improve the quality of care of our patients. However, we believe that training in the fundamentals of transplant during Nephrology fellowship should be maintained as a core requirement to ensure that general nephrologists who prefer to resume care of their patients have the competency to provide long-term care to kidney transplant recipients. Acknowledgments This manuscript is a work product of the American Society of Transplantation (AST) Kidney Pancreas Community of Practice Transplant Nephrology Fellowship Workgroup and is endorsed by the AST. Disclosures D. Adey reports Research Funding: Allovir, Hansa Pharmaceuticals, and Natera; Honoraria: American Board of Internal Medicine; and Advisory or Leadership Role: American Society of Transplantation, OPTN/UNOS Policy Committee, Conflict of Interest Committee; American Board of Internal Medicine, Subspecialty Governance Board; Awards Committee; and Transplant International Journal, Editorial Board. E. Akalin reports Consultancy: CareDx, Immucor, and Transplant Genomics; Research Funding: CareDx, Immucor, and NIH; Honoraria: CareDx, Immucor, and Transplant Genomics; and Advisory or Leadership Role: CareDx and Immucor. P.M. Anand reports Consultancy: Calliditas; Ownership Interest: Stock—CareDx; Honoraria: Calliditas, CareDx, Natera, and Veloxis; Advisory or Leadership Role: Calliditas, Natera, and Veloxis; and Speakers Bureau: Calliditas, Caredx, Natera, and Veloxis. R. Bloom reports Ownership Interest: Gilead Pharmaceuticals; Research Funding: CareDx, CSL Behring, MEMO Therapeutics, Natera, and Veloxis; Patents or Royalties: Royalty-UpToDate; and Other Interests or Relationships: Editorial Board- American Journal of Kidney Diseases . D. Cibrik reports Consultancy: CareDx and Eledon. B.P. Concepcion reports Consultancy: Guidepoint; Honoraria: Veloxis; Patents or Royalties: Springer; and Advisory or Leadership Role: Editorial board member of American Journal of Kidney Diseases , Monogram Health Clinical Advisory Board, NKF health equity advisory committee, and UNOS Kidney Committee. D. Dadhania reports Consultancy: Advisory